      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>BY ANNIE 0. TIDBITS.</head>
        <p>BY ANNIE 0. TIDBITS.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Love that Won," "Hunte4 Down," "hidden Hands," "The Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," Cce.,</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF OPENING CHAPTERS: As a vessel is going down Olga Murray tells Dr. Dacre that, six years since, site murdered Stephen Henderson, of Greatbridge Hall, for which crime suspicion fell on Richard Sterry. If possible, the statement which she signs is to be kept from • her lover, Sir Geoffrey Verriker. A bottle containing the document is flung into the sea. Sir Geoffrey, who rescues Olga from the wreck, insists on taking her tb his mother at Kemscote Hall, of which he has just come into possession by the death of his uncle. Mrs. Dunston was to have married the uncle, and she plots with her daughter Julia to make the latter Lady Verrilier by a marriage. with Geoffrey or Geoffrey's COU3ill Edward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>CHAPTER Iv.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER Iv.</p>
        <p>She gave the blind-cord a savage twist, and stared nut of the window. The little country town lay quiet and dusty und2r the sunshine. Scarcely anyone was about. Julia idly watched a woman stroll past, and then sud- denly her eyes brightened.</p>
        <p>"Mother, here are the Verrickers," she said, harshly. ' "They are driving—Mrs. Verricker and Ethel, whiter than ever. Haw I hate that girl ! "</p>
        <p>Mrq. Dunston came back: to her daughter's side in the window. Her face looked grey in the sunlight beside Julia's, and the -colour that flushed her cheeks at the sight of Mrs. Yerricker outside was hectic and unnatural.</p>
        <p>"Hate her !" ,she cried. "Well you might. It drives me mad to think that sle is there where you should be, that little chit of a girl—where you would have be if Sir Henry had not died! Oh, isn't it enough to drive one mad!" She clenched her hands and stara out of the window. The carriage had stopped, and Mrs. Verricker was leaning forward to talk to a man who stood on the other side. Suddenly Julia gave a cry.</p>
        <p>"They are talking to Edward," she said. " Mother, suppose tL2re is some news of the boat "</p>
        <p>Mrs. .Dunston stocd rigid. Her thin face, with its queer, s%rn-out, exhausted leek, had changed. The lines tightened about her mouth.</p>
        <p>" I - hope it is at the bottom of the sea," she said, between her teeth. "I hope Geoffrey will never come back. Oh, if only he is dead, Julia, the way will be clear. Edward will succeed to the -estate—he will marry you, and then—then we can oust the two—that little white woman and that -,;;•-md girl! Oh, what wouldn't I give to oust them as they ousted me! Directly the news comes that the boat is lost, we'll get Edward to turn them out. give them no time. We'll turn them out neck and crop, and " _</p>
        <p>She stopped. 'Edward Verricker was rais- ing his hat, and the carriage was going on. Airs. Dunston had a glimpse . Mrs. Verrieker's and Ethel's faces as- they passed, and her heart jumped. "There is bad news ! " she cried, •in a harsh whisper. They are both as white as ghosts. Julia, look! 'Edward is coming here. Go and open the door." Julia obeyed. She had grown , suddenly cold. Her fingors fumbled strangely with the latch when she reached the hall door. It seemed difficult to move, somehow, for in her heart the old wild passionate cry for Geoffrey -was beating up, and the thought of that face that would confront her when she opened the dcor sent through her a sickening despair. She got it open at last. Edward 'Verricker stood en the doorstep, with an ugly smile on his dark face, which reminded Julia involun- tarily of the face of a murderer she had seen. lie sprang -inside, and caught her in his arms. -" Julia—Julia ! " he cried. "The luck is ours ! The boat is lost ! "</p>
        <p>_ She, caught her breath with a choking cry, and wrenched herself free. "And—and— Geoffrey ? " she stammered. "Went down with the rest," said Edward. " The news has inst come through. 'Mrs. Verricker told me, and she is just going to gat confirmation—to m ko, sure.' "And, suppos3—suppose it is not true ? " Julia said, with white lips. . .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dunston, who had come to the door of the drawing-room, put up her white hand and leant upon it. Her face looked drawn, haggard, hectic. "It will be true," shc whispered. "Edward will succeed yet. It will—it shall be true 1"</p>
        <p>Ethel -Verricker dropped back in the car- riage alter Edward had left them. She -wcz.; white, as Mrs. Dunston had said, but there was a girlish softness in her face and in hot deep grey eyes. She put out her hazel and clasped hot mother's. "Mother, don't make ,so sure Of it, she, said. "I don't believe Geoffrey is dead. I can't believe it. Oh, I feel he will come back to us."</p>
        <p>"Hush, hush, child ! " said Mrs. Verricker, in a shaking voice. " Den't try to make me hope. I dare not. If it should be true-"</p>
        <p>Ethel's hand grew tighter upon her mother's. Her face, was quivering and desperately white, but she forced a smile. "Wait, mother—rally wait,". she sai4l. The carriage drew up at the post-office, and Mrs. Verricker got down determined to write tthe telegram with her own hands. Any- thing was better than that terrible, helpless waiting. They had had five days of Ti' now— five days since the ship was ue -five deo s of horrible anxiety, culminating in the hideous news that it had been lest at sea. And now —even now she could not believe it without</p>
        <p>confirmation. While she filled up the form, Ethel strolled back to the door. The little street outside blazed in the sun. Everything looked so quiet, so calm and undisturbed, that it seemed that it could not be that the news was true. Geoffrey could nAiot be dead. It was impos- sible to be dead On a day like this !</p>
        <p>She stared out, straight before her, with wide,. hopeless eyes. She kept up an appear- ance of cheerfulness before her mother, but ith her back turned her young face grew rigid and set and strained.. It was only a few months since her 'brother Geoffrey had gme out to Australia, almost penniless, to do the best he could. Only a few months ego si;e.:,e she and her mother had been strugglint along as best they could on a miserable income. Then, such a short time after Geoffrey 11.1 d gone, hind come the news that his uncle, Sir Henry Verrieker, was dead, and that be was heir to a rich estate. And now this !</p>
        <p>It seemed too much to crowd into a few months. She lifted her face to the sky. Surely Providence would not,-. could not, take Geoffrey away- from them 'any : He would come back— surely lie would cc,.f.e back ! She was roused by a hand on her arm, and she looked rdund with a start. "Miss Verrick,.n. ! Are you looking for the moon at this thne of day, or are you exercis- ing your neck ? ". She put out her hard quickly to the man who stool before . her,. and when he saw how white lr face was, he drew back. - " Do t . joke to-day, Mr. Sterry," she cried. "I can't—to-day. They say that the Stella has gone down with all hands."_ He still held hers in his, and his face grew slowly white. " It can't be true," he cried. "Miss Verricker, are you sure " She nodded silently. "1 am afraid—" she said, after a moment, and stopped. "Mr. Sterry," she added, stiddenly, almost in a whisper, "come back with us to Kemscote. Come and help ma with mother. I—l—can't lace it any longer." Dick .Steyry looked down intp her eyes. She went nearer to him. "Come, anti—and--be jolly," she said, in a choking whisper—" just as if you did not believe it. I am trying—it will be time enough to believe it when wc know for certain. But meanwhile I am trying —to pretend that I don't believe." "I'll come" he said. `.` I'll do anything .you wish. Here is Mrs. Verricker:" He turned to greet her, and held out hir arm to her. He had never thought her an laid or a feeble woman until to-day; but she clung to him desperately now, as she would have eluna to her own son. Her &amp;pp brightened a little when she saw him. Dick Sterry had almost been like a son to her dur- ing the feA months they had been at Kems-</p>
        <p>cote Hall, helping her in little matters ccn- nected with the estate which the lawyers had given her possession of, leaping the ditch which divided his own small property from theirs regularly over morning to see how they were, and every morning growing more and more dangerously attracted by Ethel's pale face and soft grey eyes.</p>
        <p>"Mother, I have asked him to come back with us," she said now. "He is coming to tea. He—he doesn't believe that the news can be true, either."</p>
        <p>Her voice faltered. Dick Sterry looked a little taken aback, and Mrs. Verricker turned sharply to look into his face.</p>
        <p>Her face fell 'When she saw his. "Let us go," she said, abruptly-. " I am glad you are coming with us. They say the reply will be with us in less than an hour."</p>
        <p>'Then let us drive round a bit," cried Ethel. "It won't take us much more than half-an-hour if we go straight home. Let us go round." Mrs. Verricker agreed listlesslY, and they started. Perhaps none of them ever forgot that drive—Mrs. Verricker, haunted- by the vision of her son's drowned face, Ethel torn in two—trying to hope, and yet als6 seeing the future that nfight lie before- them7---homeless— a 'future of struggle and poverty; and Richaru Sterry, looking at the face that tempted him, and remembering bitterly the ugly barrier that would stand for ever between him anti the girl, he loved. They all of them saw Ugly things in the drive that afternoon.</p>
        <p>"if it should be true," said Mrs. Ver. ricker, sudden] v, in a queer whisper—" it Geoffrey—should not come back, I suppose everything will go to Edward Verricker ? '</p>
        <p>She looked straight into Dick's face. Ho nodded helplessly. " I suppose so," he said. Mrs. Verricker looked out at the dusty hedges, which were flying by them. Her white lips trembled. Even if she could ever get over her son's death, she could never go back to her old poverty, Dick thought, as he watched her. It would kill her. it was a dreadful drive. A hundred times Mrs. Verricker looked at her watch, and al- ways the little hand seemed stationary, al- ways they moved with that frightful slowness-. "We mustn't get back too soon," Ethel whispered to Dick. "The telegram must bo there waiting for us." . He nodded. anu thou plunged into a wild description of the country they were passing. Mrs. Verricker and Ethel had not had much time to; go about. " Why; we're not used to horses and car- riages yet'," Ethel had said ; " and it would never do to go out in them before we're used to them, you know. I don't want to look odd, So I am practising sitting in the victoria every morning, only I don't get very much chance, because there's always some silly man about—wanting to clean it. or something. But I shall be ready to ride about in style by the time Geoffrey gets home." -</p>
        <p>Now, there was a poss:4o,ity of Geoffrey never corning tiome. and the onpn earriarre</p>
        <p>illi</p>
      </div>
