      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>, ! . . , .. • ........ , l . . . _</p>
        <p>Aow as tLey passed tae luoge and ecapc.-1 from the thick, short avenue of trees inlo the open, the park lay before them dull and growing a little. misty. The Hall remained out of sight for a few moments. Then suddenly it burst upon them —a huge, rambling building cf reddish bricks and grey stone, standing out in the fading sunlignt silent, secret, mysterious—a strange, grave thing that _ seemed to have held the secrets of generations and to pcssess a strange knowledge of things to come. Ethel shivered slightly as she got down from the carriage. Dick Sterry was holding out his hand to help her. His, too, was cold, and ,as she walked in under the broad rid porch it seemed as if she walked into the midst of shadows. Mrs. Verrickor was in the Erall before her. The butler had taken/ up a silver salver and was coming- towards her. Ethel darted forward. " I will open it," she cried, breathlessly. " I—mother; wouldn't you rather I opened it ? " Mrs. '1 orricker' turned away slowly. Ethel took the envelope to the window, thron,h which the sunlight poured into the -Id Hall. It fell, full on her head, bringing -ut gleams of gold in her bright hair. Dick Sterry watched her, standing rigid in he shadow. . his face was very grey, for he new that the ghastliest horror lay between IS life and hers—and would lie between them 'or ever ! Yet his eve. 3 lingered hungrily en or fair face, and he started a little when add,r-117 she came from the window and ;aught her mother's hands in hers. - " Mother—mother, it's all right ! " cried, hoarsely. " Geoffrey's all right. He's coming home. The boat did go down, but he 13 saved, and is coming home." She caught up the telegram, and pointed to it with shaking fingers. Geoffrey had not spared his words in the telegram.</p>
        <p>"Dearst mother," it ran, "I am safe and sound. Was picked, up by another ship, and be home to-morrow. A friend with ric, writing.—G eoffrey."</p>
        <p>,• Ethel dropped the telegram, and across, the hall she met Dick's eyes. He was coming to- wards her with outstretched hands, and uhe sprang to her feet to meet them. " Thank Heaven ! " he sail. "I'm more than glad for you." Ethel could not speak. They looked into each other's eyes, and they neither of thlti saw the dark shadow of a woman growing up Lotween them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>"Butt, Geoirry, I can't—indeed- I- can't." " But, darling, you can, and will. I have written to my mother. Everything will be all right. You ne-3d not worry about, any- thing, darling." Olga turned her face away, and stared out of the window. Her hands were clenched. Before her stretched the sea and sky—the endless sea and sky that had ended all too soon for her, and 11,2 r dark eyes grew despe- rate. At any cost INlle must escape—at tuiy cost! And whi: had she not already done so?</p>
      </div>
