      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>D:ck Sterry watched her, si</head>
        <p>D:ck Sterry watched her, si</p>
        <p>was out for the first time since Mrs. Ver- ricker and Ethel had been at Kemscote Hall. " What is that place yendel ? " Ethel said, suddenly pointing. Thby had reached the top of a hill, and before them lay a valley halt hidden by trees, With a little stream winding between sleepy cottages and one or .two mills. A sudden change came over Dick's face. It grew suddenly drawn and white. "I had no idea we had come 7 so far," he safd, hurriedly. "We ought to turn back now. That village is Little Spinham. Greatbridge is only two miles further on. I think we should turn back now."</p>
        <p>Ethel stared out across the valley. "It looks very pretty," she said; "and is Great- bridge a town</p>
        <p>It- was a --Moment before be could reply. The sun had been suddenly blotted out from them, and blackness filled the sky. He stared ahead, seeing not the pretty country bathed in the sunshine but the wreck of hi's own life.</p>
        <p>" No, not ,much of a town," he said, in a queer. jerky way; "but I—l have not been there for years. Shall we turn back ? " Something in his voice made Ethel look at him quickly, and the pallor of his face gave her a sudden shock. Yes, if you like," she said, quietly. , She fell back in her seat, and the carriage turned. Dick sat exactly opposite to her, and his grey face, and sat lips seemed to blot out the whole world beside. She stared at him. What was it that had sent the blocd from his cheeks? What was there about a sleepy village in a hollow to send that strange lock of horror to his eyes? Why should the men- tion of Greatbridge upset him? The feeling that there was some '7mystery about Liehard Sterry came back to her now as she faced him, and; something cold gripped her heart. Yet what right. had she te feel anythini, .about Richard Sterrv? He was a friend of only a few months' standing. They had never even heard his name before they came to Kemscote Hall, to wait for Geolfrey's return. She had never seen him before they. came there ' • and even now, altheugh ho was their next-door neighbour, and although tl:ey had seen him daily for months, even new it was strange how little they really know of him. He was 'young—scarcely more than thirty; he lived alone on his small estate, close to Kemseote Hall, and he was loved by his dogs and by an old man who attended - to him, and that was all. They knew nothing of his past life, nothing of his history. It was strange, Ethel thought, as she locked into his face, how very little they knew of him. She wished suddenly that he would tell them more. She wished suddenly that she _knew why 'there was that strange shadow upon his face as he stared out at the disappearing village. The great heat of the afternoon was giving way to the cooler air of night when they reached the Hall at last. The .great gates were closed, and a child from the lodge ran forward to meet them, touching his cap as they bowled through:</p>
        <p>Ethel's heart sink heavily. How proud they had been when they came there first to wait for Geoffrey! How delighted they had been with it all a few months ago!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>andidg rigid in the shagow.</head>
        <p>andidg rigid in the shagow.</p>
        <p>She could have d;sa.ppeared in the night. Geoffrey had found an hotel for her, and put her in it. • And when he had gone to his own for the night, why had she not seized the op- portunity to disappear? Why had she not gone while she could? She did not know, and now another sunny morning was there—a day of blue sea .and all Sea all ottha m worldpton sky. looked' gay i)ly)l_yhg blue- bajtlidt htarself...</p>
        <p>She stared out. .Somewhere, a long way out at sea, that bottle containing her confession daneed blue-green and gold on the- blue-gret waves. Somewhere, far- out under the sky, the cruel sea played like a cat with the thing which would some day perhaps put an end to her which,</p>
        <p>She shuddered in the sunshine, aid Geoffrey took a sudden etep towarde her. " Darling, what is the matter?" he asked. ".Why iihould you be nervone at meeting •triy mother ,and e'st.7,;•?. They_will be only -too de- lighted—oh ! I know they will. Mother isn't like most mothers ; and she'll be ;fled to hear I am taking a wife. and gladder still to have you at the Hall with us. Darling, don't he a goose. I've got the tickets. Come on. We have heap; of time, • and we can have lunch on the train, and they are expecting us by the next."</p>
        <p>" Expecting us?" Olga turned swiftly. "I wrote last night," Sir Geoffrey said. " and told them all about you. So they will he quite ready for you. And I—Olga, lon't believe you have any idea how proud and happy I shall be to take you there. Dar- ! Olga darling, if you only cared for me as I care for V 31.1."</p>
        <p>He broke off. checked by her er7. "Geoffrey—Geoffrey, don !', she cried. "You woli't listen to ir.e. You wcn't see any reason. You have only known me for a few weeks, 7. ad----"</p>
        <p>Tie stopped her abruptly. " We.lll we can soon remedy that," he said. `,.` Come home with me, and then I shall see more of you. Won't you even come .on prohation—just to see if I shall get tired of you?" She hesitated. with her eyes out F,C3. Why not go? It was, so hard to tear herself away from him, so hard to leave. ,And Why not go with him—at any rate for a while? No one knew her as Olga Murray. Nothing Se should she not need not marry him, and She could always run away—why should she not go? She turned, with her eves on his. "Oh ! I can't leave you," she cried. "I can't let von go."• He held her to him. ',Olga darling," he cried. "don't tease me again. If I thought you had teen in</p>
        <p>She held back hr head to look at him. " Well—what?" sho aaketl.</p>
        <p>"If I thought you had been in • earned," he repeated, "I think I should bate every woman, for your alc.e. If you gave me up, Olga-. Ile stopped to look with a twitching face at hers, and she shrank and- deny from him as if, until now, she had not realis3d howi much he loved her.</p>
        <p>Her face grew ashen. he seemed to hear the roar of the sea in her ears as she turned away again. If only „he had let her drown how much .ensier it would have been! Why had Heaven let her live? And how could iSho do what she ought to do? How could she go away? And yet how could she stay with he: ghastly secret on her mind? How dared 611.2 stay?</p>
        <p>But, Geoffrey's arms were round her, lift face Mese to hers. "Olga, it would kill me—l couldn't icy, you now, even though I,saw you for the first time not Six weeks ago. What a long time it seems, Olga? Do you remember going el board thc steamer at Melbourne, and ei you remember how you nearly lost your hat'. If it hadn't. been for me, you would have lest it. I rescued it; and that was our first meet- ing. I have never forgotten your face, sleep- ing or waking since. I dreamt of you that night. Do you remember waning the ilecl't the -first night at sea? I tried to talk to you. but you wouldn't have anything to do with me. I remember. I believe I should reix.*7 have got to know you on land. You were sc standoffish ; and if it hadn't been for the wreck, I believe I should have had much harder work to get you to say 'Yea.' "</p>
        <p>She shudeered. His arms were round her shoulders, but Rho -stood with her back to Ws chest, her haudo clasping his in front of her, her eyes still on the sea.</p>
        <p>Somewhere, far out. swam the bottle with the 'confession of her crime within it. Would e• it ever find its way to shore? or would a float - in -spar or a bit of rock knock a hole in it and send it to the bottom?</p>
        <p>A wave of cold passed over her. Geoffrey's voice sounded far away. His lips rested on her dark hair. and he could not see her face. "And so now, darling, I am very pleased we were wrecked, even though I am afraid it will have given my poor mother a bit of a fright. And ,that reminds- me of the train. We mustn't mias it, darling." She stood very still. She could not go. She must not go. She looked round wildly for a way of escape, and there was none, even if she had been strollg enough to take it. Her _</p>
        <p>hat- and coat lay on-a table close by. Geoffrey picked them up. • "Come along." he said. " We'ro in a nice free-and-easy condition—no clothes—no luggage—l Sappese our trunks taking a swim somewhere off the Cape. Well, we'll buy a toothbrush on our way to the station, just to keep ourselves respectable. Are you reldy?"</p>
        <p>Slie was. Almost without realising what she was doing, she slipped into her -jacket. Her I.:ice was set and very grey as she looked into Geoffrey's, and risked herself what else she was to do. She had no money, no friends. in Southampton. There were people, of course, who had heard of the wreck who would be slid to help her to get on her feet again. But what would that Mean? Teaching again. The old drudgery, the old hopeless work until— until the sea left off playing with her conks- 'ion and threw it ashore !</p>
        <p>Well, perhaps it would not he long before it did, and why should she - not snatch at happiness while she could? As she told Dr. Dacre on board the . sinking vessel, she had had no chance in her life. The world had bEen cruel to her. She had never even had the chance of being good. And a good man had never loved her ,until flow! Other men had -,-she shuddered as she thought of other men who had loved her, or pretended to love her, and who had turned life into such a cruel thing for her. Well, it would not be long now, and why not go with Geoffrey until there was some news of that missing bottle? Why not go until the paragraphs announcing its dis- covery appeared in the papers? Why not be happy until then? It would be time enough n to think what to do, where to gp. And perhaps she would find some way of telling Geoffrey the story of her life before then. At li-emscote Hall it might be easier. She put out her hand to him. t"I am ready,' she said.</p>
        <p>He bent and kissed it. His handsome face ss alight in strange and almost' terrible con- trast to hers as they walked to the station together. .</p>
        <p>He had sent for tickets already„ so that there was no delay at the booking-office. • He put his arm through hers and escorted her through the great station. - -</p>
        <p>It -V as sWa-rming with people of all sorts and conditions,--people who All seemed busy, all in a hurry, all eager '.nd full of life.</p>
        <p>At the bookstall Geoffrey hesitated. "I suppose we ought to -keep up appearance by buying some books?" he said. "I'm going to try to look as if I had been ten years married; and your ten-year-old husband al- ways has limps of newspapers so that he shan't- have occasion to look at-h wife."</p>
        <p>He bought a pile, but all the same he did not .loose his arm from hers, and it was in it still as they passed throo3h .on to the plat- form together. Olga drew a deep breath. Now there was no escape. Now ,she would have to go—she would have no other chance now.</p>
        <p>In a queer, dazed way she let Geoffrey do all he wanted. He bought pillows, and settled her comfortably in a corner.</p>
        <p>" And now I'm going to stand and glare at anybody who comes in," ho declared. "You have never seen me glare, have you ? Well, it will, keep everybody away." It did apparently, for when the train began to steam away out, of the station their carriage was empty except for themselves. Then Geoffrey threw down pis papers and went over to, her side. lie took her hand in his, and, pulling off her glove, lcokcd down at it.</p>
        <p>"Now we're off—going home, olga," he said, "to a new home and a new life! wonder what Kemscote Hall is like nowadays? I scarcely remember it, exceilt that it was very big and jolly, and there were 'peacocks, and sets of armour in the hall that made me forget everything else. I WM3 a boy then, and wished. desperately to possess them. But l never dreamt I should. I never dreamt ar this."</p>
        <p>He looked down IA her, but her face was turned away again. They were rushing through green fields and fields of , growing corn, past roads and rivers and tiny hamlets that looked so tempting as theY flew by. Olga stared out at them. She had no Words to say. She had made her .'choice. She was going with Geoffrey—for a time. Going to live in hie home for a few months, if she x:as lucky. She was going to stay with him, within touch of him and within sound of his voice until— until Fate overtook her.</p>
        <p>She wriggled a little nearer to him. He put his arm round her, and she drew a deep sigh. Now she would forget—she would for- get everything except him—everything until she was reminded 'again! It was growing dark when they jelled into Greatbridge Junction. The great stadon was beginning to twinkle with lights, and the singsong of the porters as they ran with the train seemed to come to her sleepily, dully. She looked out carelessly, Geoffrey still oy her side his arm still about her shoulder and his hand still hording hers. She sat up a little as the train stopped. , "Do we change 7" she. asked. '4` "No, darling the train goes on to West Warlingham, and Kennet is the -next place we stop at.' She started ''s'uddenly violently upright. "Kennet Warlingham ? " she repeated, wildly. ,"Yes, darling; Kennet is the name of our station. This. is. Greatbridge." " Greatbridge ! " Her voice wr,s mechani- cal, without tone. "It is the biggest junction hereabouts,' Geoffrey explained. As he spoke the doer opened and -a man got in. Olga fell back a 9 ho stumbled past her. and put his. bag on the reek opposite. " Greatbridge," she •repeated, in a w.hispor. Saidenly a light was shot down into the arriage ceiling above their heads, and Olga $3O k.ue man saw each other face to face.</p>
        <p>Olgs. gave a choked cry and shut her cycti. (7'o be continucd.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LAWN TENNIS.</head>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS.</p>
        <p>PENKETH v. PADGATE.</p>
        <p>- The return match between these cluFs was plased at Penketh on Saturday. The borne side more than avenged their previous reverse by defeating the visitors by nine events to three. For Penketh Mr. Medley played his usual brilliant game, the Misses L. and E. Poster were conspicuons,and the team generally showed decided all-round improve- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>[By !Map.]</p>
        <p>SELECTING AND BUILDING A STRAIN,</p>
        <p>Hupazhard mating and breeding of poultry, spells failr7e, while careful selection in creating pedigree stock cougts for success. Much of the neglect in this branch of poulty fanning is mainly due to ignorance rather than lack of interest, for I feel sure that if . the poultry keeper could only realize the intense interest derived from such a study, be would not hesihte to proceed on the lines I intend to saggest in tl-;s article The question of</p>
        <p>SELECT IoN</p>
        <p>then is the f-st point for our ccnsideration. I recom- mend that your stock should be numbered by carrying a leg These rirtgs are made and sold expressly for the pm pose of keeping emrect records of mdi idual birds. tare:olly observe the habits of your flock, rigidly weeding out undesirable speci- mens, such as those who prefer to mope -lazily about the r ans, feather eaters, and those of 'a puganacious character. Save yoar time and corn bill by placing such birds in the fattening peias and prepare them for the "pot." Keep only those wbich are bright and active. It is easy to pick out spe2imens most likely to make the best layers. You ni" see them out first in the morning and last to roost at right, reaming over the meadows (if on a free range) foraging for natmal food. The best specimens in a confined area are noticeable chiefly for their healthy appearance— tight in feather, red in face and comb and always on the alert, busy in the scratching shed and run, never huddled together on a perch in the sleeping quarters. P -Fier using your observations in the re-inner stated you should then put them in various lots to undergo even a closer inspection, that of being confined to the method of</p>
        <p>TR %I' NESTING,</p>
        <p>More trouble and expense is incurred aS a result. But if you are to create and build up a strain of pedigree layers this system is essential. All the best la ing strains have been produced in a like manner. Just as minute details have to be rigidly observed in the pro- duction of all cther pedigree Stock,.. so it is with poultry. And you can neither get or expect to produce the highest qualities unless a 'scientific method be 'adhered to. The finest lisying sfrains produced in America were the-resrlt of the trap nest system. In our utility lay ing competitions they are often used so tlsat individual merit can be ascertained. The extra troublia_and expense spoken of is amply repaid in the longAun. But I must explain to my readers what a trap'nest is.—An illustrated diagram would have best ser ;ed my purpose. However, I will do my best to explain. A trap-nest is a contrivance which when entered by the hen,automatically closes and makes the bird a prisoner until her duty of laying Pit egg is performed. To look at they most resemble a roomy box with a Fnged door at the top. The front when not occupied is open, as the hen enters and walks off the a"ghting board two doors fold gently together and all is over rntil You, are called by a " cacl-le " to release the prisoner. Then you should gei:tly li the bird out and take her ring number and record the result. One has to admit that such a nrecess is per- haps somewhat tedious. There is no doubt, however, that it is really the orly successful method of keeping correct records, and thus taking the first step in creating a pedigree laying strain. Pullets of the same age should ha penned together and at the end of a certain period of laying you could then go through the records and select according to merit those birds for next season's breeding pen which had given the. best results. Dispose of al t which do not come up to a rensonable standard and give all the spa eat your disposal to the best of your stock. So you go on year by year selecting orly the best even if but few in number. Don'tlose sight of the fact that it costs just as much to keep an inferior bird as it does a good one. Another important item in the breeding pen is that of the ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MALE BIRD</head>
        <p>MALE BIRD</p>
        <p>•which should undoubtedly be chosen with,as great a care a 4 the pullets. He must be a bird bred from a good laying stfain, with . not too large a comb but bright and red, of good stamina and active habits. Ik :rom experience you fud that his progeny turn out well you can mate I, second year with some of the pullets with satisfactoi y results. Do not adopt the system of too close inbreeding, however, for if you do, especially in stock for utility purposes, the result wi". be rather unsa-Csfactory. When you do' introduce new blood see that you get a male, bird that has been bred f..om a good strain, whatever the breed •may be. It is strain that counts much more than blood or breed. I would aNcn prefer the red blood Of the peasant, than the blue blooded aristocrat. By jud;cious selection and careful mating and breeng you will eventually build up a strain of egg producers that will jay.' ,As time goes on la3lng strains Will have been So vastly improved that little difficulty and trouble will be explienced in obtaining pedigree stock, I fancy I hear someone say: " \rely well, then; I am content to wait until they come." Let me give you a note of wrning. You are mal,ing a mistake and ir;ssing opportunities by thus Waiting. As with all industrial progress so with scientific poultry farming. "He who hesitates is lost." It is the man who obtains first fruits that gains the best prices, Let me illustrate from the fanciers point of view. When a new breed of fancy poultry is being boomed people who take up such a breed generally go to the fountain head for their stock. The consequence is that the original breeder can command fabulous prices. It will be more so in a few years than it is now, that the poultry farmer who makes utility poultry his special study .will be the than who will get the most profit. So let me advise my interested readers not to wait, but set .about improving your stock and therefore improving your pceket as a result.</p>
        <p>THE BACKYARDER.</p>
        <p>Last week I promised to give a few seasonable hints to the backyard poultry farmer. It is chiefly on the question of cleanliness that I wish to write. 1, have heard complaints from many who do not keep fowls 'as to ,the ditiv conditions of fowls at.d "ens kept ,by their neighbours. Smelling drains and dangerous diseases may result from careless manage- ment. You should scrape the floor of your pens at least once ,a week and cover lighily over with P • - owdered lime. Rinse well all the grids and drains and disiCfect with carbolic. Lime wash your houses and add to the limewash a Fttle paraffin oil so as to destroy and keep down vermin. If you carry out these simple details you will hear less complaints. Not only so, but your stock will benefit by it, and you will have no fear of the condemnation of either neigh- bours or sanitary officials, 1 shall not give further details now, as my next week's article will apply equally to the back'-yarder- as to others. For ' intend to write on the " Moulting Season " which I' demands special requirements and attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
