      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LOYAL PRINCE OF WALES LODGE.</head>
        <p>LOYAL PRINCE OF WALES LODGE.</p>
        <p>P.P.G.M. T. P. BRADSHAW AND STATE SICK PAVC; The annual smoker and distribution of prizes in Oa- nection with the "Losal Prince of Wales" 1-n4T bowline handicap took place on Saturday evening f't, the Pelican Hotel. There was a la4e attendance. chair was occupied 1 y P.P.G.M. T. I'. Brachlriw, scp" was supported by Prov. Grand Master G. F. Freer:"'Deputy Grand Master T. P. Forbes I.P.P.G M. L'" Brooks, Ploy. C. S. F. Webster' I*.b. F. Wakefield, V.G. P. McLachlan, L.G.M. T. F. Hughes, P.P.€3•°. J. Massey, PG. W. King, T. Wadsworth, F. Webster,- E. C!a3ton (secreta7). The Grand 51as ter made tbie presentation of prizes to the following brethren : William Daniels; 2, J. Lister; 3, W. Woods; 4, 1' Taylor; 5, A. Stringer; 6, H. Coo' ; 7, A. Duckworth; B,J. Higham; 9,1). kherwood ' • 10. J. Wretch, 119,1 k; E. Thornley ; 12, D. Bromley •, 13, J. Hitchrnough ; „r G. F. Freemen; 15, E. Finch; 16, R. ThornleY.: musictl programme was gone through songs bST. rendered by Bros. A. Both., H. Bennett, F. Moon, McLachlan, Higham, F. Webster (pianist). P.P.G.M. T. P. Bradshaw bef, re c =Bing upon the' Grand Master of the District to present the Prile; complimented the competitors on the success of t annual bowling handicap and informed the memberos that the Competition was 'promoted with a view t, ha. cncouraging the social side of Oddlellowship, so the members would take a greater interest in the affl3o' of the Lodge. The position of the Lodge could not maint tined without the support of the members and bp: urged the mernters to rualise that a share of t, responsibility rested on their shoulders. Financial they were in a sotind position the total worth of the, Lodge at June. 30th being ,'.23,533, which was wel` invested and that wts a strong recommenistion intending members. During the past stx in )nths th . bad paid out in sic' ply the sum of £203 103. 4 thirtecn new members had been admitted and the memberthip was 488.</p>
        <p>SUPPORT FOR GOVERNMENT SCHEME. The question of State sick pay %cis aclrg at present engaging the attention of the leaders friendly societies throughout the c )untry and thcle he had no authority to speak to the District or t', Lodge he personally vias in Livour of the scheme 3; submitted by the Chancellor of the Exchequer. Is' cr,tl to be regretted that the Grand Master of the Ileot-cd. should in his address at the hist A.M.C. at Bradfofd attacked the scheme, %hich I venture to suggest wollit be to the best interests of the friendly so :teties. % f should be remembered that there wire in the 011'3 the country thousands of men were not members of 0?1-4 society, probibly did not know of friendly societte- and had those unfor:unete individuals to be cast int° the gutter ? No, in the n tree of clrrity they must be as.sisted. Finally they had the assurancs of the GoverePl merit that the existing friendly societie; machinery '9'3: to be utilised and every member of a friendly socie„ty' straight on the books xvon!d s exceupt frifin deductions from his wages. lie fully support el ti, scheme for State sick say. (Loud applause).</p>
