        <p>WARRINGTON v. TOXTETH. These tams tried conclusions at the Legh-street xraths on Tuesday evening. Both sides were at full strength. In the initial half Toxteth were called upon to detend the deep end, having loss the toss. Warring- Were soon on the aggressive and after an exciting bLut, To ter succeeded in notching a goal from 1-11 10'. Shortly afterwards Pemberton registereianother. ; " Play was exceedingly rough and exciting, the .Jovenile speeltors repeatedly "hooting' and "booing " C referee when his decisions did not commend them- Several players were cautioned fur ' fouling." either team sow :ded ;ra again scorieg i this 1,41 r. The second arrived with Warringtmr two goals ahead. ;I'e second half saw Toxteth stickinm manft."y to their task and Woody er bad repeated Lard hr 's In not tearing from three-quintet. way. Ince was -Prominent and came to the rescue of the home team in l_teveral de,tgeroes situations. From a long rll4 Turner rufeated tte Toxteth custodian by a well .irned ball. vte:o made an unsuccessful attempt at goal, the ball I,l:king the crossbar and bouncing out of play. From , ead pass by Pepper, who played an exceedingly fine I:l'2er Pemberton scored. Woodyer, fur the opposing ,a‘l`,llll, now came to the fore, and after two splendid `eorpts from thrt 2-quarter way, which were cleverly :Lrostrated by Woods, the third ball sailed gncefully a°tve.r, Woods's outstretched hands, and hit the goal net tiat" bock- After a few minutes play time wei o,pro- Warrington winning by five goses to cur Mr. Imam McDonald (of Liverpool) was the referee.</p>
