      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>NOTES ON GOES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GOES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Warrington had hard luck *on Satu-dly. They went t 3 Stockport w:th a. strong side, determined to do uttrost to avenge their only defeat. of the season, but Ole elements were against them. After half an hour's play rain fell heavi'y and as tliere was no prospect of an improvement at the watch was abandoned. Warrington had made 15 for the loss of three wickets, J. Callon (18) and W. Richardson -(l7) being the Chief contributors.</p>
        <p>To-day Warrington entertain, St. Helen i Rees., and Cresswell, the home professional, will take a. well- earned benefit.</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd tried e3nclusions 11 Stocipart 2nd and an exciting game 'ended in a victory for the Warringtonians by a margin of 12. Batting first, Stockport amassed 101, G. 1: Baker being responsible. for 31. W. Bennett with five wickets for 40 runs and J. Waywell with three for 31 were the most successflo bowlers. Wan ington start 3d unpromisingly, a wicket falling with only, a s;ngle - on the score sheet, but a splendid stand by T. Houghton and J. W. Bainlnidge, who made 22 each, put a brighter' .compleN:on on the game. W. V. Turton was also in grand form and knocked up 29.</p>
        <p>In the West Lancashire L.:ague Prescct gained a iidienlouly easy victory at the &amp;pus° of Earles- town,. W. A. shall mak:ng the fine score of 57 not out and taking four wickets for 16 runs. St. Helens had litde difficulty in di-posing of Newton.</p>
        <p>The following are the League tables up-to-date :</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION. ,Chub. P. W. L. D.. Pts. Prescot Warrington St. He'ens Recs. St. Helens 6... 2 ... 2 ... 2 ... EA rlestown fNe%ton No points deducted for playing ineligible player. SECOND DIVISION. •</p>
        <p>Prescot St. Helens Rees Warrington Eirlestovrn</p>
        <p>Newton St. He!ens</p>
        <p>St. James's, the champions of the Warrington and District League, are stiil going strong and eit Satur- day they inflicted a heav defeat on Wdderspool Brewery. Declaring their innings at 131 for 6 wickets they dismissed their opponents for 40. J. Hallam was in lnilliant fot at and amassed 63. The bowlers had a good time, Constable taking five wickets for 18 runs', Holywo for 20 and Hallam two for none.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists severely trounced Whitecross creation, M. LlewellyA (32) and L. Walker (29) being the top scorers. E. Cliffe and A. Fletcher wrought great havoc with the hall, capturing five wickets for 22 runs and five for 15 respectively. For the losers Lavill batted well for his 21. 11. Wright with five for •34 and 11. Maybury with two for nine bowled effectively.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Junior League St. Mays triumphed over llowley and Stretton and Appleton beat thford by a margin of 61. 11. Brown played splendidly for the victors, placing 41 iins to his credit.</p>
        <p>Warrington Altry'cipal Officers went down at llarbury, not one of their representatives reaching double figures.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills visited Hough Green and had the satisfaction of beating the villagers by 45 runs. Batting first, the visitors put together 86, J. Whitfield (24) giving the best display. Cole bcwled well for Hough Green, taking four wickets for 23 runs. Monks captured four wickets at a cost of two runs each. Hough Green made a weak stand and were dismissed for 41 runs, the bowling of Woodcock and Whitfield giving them a great deal of trouble.</p>
        <p>Penketh Friends'-School disposed of Hough Green</p>
        <p>2nd, by a margin of 64. R. Brown played spendidly for the victors, placing 44 runs to his credit.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool and Latchford engaged in friendly rivalry, the first-named winning by 19 runs. H. Holmes (31) and E. Jackson (20) bitted creditably for their respective sides.</p>
        <p>Monks, Hall and Co., made a roor display against Latchford Ban;ists, who owed their success mainly to the bowling Of Conway and Cox who took four wickets for five runs and five for ten runs respectively.</p>
        <p>,Grapperhall gave Altrincbam Wesleyans a bad time, for after declaring their innings at 110 for 8 they got the Wesleyans out for 31. A. Wilson batted brilliantly for his 45.</p>
        <p>Altrinebam Wesleyans 2nd turned the tables against Grappenhall 2nd,, defeating them by 42.</p>
        <p>Lymm were vanquished by Hale but their neigh- bours at Oughtiington Park were victorious in their encounter .0 Ath Broughton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The result of the two f,ames a last Saturday in connection uith the Works Bowling Competition showed a remarkable coincidence, both the winnivg teams scoring 166 and bah gaiqing a majority of 13.</p>
        <p>Fletcher's No. 1 met Rylands' No. 1 and were defeated. Fletcher's stai..ed with an advantage of 5: Rylands' being scratch. Very good play was witnessed throughout the game, the scoring being very close.</p>
        <p>One of the finest games was between F. Heesom (Fletcher's) and A. Stringer (Rylands'), the former only winning by the nal row margin ot 1. The players kept weir up with each other, pealing at 3 and 7. Then Iletcher's representative gained the lead, reaching 13, whilst his opponent orly added another one. At this point St:inger pulled himself together, as it were, and w :01 some fine woods peeled at 13. He added another point before Heesom, but in the enel he was unsuccessful, his contestant winning with a clouble.</p>
        <p>Another strange feature of the day's play was that two men of the same name—J. -Woods-7-tried conclu- sions. Besides fatnishing a coircidence,they also pt.( • vided a.fine game. They equalized at 7, and then Fletcher's man gained a lead of 3. Ryland's repre- sentative pulled up peeling at 12. Again they were level, this time at 14, arid after a k itly contested game the honours went to Rylands'.</p>
        <p>W. Roughley for Rylands' No. 1, had almost a " walk over. " defeating J. Winstauley by 9 'points. ,</p>
        <p>That the match between Armitage and Rigby (10) and Corporation (5) was a fine one, is evident from the narrow margin of 13, by which the Corporation won. Excellent contests were seen, and the enthusiasm of the spectators was intense.</p>
        <p>Towards the end of the game between J. Wilson (Armitage and Rigby) and Vv. Robinson (Corporation) the play was extremely exciting, for the former having at first been behindhand, increased h's total wonder- fully well. Robinson had obtained 5 before his opponent could put one to his credit ; but Wilson reached 10, without his contestant stirring. Then the woods began to" fly." and Robinson got within two of Wilson. Wilson increased his figures to 11, and Robinson to 10, but the lattee with a fine effort peeled at 14,, and with his n?xt wood won the game.</p>
        <p>J. Jolley (Coi„)oration) easily accounted for P. -.awless, gaining a icto:: of 0 poiats.</p>
        <p>S. Riley (it.-mitp.ge and Rigby) made a good show against J. T. Massey. Towards the close uf the game Massey was 13, whilst his opponent was 9 behind. Riley then played brilliantly and increased his total to 10, but he was too late ani Massey won by a majority of 5.</p>
        <p>Another gool game was ‘played by A. Newey (Corporation) and C. North. The former kept a slight lead up to 11, when they peeled. They again equal- ised at 12, and North registered a victory by a margin of 3.</p>
        <p>The Crosfield National Challenge Cup is not to be the proud possession of the County Palatine during the next twelve months, for Lancashire and .Chcahire have been beaten in the:r matches with -Stafford-shire by 58 points. Several Warrington players partici- pated in the retain match on\Satarday and did god seivie.3. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Swimm!nf.</head>
        <p>Swimm!nf.</p>
        <p>The plunge batlis in Leigh-sereet have been largely ratronised of late, and on Sattnday afteinoons especially the first and. second class baths are filled with devotees of the use:al and exhiliarating sport.</p>
        <p>Now the arnual gala is in sight, intending ent2antc are pra!Ccing theic respective disances and" record times " are the order of the day.</p>
        <p>Bravo Worrington ! They have achieved the dis- tinction of cl-ecking Toxteth's unbeatable career. By a great efroft on Tuesday night they registered 5 well earned goals to ToN:eib's one and thus inflicted upon the vir;tof s their first defeat this reason, it was a game well ,worar seeing and there was a large "gate." Oae unsatisfactoiy feature was the almost innumerable fouls which had to be pointed out by the referee (Mr. W. McDonald) on both tildes.</p>
        <p>All the home team played a splendid game in their respective departments and Pemberton, Tv-Aer, Ince and Beaman were especia'ly prominent. That was a splendid bit of work by 'nutter • when he made a sae7-essrui start from three-quart&amp; way in the first half, and PLatbetton with: his usual strong shots eaEi'y beat Watkins, the Toxeth custodian. •</p>
        <p>A word of praise should be accorded to C. Wocds, Warriurton's chanirion goal keeper, who appears to be (Ilya) at hcine with a host of foemen struggling in his goal mouth.</p>
        <p>Pepper was in his usual form, and although not oring himself, wsts instrumental in Pemberton Fcoring the last try by a smart pass to that player.</p>
        <p>Woodyer and /%'rd played fine games for Toxteth, and were conspicuous whenever thaWai rington goal was in danger. Woodyer should be congratulated on his scientific long shots, which are generally splendidly directed to nati•owly miss the crossbar and pt zzle the goalkeeper.</p>
        <p>The following teams have entered for the Squadron Races during the season :--Cc operative v. Orosfield's, Ryland's v. Cot poration Post 01fie 3 v. Walker's, and P. &amp;K. v. Monks, Hall and Co.. The four winning teams will swim off on the Gala night.</p>
        <p>A " Red Letter " night is in anticipation on August 12th, when Wari:ngton will play Wavertree A new feature is promised for this occasion in the shape of a ladies' fifty yards race. A goodly number of 'entries is anticipated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
