      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 17TH. 1909.</p>
        <p>Lord GORELL,who was DRINK AND until recently known as DIVORCE. Sir GORELL BARNES, President of the Admir- alty and Divorce Division, made a curiously, interesting and sympathetic spee6h in the House of Lords on Wednesday on the sub- ject of facilitating the access of the poor man or the poor and wronged woman, to the relief provided by the divorce law. - His argument, was based on the exceeding difficulty and costliness of divorce proceedings, and he moved a resolution to the effect that it is expedient `that jurisdiction to a limited extent in divorce and matrimonial cases should be conferred upon County Courts in order that:the poorer classes may have their cases of that nature heard and determined in such courts. The opposition to this pro- posal came, of course, from the Church, which tends more and more to the adoption of the hard and fast lines of the Roman Catholic Church on this subject, and goes to the length, in some cases, of refusing marriage to divorced persons. The Arch- bishop of Canterbury and Lord Halifax, the President of the English Church Union (of whose opinion and motives, of course, we speak with the utmost respect), declared active hostility to a resolution which did not propose to change the law in any respect, but only to make it more uniformly operative.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that .a case is so well and lucidly and simply stated, either in the House of Lords or elsewhere, as was Lord GORELL'S case for making divorce no more difficult to the poor than it is.to the rich. There was only one startling statement in his speech, but this must have conveyed to his auditors a most decided thrill. He said that if the habit of drinking intoxicants could be abolished the Divorce Court might as well close its doors. That is really a tremendous dictum when we think of it, and it comes from a man of cautious and legal mind, who has had every possible opportunity of obser-' vation. As, however, the habit is one that is more or less ingrained in the race, we cannot with reason look forward to its extinction, though happily _there are many signs that over-indulgence in intoxicating drinks is gradually becoming more rare. However, until the beginning of the Millennium or thereabouts, we shall have to look the drinking habit in the face as an indisputable fact, and to keep ourseNes pre- pared to deal with its consequences. But we take the statement of Lord GORELL to be something very- different from the repre- sentations that are made by the novelists, and extensively believed in by their readers. It is a declaration, on the part of a man of ' unique experience ,on this subject, that marital unfaithfulness does not spring mainly from incompatibility of temper, or even excess of the lower passions, but from a com- bination of these with the unnatural excite- ment produced by drink.</p>
        <p>Lord GORELL contends that the difficul- ties thrown in the way of divorce in the case of persons of restricted means is not in the interests of morality. These difficulties are among the causes of bigamy, he maintains, and they have many other consequences that, to use a favourite phrase, are more easily imagined than described. The Church declines to regard the matter from this point of view. It stands by thek saying, " Whom God has joined together, let no man put asunder," not considering perhaps that God has not always joined together those who have been joined either by the Church, the minister, or the registrar. Lord GORELL'S argument seems to us to be not only strong, but irrefragible. The law recognises the right for adequate reasons. All men should be equal before the law. Therefore, divorce should be made no more difficult for the poor than for the rich. We have placed the argument in the form of a syllogism, and it seems to us, as it seemed to Lord GORELL, that it cannot be met by any syllogism more powerful. Even, he said, if the amount of divorce business before the County Courts became so excessive as to be embarrassing, that is a circumstance that should not be considered. The one real consideration is that justice should be done. As the Govern- ment, through Lord WOLVERHAMPTON, promised that the matter should be made the</p>
        <p>subject of inquiry, Lord GORELL'S motion was withdrawn, We may conclude, there- fore, thst we are on the. verge of another great legal reform.</p>
        <p>There are at least two • OBSTACLES TO COM- insuperable obstacles PULSORY SERVICE. to the acceptance, by</p>
        <p>the sober sense of the British public, of any such proposals as those which Lord Roberts and Lord Milner advo- cafed in the debate in the House of Lords. Any form of compulsory service would be fatal to the fulfilment of those unique con- ditions which are presented by the problem of the defence of our island home and world- wide Empire. And compulsion is utterly repugnant to national sentiment, and would be in flagrant hostility to some of the most salient features of national life. As an island people, for whom the avenue, of our food. supplies and of our communication with the other branches of the Empire are on the high- way of the seas, naval supremacy is the first condition of our safety and our strength. To ask us, as Lord Roberts and Lord Milner do, to approximate our military system to that of Continental nations, with enormous land-frontiers, and practically no colonies is to ask us to starve our Navy in order Ito support a bloated and useless military armament. And the striking-force of Regulars which we need for an emergency in any distant part of our Empire can only be recruited by voluntary means. If we are to adopt compulsion, for the sake of an unnecessary " landwehr," we shall starve not only the Navy, but this equally vital necessity of an expeditionary force. While the objections on the side of National defence are so strong, the objections ofi the moral side are not less valid and those Bishops who talk of compulsory service as wholesome discipline are Churchmen talking of what they do not understand.</p>
        <p>The Town Council of. SENIORITY.' Tuesday made another A VEXED effort tot determine the QUESTION, definition of seniority, and altered the Stand- ing Orders for the third time since 1895. Whether we have now arrived at a permanent settlement of this vexed question is very much open to doubt. In fact, the personal equation is so often a primary factor in dis- turbing this Standing Order and causing principle to be thrown to the winds that we have little faith in the new ruling as to seniority being observed 'with any more exactness in the future than in the past. Seniority by virtue of uninterrupted service was tried eleven years ago and did not give unmixed satisfaction. Five years later it was abandoned because it created an injustice, and it is now reaffirmed to obviate another injustice. The only difference between the first and last Standing Orders is that the new one specifies 15 months instead of three months as the period during which a mem- ber may be out of office without losing count of previous service. Last week we chose to describe the situation as G-ilbertian. Coun- cillor Henry Roberts quarrels with our inter- pretation of the humorous, and challenges our statement of fact. We can only retort that the humour has developed into farce, and by examining the facts justify our deductions. It is no new thing to find that one man's hum Our may be another man's poison, but the onlooker has a free and unquestionable right to see humour and smile thereat when and where he will.</p>
        <p>Now as to the question</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR of fact. Mr. Roberts ROBERTS AND THE said on Tuesday that "EXAMINER." the article in the Examiner "srnade it out—and he knew it had been the impression which several people had held, because he had had to contradict it before—that the old Standing Order enabled him to profit by being elected an alderman over the head of his respected friend, Alderman Pierpoint." We did not "make it out" quite so clearly as Mr. Roberts has put it, because we wished to avoid any implica- tion of unfairness. What we said was this: "Eleven years ago Councillor Henry Roberts was elected an alderman -by virtue of just such a Standing Order in preference to Councillor William Pierpoint," and we stand by our assertion. Councillor Roberts does not prove the contrary by his ready use of figures. The fact may be admitted that at the time of his elevation he had thirteen years and six months uninter- rupted service against Councillor Pierpoint's twelve years and six months, with a break of three years. Under the Standing Order Mr. Roberts was the senior Councillor, and was elected as such. But he "omits to mention one or two other facts which are material to the point at issue. He does not say, for instance, that in the year previous to his election as Alderman he was largely responsible for getting the signatures of all the members of the Council to an agreement promising to abide by the Standing Order as to seniority; nor does he mention that he was the first Councillor to profit by the arrangement; or that at the election itself Councillor Pierpoint made a speech in which he said he had signed under a misapprehension this document or agreement, which was brought to him by Councillor Henry Roberts himself. Mr. Pierpoint expressed his dis- satisfaction at an arrangement which prevented him counting his previous twelve years' service,and, reading between the lines, it is quite obvious that he thought Councillor Roberts had been elected Alderman "over his head." The three facts which rebut Councillor Roberts' contention to-day are: 1. That the old Standing Order prevented Councillor Pierpoint being Alderman, and enabled Councillor Roberts to be elected • 2, that Councillor Roberts was instrumental in getting the Council to sign an agreement observing seniority in Aldermanic elections ; 3, that Councillor Roberts was the first member to be elected Alderman after he had persuaded.Councillor Pierpoint,among others to sign the agreement in question. We there- fore re-affirm that by virtue of this old Standing Order, Councillor Roberts was elected Alderman in preference to Councillor Pierpoint who entered the Council two years before Mr. Roberts.</p>
        <p>Mr. Roberts in .1898 REASON FOR A was proud of the TIME LIMIT. seniority rule and said,</p>
        <p>after his aldermanic election," I feel in honour bound to respect it on every occasion it is brought before the Council." Yet on Tuesday he voted against Councillor Wilkinson's motion to restore a Standing Order on all fours with the one which he himself brought into operation eleven years ago. In this case, how- ever, it deprives Councillor Roberts of seniority because he has lost his claim to an unbroken tenure of seivice, by an absence from the Council of over six Srears. The motion was carried by 14 votes to 6 because the Council felt that some time limit was necessary under the circumstances brought about by the return of Mr. Roberts after so long an interval. Though a man is entitled to full credit for his previous service, it is "obviously unreason- able "—as the Manchester Evening News says very succintly—" for a gentleman who</p>
        <p>has been out of the Council for some years to expect to regain his seniority on re-election at the expense of those who have been more fortunate in keeping their seats."</p>
        <p>The last few days MARRIED WOMEN` have witnessed a TEACHERS. startling denouement</p>
        <p>to the case at issue between the Corporation (representing the Education Committee) and the married women teachers who have received notice to _ terminate their engagements because marriage is considered an educational dis- qualification. The point has never been settled at law, and two of the teachers had taken steps in the Chancery Court to secure an injunction against the Corporation. At a special meeting of the Education Committee on Wednesday, the Town Clerk, acting on Counsel's opinion, advised the Committee to withdraw the notices, and this was forth- with done by letter to the ladies concerned in the action. The question therefore remains undecided, and is _complicated by the fact that several other married teachers resigned rather than receive notice. What the upshot of it all may be we cannot pretend to guess. The future of the married women teachers will certainly have to be determined both in law and eduity, and we would suggest that 1 the Board of Education take steps to make the situation clear and incontestable. Neither' the teachers nor the responsible authorities know quite where they are at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIO3S</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIO3S</p>
        <p>Slot gas meter users are anxiously await- ing the concessions which they were promised some time ago. The delay has been as to the most suitable method of carrying the change into effect. Originally the intention was to grant a discount but it has since been con- sidered that this arrangement would not be the most workable. At a meeting of the Gas Committee yesterday, the committee decided to allow the slot meter users a larger quantity of gas for the amount they are at present paying. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Alexander Gordon C. Harvey, M.P. for Rochdale, who will address the meeting in support of the Budget at Bridge Foot on Monday evening, is a native of Cheshire. He was educated privately and at Owen's College, Manchester, and has taken a prominent and successful part in commercial life. He is a cotton manufacturer and merchant, a partner in the firm of Harvey and Fothergill, Manchester and Little- borough, an alderman of the Lancashire County Council and a justice of the peace.</p>
        <p>*-* * This sadly disloyial story found its way from .jauite exalted 'lips to rather humbler ears. and iso to this extremely humble pen (says the "Manchester Guardian.") At luncheon at Buckingham Palace the other day one of the young princes of Wales began "Oh ! grandpapa," when the King interposed with some severity, 'Little boys should be seen and not heard. Go on with your luncheon, and don't talk." Collapse of small prince. To him presently the King relent- ing "Well, now you can say what you were going to say." Prince, with a world of meaning: "Too late, grandpapa ; too late." The King: " Nonsense I If it was worth saying five minutes ago it is worth saying now." Prince, fir lily : No, grandpapa. There was a great enormous slug in your salad, and you've eaten him." * *</p>
        <p>Mr. Herbert Samuel, who returns again to thi3' House of Commons as member for Cleveland, has been one of the marked successes of the present Ministry. He entered Parliament in 1902, after two unsuccessful endeavours in Oxfordshire, and he at once took a commanding place in the Assembly. In 1905 he became Under-Secretary for the Home Office, and in that capacity did brilliant work not only in administration but in the House itself. His skilled conduct of the. Children's Bill was a masterly perform- ance, which marked him out for higher office whenever a vacancy should arise, as it did on the retirement of Lord Fitzmaurice. A. skilled debater, with an excellent Parlia- mentary manner, Mr. Samuel will continue to be a strength to the Ministry in the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>In some new houses which have recently been built at Knutsford a very pretty idea has been carried out to encourage the bird- loving propensities of the occupants. The idea is to leave nesting-places for our feathered friends (says Country Life.") Instead of filling up the holes left by the scaffolding, the architect had closed them with a thin covering a stucco, pieced with a round hole. The birds enter and build inside. Sometimes ybu may see a tiny step just below for the bird to alight on, and a little cornice over the gap to keep out the rain. Other holes have also been purposely left in the brickwork for nests, and it is said that the birds understand it all perfectly. Many bird-lovers will, no doubt, be glad to follow this good example and provide homes for the birds when they are building their own. The only objection to us appears to be that the greedy and übiquitous sparrows are more than likely to appropriate the whole of the nesting sites. * * *</p>
        <p>The famous petition in favour of Free Trade presented to the House of Commons in 1820 by the London merchants-16 which reference is made in the striking manifesto on the same subject now issued by City bankers and business men—was drafted by Thomas Tooke. This gentleman was not only an eminent economist and statistician, but a leading merchant as well. In 1820, the "Free Trader" recalls, the British tviff was all embracing, over 1,109 Acts of Parliament imposing Customs duties being in full operation. It was at that period, too, that the question of admitting a mummy caused great trouble among the Customs officials. Finally it was decided that the mummy must be classed as a manufactured article, and, as such, liable to an ad valorem duty of 100 per cent. No one needs to be told that the protective system of those days had none of the advantages which the Tariff Reformers of the present day assert would follow the adoption of their proposals. * *</p>
        <p>Coroner Brighouse seems to become more intolerably autocratic every-day, and many 1 of the inquests he conducts are more like inquisitions than inquiries. Witnesses, instead of receiving the protection of the Court, stand a good chance of being bullied by the Coroner if their answers do not satisfy him in all respects. Yesterday he fell foul 1 of a junior house-surgeon from the Infirmary because he did not give an exact opinion on the cause of death in response to an inquiry from P.C. Woollacott, the Coroner's officer, on the telephone. In our opinion Dr. Green gave, all the information that could be reasonably expected. Medical opinions have to be given with circumspec- tion and not produced hastily at the demand either of the Coroner or the Coroner's officer. Mr. Brighouse was merely rude when he advised the witness to consult some member</p>
        <p>of his profession with more experience and his reflection on young house surgeons generally was a characteristic piece of impertinence. Some day we hope a self- reliant witness will remind the Coroner that while he may understand the law, he is not omniscient.</p>
        <p>* * * In regard to one matter at least the Town Council was ruled by sentiment on Tuesday. Two of our best-known seedsmen, Messrs. Pierpoint and Starkey, have been endeavour- ing to obtain a licence for the sale of poisons under the Poisons and Pharmacy Act, 1908, and their applications have been refused by the Health Committee after careful consider- ation. Councillor H. Roberts succeeded in getting the minute of the Health Committee referred back on an amendment, the principal argument being that these tradesmen ought to have the privilege of selling weed-killers, sheep.-dip and the like poisonous compounds often required by their customers. According to the Act the licences should only be given to seedsmen when proper facilities for the requirements of the district are not provided by themists, and we think the Health Com- mittee was quite right in upholding this view. It ma seem hard on the seedsmen, but the sale of poisons is of too serious a nature to be lightly sanctioned, and senti- ment ought not to enter into the question at all. People are finding it much too easy to purchase poisons in various forms, and in our opinion the sale should be restricted entirely. —at least in the towns—to the pharma- ceutical chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS. j</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS. j</p>
        <p>In Support of the Budget.</p>
        <p>A den-fonstration in support of the Budget will be held at Bridge Foot (near Arpley Cannons) on Monday evening when the principal speakers will be Mr. A. Harvey, M.P., for Rochdale, and Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. Mr. E. Wynne Jones will preside. In case of wet weather, the meeting will be held in Cairo-street schools. •</p>
        <p>Local Wedding. • The marriage took place very quietly on Saturday of Mr. Joseph Baxter. clerk at . the Great-Western Railway Company's receiving office, Sankey-street, and son of Mr: George Baiter, of Chester, and Miss May Thompson, daughter of Mrs. Thompson, of Suez- street, Warrington. There was only a small gathering of • intimate friends at the ceremony. -Mr. Norman Latham discharged the duties of best man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>Presentation at the Secondary School.</head>
        <p>Presentation at the Secondary School.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the scholars of the -Secondary School adjourned for the summer holiday, and the breaking up proceedings were characterized by a very pleasing incident. Miss Frost, a teacher at the school, has resigned her position owing to her approaching marriage, and she was presented with .a. handsome silver soup dish and a bouquet by Miss E. Cooper, on behalf of the scholars of the school. She was also the reCipient of a travelling bag from the scholarship class, the presentation being mule by- Miss E. Swaine. —Miss Frost, replying, said that she hoped the school would prosper. She felt it very much at leaving, as her whole heart had been centred in the work of the school since its foundation.</p>
        <p>Cycling Club • " President's Day" in connection with the Warring- ton Cycling Club was celebrated on Saturday last when the members visited in good numbers the home of their President, Mr. John Naylor, • at Beeston Towers. A fine, breezy day favoured the party, and the twenty members who journeyed by wheel had a delightful run through Delatnere Forest. At Beeston they were met by the ladies and other members who had gone by train, dnd about fifty sat down to tea. Afterwards a musical programme was gone through, Miss Armstrong and Mr. Hassall contributing, solos, while Mr. Percy Smith was the accompanist. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. NaylOr for the splendid manner in which he had entertained them.</p>
        <p>Open-Air Musical Festival. The Workingmen's Committee of the Warrington Infirmary have organized an open-air musical festival in aid of the institution, and it will be held in the Bank Park on Thursday evening next at seven o'clock. The programme is a most attractive one, and given fine weather there, should be a very large attendance. The Warrington Male Voice Choir and. the Apollo Male Voice Choir (combined) under the conductorship of Mr. Harry Berrey, will sing such favourite selections as " Soldiers' Chorus" (Gounod), "On the Ramparts" (Saintes), "Destruction of Gaza" (De Rale), "Martyrs of the Arena" (De Rale), and several other choice items. Crosfield's Band, conducted by Mr. G. F. Bedforth, will play some of their most popular pieces, including "Poet and Peasant" (Suppe), "Tannhauser" (Wagner), the descriptive piece" A sailor's life " (S. Cope), and "William Tell," including the famous storm movement, (Rossin;). There will be no charge for admission to the park, but a collection will be made and all entering the park will be expected to contribute. There will be a number of chairs, for which a small charge will be made. GOOD SECOND-HAND PIANOS, R 5 55., £l3 . _</p>
        <p>and £l2 12s. Others at higher 'prices.' Fully. guaranteed. Pianos tuned and repaired,—J. R. WOOD, 47. London rd., St.nkton Heath. Advt. Alliance Box Convany's Picnic. The Alliance Box Company's employes had a very pleasant excursion to Matlock on Tuesday, 13th, which was due to some extent to the recent Royal visit to the town. The Alliance Directors, recognising that their works were too far from the route of the Royal procession to make it worth while to decorate them, decided to celebrate the occasion by a treat to their workpeople. This occasion resulted in an invitation to all those employed at the Alliance Works to a dinner and tea, and it was left to those invited to decide where the treat was to be held. By unanimous, consent Matlock was selected, and on Tuesday last the party, to the number of 206; left the Central station in a special train supplied by the Midland Railway Co. Although the weather was wet on leaving Warrington, it was found on arrival that no rain had fallen for some days. This circumstance made the drive through Matlock and New Bath Hotel all the more enjoyable, and the dinner and tea was</p>
        <p>also thoroughly- iippreciated. No expense beyond the railway fare fell on the firm's employees, the rest of the entertainment being provided by the employers. Many of the party secured conveyances and proceeded to some of the beauty spots, for which Matlock district is famous. Others visited the caves and tors in the immediate vicinity, and when the return train left Matlock Bath station all those who participated in the excursion felt that they had enjoyed a very happy day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>TENNIS, CROQUET, AND GARDEN FURNTITE.— J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warr ington.—Advt.</head>
        <p>TENNIS, CROQUET, AND GARDEN FURNTITE.— J. S. GARLAND, Bridge st., Warr ington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Musical Successes. The following pupils of Miss Maud Jennings, A.V.C.M., 140, Wilderspool Causeway, passed very successfully at the Victoria College of Music, Manchester Centre, July sth, by Dr. Bentley : Advancecl senior grade piano, Miss Clarice Daly, 88 marks, Ist class honours; junior piano, Lily Penketh, Stockton Heath, 86 marks, Ist class honours; primary piano, Bessie M. Jones, 85 marks, ht class honours; sylvester Hall, piano, 80 marks. Ist class honours; preparatory piano, Doris Hosker, 95 marks, Ist class honours; Ellen Sharpies, piano, 90 marks, Ist class</p>
        <p>onours. At the recent Trinity College of Music examinations held at the Liverpool Centre the following pupils of Mr. W. FL Payton, Mus. Bac., were successful in the Senior Division Pianoforte :—Miss Gladys Rose Hale (Leigh Villas, Grappenhall), obtained honours, gaining 90 marks ; 3liss Gwendolen Thomas (Stockton Heath). 75 marks and Miss May Pollitt (Froghall-lane), 66 marks. At the examinations held in Manchester early this week, under the auspices of the London College of Music, Master Tom Tanner was successful in passing the Intermedille test in organ playing, obtaining 97 marks out of a possible 100, which entitles him to the "with honors " certificate. At the recent local examination (Manchester) of Trinity College, London (the first college to institute local examinations) Miss Violet Brown (10), pupil of Mrs. Hubi-Newcombe, obtained the maximum marks 100, for senior singing.</p>
        <p>4 PRICOTS, 4s. Gd. per doz. ; STRAWBERRIES LIL (Cheshire), 3s. per doz. ; BLACK CURRANTS, ss. per doz. ; PICKLING WALNUTS. Is, 3d. per 120. aim McDERMOTT, `• Cheapest Man Alive." MARKET &amp; BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Licensing _Application.</head>
        <p>Licensing _Application.</p>
        <p>Thoma€TDum- bell, made an application to sell at the White Hart Hotel, Sankey-street, in lieu of Frances Watkins. The Chief Constable stated that the police had no objection as the man had a very high character. He had two references from- schoolmasters in town and one from a clergyman. The application was granted.</p>
        <p>Scholarship Success In the recent competitive examination of candidates from endowed schools for admission into Christ's Hospital, George Irwin Carruthers, son of Mr. G. Carruthers, M.A. of Latchford, was successful int securing a scholarship. He is ten years old and has been at Warrington Grammar School since Easter 1907. Competitors must be nominated by the governing body of the school they bare attended for at least two years previous to the date of the examina- tion and must be between the ages of nine and thirteen.</p>
        <p>TRAVELLING TRUNKS, DRESS BASKETS, SUIT CASES, etc. Special lines at 175., 205., 225.. and 265. GARLAND, Bridge St., Warrington.—Advt. • Trades and Labour Council. The monthly meeting of the Trades and Laboni. Council was held on Tuesday evening. In the absence of the President, *Mr. E. Bullock was voted to the chair. After the routine business was disposed of, the following resolutions we unanimously passed : " That this Council urges upon the Chancellor of the Exchequer that there should be no weakening of the Land Clauses in the Budget, and hopes that the Borongh Member will record his vote against any attempt from whatever side of the H9tise to make those clauses less efficient."—The question of the forthcoming visit to this country of the Czar of Russia was also dealt with, and references were made to the awful atrocities under Russian administration. The following resolution was unanimously passed, and the Secretary instructed to forward it to his Majesty the King, the Prime Minister, Sir Edward Grey, and to the Secretary of the Labour Party :—" That we, the Wai rington Trt.des and Labour Council, do solemnly and emphatically protests against this country being compelled to receive the Czar of Russia, and deeply regret the action of the Government in bringing this disgrace upon us."—A committee was appointed to forward arrangements in readiness for contesting certain Wards in November next, with bona-fine trade union candidates.—The congratulations of the Council were accorded to the secretary (Mr. Edwin Plinston) upon his election as chairman of the School Attendance Sub-committee in place of the late Alder- man Luke Ellison.</p>
        <p>Warrington Charities Committee. Councillor Henry Roberts presided over a meeting of the Warrington Charities. Committee on Thursday evening in the Lion Hotel. There were present, Messrs. H. Hope, (secretary), C. J. Hayes. E. Pickavance, W. Litton, J. Fargher, and A. Pemberton. The Secretary read the statement of accounts which showed that the profits on the draw were £79158.11d. profits from the sports £32. 4s. Id., making a total of £ll2. From that £76 had to be given to the Infirmary and Nursing Association; which left a balance of £36. He proposed that £25 be giVen to the Infirmary and £2O the Nursing Association. leaving £1 in the bank. On the proposition of the Chairman seconded by Mr. Pickavance, the accounts were passed, each agreeing that they were extremely satisfactory. It was decided that in the future of the money they received two-thirds • should go to tue Infirmary and one-third to the Nursing Association.— The Chairman said that as hon treasurer of the Nursing Association he felt indebted to the eoMmittee for the very substantial aid they had given that Institution during the past twelve months.—Votes of- thanks whre accorded to the Misses Ewart, Rylands, and tfoughton, the Chief Constable and the staff, Ur. Milner, and the Whitecross Co, Mrs. Smethurst, and the Warrington Football Club, all of whom rendered valuable services to the success of the sports. --A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mr. Hope, who as the chairman, Mr. A. Pemberton and Mr_ Pickavance, said must have worked very hard for the success of the. various enterprises of the Committee.— Mr. Hope proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, and-said that in Councillor Roberts we had got one of the best gentlemen in town for the helping of local charities.—ln seconding Mr. Pickavance said he must congratulate Mr. Roberts on again entering public life in Warrington. We had lost him for some years. He felt certain that things would go on much better than they had done in the past. LAWN MOWERS.—Several good machines on sale in good condition at prices from 12s. 6d.-51, Bridge at., Wari ington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>The Mersey Boating Fatality. At the Parish Hall, Halton, on. Saturday morning, Mr. A. E. White conducted the inquest on the body of Percy Buckley, the young Warrington man who was drowned in the river Mersey on the 2nd inst.—James Buckley, 22, Collins-street, • Warrington, wiredrawer, said the deceased was his step-brother and lived .at 27, Atherton-street, Bank Quay.. He was an apprentice wiredrawer, serving his time under witness. He was 181 years old.— John Badger, 101, Bramhall-street, Warrington, wire- drawer. said that on the 2nd inst. he met the deceased and two men named Phillips and Mason at Widnes, .near the promenade. They went along the river and got in a dinghy, in which two of them had come from Warrington. They rowed out to their little yacht, which was lying aground near Houghton Marsh Point, having stranded on the previous day. It would then be twenty minutes to ten o'clock. They were rowing to the yacht when Buckley called out " TLe boat's filling. He had hardly said that before the boat sank. Phillips was rowing at the time and the deceased and Masi!' were seated at the stern end of the boat. The boat rose again after their weight had gone out of it. Witness swam to the 'shore. Phillips was swimming round the boat. Mason and Buckley, who could not swim, clung' to the boat. Deceased called to a man named Rustage to bring his boat. Rustage picked up Phillips and Mason but Buckley sank. Deceased was trying to get his overcoat off when he sank. The boat had once had a false keel and cement had been used to fill the hole up,- he thought that this must have come out.—John James Dobson, Moss Side, near Warrim2ton, a labourer in theemploy of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, said he was going home along the Ship Canal Bank on Thursday afternoon when he saw the body of the deceased lying on the river bank at Norton. Witness went to the telephone at the sluices and communicated with the police at Runcorn. They removed the body. The jury returned a verdict of " Accidentally drowned.</p>
        <p>TIIE KING'S VISIT.—FLAGS, STREAMERS, BUN. T- ING, etc., for sale or hire. Large stock. —GARLAND, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Shepherds at Appleton.</head>
        <p>Shepherds at Appleton.</p>
        <p>Tile Loyal Oid-er of Ancient Shepherds (A.U.) Warrington Distlict held its quarterly meeting at-the Thorn Tree Lodge," Thorn Ti ee " Inn, Appleton on Saturday the 10th inst. The District Chief (Bro. J. Gillooley) presided and he was supported by the Deputy Chief (Bro. W. Miles), Past Prov. Chiefs L. Weir, T. Baskerville, jun., T. Miller, P. L. Edgar, W. Eckersley, T. C. Duckworth, W. Hall, James Parr (district treasurer), Peter Edgar (district secretary) and delegates from the various Lodges. The Secre- tary presented his accounts for the quarter and after the Auditors (Bros. Miller and Allen) had given in. their report the same were passed. The meeting had again the pleasure of having to confirm the initiation of a good nuniber of new members which, together with the financial progress that is continually being made, is very satisfactory. Bro. T. Miller of the Morning Star Lodge was re-elected auditor for a further term of six months. Bro. W. Miles (Oliva Branch Lodge) was unanimously elected Prov. Chief Shepherd for the next twelve months. For the position rendered vacant by the appointment of Bro. Miles to the highest honour in the District the meeting had to chose' from two nominations, Bro. Champion (Rose Bud Lodge) and Bro. W. Taylor (late Spring Rose Lodge.) The result of the ballot saw the honour conferred-on Bro. Taylor. Bro. L. Weir (Sons of Mersey Lodge) and Bro. D. Harris (Ironworkers Home Lodge) had the pleasure of being unanimously appointed District Examiners for the next twelve months. The reading of the reports of Bros. P. L. Edgar and F. 0. Leigh, who represented the War- rington District at the A.M.C. held in Worcester during Whit-week,on the proceedings of that Conference, was listened to with great attention. The meeting expressed themselves as satisfied with the contents of the report, and the delegates were accorded a hearty vote of thanks for their services,—On the proposition of Bro. T. Miller, and supported by several delegates, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Bro. J. Gillooley for his conduct and valuable services rendered during the past twelve months as Prov. Chief Shepherd. The installation of the new officers was impressively performed by Bro. J. Gillooley, and the meeting closed in the usual manner. WHOSE HAND? Why, TONGE'S, of course. It contains three King's, a Queen, and a Princess.— THE STUDIO, Tram Terminus, Latchford.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>Don't be satisfLd with saying "I'm pretty well, thank you." Vi-Cocoa will enable you to say " I'm first rate."</p>
        <p>Before starting work in the morning, either with breakfast or before breakfast, every father of a family should fortify himself with a large cupful of Vi-Cocoa.</p>
        <p>It will increase the vital energy so necessary in the strenuous_ life men lead to-day. On his return home in the evening, perhaps tired and worn out, a large steaming cup of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Es *Cocoa</head>
        <p>Es *Cocoa</p>
        <p>should be taken by every wage- earning father whose strength must be maintained. It will quickly cheer and refresh him after the days toil, and produce a renewal of energy and vitality. If you are wise you will give Vi-Cocoa a trial at once. It may be the one thing you have been searching for for years. Your grocer keeps it and you can get a package to-day for sixpence.</p>
