      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>HORSE TRANSACTION.</head>
        <p>A HORSE TRANSACTION.</p>
        <p>At "is Warrington Borough Police Court yesterday-, hcfura file Mayor and other magistrat- lhompson, or Fisher, was charged with o'itaining by alse pretenles a mare, of the value of £24, the property of Alfred Allen Timmins, of Grafton-street, on May 14th. Mr. W. H. J. Jetlins appeared to prosecute, r it was alleged th it on the date in question the prisoner and a mau named Hart went to the prosectitm's house with reference to the purchase of a horsc Prisoner repres,nted himself to ha a large coat rector, and said he had a Lem on which thee were ample facilities foc turning horses °it to grass. On that representit'on Timmins sold thr-3 mare to prison3r for £2l and received a bill of excia,ige. That bill wrir not met, lie went to see prisoner at Liverpool with refer- ence to the bill and prisoner gave him a fresh O.IP, I Ut was not met and Timmins tecame uneasy and took out a w.irrent, the prisoner being arrested at Burnley. It was not true that prisoner was a large farmer or cmitractor and that he had means.—After heariig the evidence the Mayor said that they considered prisoner 13,14 i been swindled out of his h0n..% but there .vas not sufficient evidt ne to stin.d the Rrisoner to trial for fa',e pretences.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED POST OFFICE PORGERY.—SIIOMIAN sExr FOR TRIAL.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Monday, before Mr. IL Forshaw, George Beard, a yorng man, was charoi with having, sometime between the 20th and 25th day of May, somewhere betwcen Writ 2ington and Tyldesley, felunionsl7 stolen one Post Offieo bank I Ink, the property of Thomas Johnson. He was also charged with having on the 24,h day of May, at Newton-le-Willows, forged a certain order, purporting to be an order from Thomas Johnson, for payment of money, to wit, a sum of £24, with intent to defraud.— Mr. F. Elliott, of Manchester, prosecated on behaq of the Post Office anthoritits, ant stated that both John- son and prisoner were employed Sanger's Circus and slept in the same tent when travelling. Johnson sv)s a depositor in the Post Olics Savings Bank, having opened an account at Honey, in Surrey, in 107. The book was kept in a box', which was not securei by a lock, hut was tied by a role. The prisoner admitted ) In.pec•or Carr at Ea-lestowo that he htd ;kin the book. He s Lid ho was very sorry, but be had had a lot of drink a-3,1 hut got the " sack " at Newton. He WM .3. taking his own things out of the box when the book' fell out and he too( it. He received the £25 at St. Helens, and he hal either lost the bank b3ok or it had, been stolen from him at Cardici.—EVict,nee having been given, rrisoner expressed his serrow for wh t hal happened and said it was all through a few words that be and Johnson had. Prisoner said he spent the money on drink and in any way he could best get rid of it.— He was conreltted for trial at the Liverpool</p>
