      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Warrington Borough Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>3IGNDAV.—Before the Ma 3 or (in the chair), Messrs. W. Roherts, H. Thornton, A. J. Jolley, 0. Pilling and W. Furness.</p>
        <p>A SLAUGHTER Housr."—Patrick Collin, of D( thin's-yard, was charged with leing di yak and disorderly in Deakin's-yard on July 2ad.—P.C. Clough et Itsd that in consequence of a complaint made at the Police Station on Vriday night, the of July, witness, in company with P.C. 42 Woods, visited Deak,n's-yard and there found the defendant, who %as lying on the ground outside the house in a veiy drunken condition. Ile was coverei with blood. Witlt.,is aoied defendant to get up g everr 1 times, but he refrsed and used most filthy language. Subsequently he hr 1 t, Le careed inside the house. The floor and walls were covered with blood and the place looked like a slaughter house. IVitness ascertained that defendant had Leen fi2hting with his father, dming which the freirova ht:cl been used.—The Chief Constable stated that the defendant had been eight times previously b3fora the court, the last time being in December last year. Continuing, he said it was a mest regrettable state of affairs. as the defendan•-, %Ito %CPS only a youth, was always drunk. A GAME or " ALL FOCRS."—Bernmi Redd,ican, Stanfield-street ; George Miilar, Watkin-street ; Thomas Ansall, Winwick•ruad, rnd GI-,arles Mackie, Dr llam-lane, were all summoned for playing a certain game of chance namc•d "All fours" for money.--They all pleaded guilty.—Sergeant Stuart ststed that about seven o'clock the night before Walking Day he was eycling across Owen-street to Dallam-lane when he saw the defendants along with another boy playing at "Ail fours" for halfpennies. Witness caught Reddincan, obtained his name and address and the name of the other defendants.—The defendants were ordered to pay Is. each.</p>
        <p>A MEAN BOAST.—Lonis Palmeron engine driver, was clurged with having been drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street on the previous night.—P.C. Cox stated that he was on duty in Butte rruarket-street when he saw the prisoner surrounied liy,a large crowd,making use of very bad language. On witnesi going up to him prisoner asked the way to the clergy house belonging to Buttermarket-street Chum+. He was directed there and on c3mlng out boasted that he had obtained a shilling there and thit he would spend it in drink. Witness took him into custody.—Prisoner WPS ordered to pay ss. and costs or 7 days in default.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Befora Nipssrs. Charles White (chairman J. Rymer Young, W. T. Williams nicl Dr. Nader'.</p>
        <p>FRESH AIR WAN LTD.—William Lee, Scotchman, ot ko fixed abode, wits cbarg( 1 with begging in Hoise.</p>
        <p>market-street, on the 14th July with begging and also vith deserting frrm the Ist Battalion of the Royal Highlanders stationed at Edinburgh in 1903.—The Chief Constable asked for a remand nail Alianday.—Pi isoner (t-aluting): I would ask an hour's fresh air ?—The Chairman: Ile Chief Constable will see ta that.</p>
