      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Juvenile Court. • ROBBING IJ.TILE CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>Juvenile Court. • ROBBING IJ.TILE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>At a special sitting of the Warrington Juvenile Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and other ma',6istratev, William Herbert Webb, aged lA wire- wiridcr, of 26, Catherine-st rest, pleaded guilty to steal- ing by means of a trick, 2s. 6d.. 2s. 3.1. and 2s. 6(1. from Charles Hancock. William Hartley end Sel:na Savage on July 3rd, June 26th and June 19th la sr actively.—The Coief Constable stated that f( r some weeks prst the police had r eived complaints of purses end money being stolen from children. Defendeit hid 1 3en the habit of stopping children cn Saturdays, telling them a tale and getting them to go to Ark nson's r Bridge- street and other places for parcels. In the inaantime he took the money from them, rid when the children returned they found he was missing. On Saturday Detectives Lough end Metsalf were detailed for duty. and at four o'clock the defendant vas seen by one.of the boys, who pointed him out ta Detective Metcalf. The officer brought Webb to the Polka Station and-the boy at first denied the thefts but afterwards admitted them. He had also admitted the theft of 31,1. rid a basket containing tins of milk, red sums of 25., Is. is. 3d.—Defendant's father said he could not understand the boy doing such things, lie was lb good boy laborite and sent to church sod Sunday scho.)l.—Defendant was sentenced to six strokes with he birch rod. A MARKET THIEF WHIPPED.—At the Juvenile Court on Thursday before Messrs. Charles White (in the chair) J. R. Young, W. 'l'. Williams, W. Habeas, and Dr. Naden, Bertie Jones, age 11, the son of John Jones, basket maker, 3, Florence-street, was charged with stealing on the 14th July from the stall of the Peacock Bazaar in the market one tablet of soap and a small looking glass value 2 I.—The boy admitted t tidog the articles.—The Chief Constable said the Hazier Company are prosecuting on the plea that they might at least give him a goad talking to. The lad bad been told to clear off many times.—Harriet White 18, West- street, who is manageress of the still in reply to the chairman said they had a wirp ar•tund the stall ; and also gave evidence as to telling the boy to go away.— The Chairman said if his parsn's would not correct him they must. He would have 3 strokes of the birch roe</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>tam</head>
        <p>tam</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews received a hearty welcome from his old congr6g ition at Latchford Baptist Church when he visited the church on Sund iy to preach the anniversary sermons. There were crowd 3d congreea- tions at ell three services. In the in wiling Mr. Matthews took tor his text C )1. iii. 17 : " IVh itsoever ye do in word or deed do all in the Name of the Lord Jesus." In the afternoon the choir gave a musical service. Mr. S. Albinson presidsd and Mr. Nlatthe vs gave an address, in the course of a hich he compli- mented the organist and choir upon their efficiency. Every teat was occupied in the evening and many were unable to get inside the bullding. The text was tsken from St. John xxi. 17: " Lord, Thou knowest all thing. Thou knowest that I love Thee." Mr.MattheAs expressed the delight it alTorde 1 blm to sea his old cmgragation after an absence of five months. No on', he said, would rejoice more thin he did to heir of a min standing in that pulpit as their pastor who ha I won their love and esteem and bad mad-ibis personality felt in the Church and neighbourhood. Several anthems were rendered by the choir, the solo in the evening being feelingly sung by Miss V,luli Jon 39. Mr. re'.er Harrison preside 1 at the orea,i. Toe clllectro is realalized £lB, which is a re33r 1 for the anniversary.</p>
        <p>r /8, it .• coo), t 6-)</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptist Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Lady Preacher at Cairo-street Chapel.</head>
        <p>Lady Preacher at Cairo-street Chapel.</p>
        <p>The novelty of hearing and seeing a lady preacher drew a large congregation to Cairo-treat Church on Sunday wlien the Rev. Mary A. S afford occtipiel the Pulpit in the morning and eveninl. This lady comes from America and is a fully qialifi-d minister, repro senting the first Unitarian Society, Dec Moins, Tow I. From her appearance she seems to be on whit is termed the wrong side of -10 years of age. She pouiesses an intellectual aad sympathetic face. Her sermon in the evenin; wrs characterised by gra 'it e anestness and the conducting of the sarvice bore sigas of warmth. Her subject "Tile secret of moral earnestness, or obedience t 3 the Heavenly vis:on," was admirably salted to her, and the eloquent way in which she dealt with it w,.s evidence of consider- able breadih of view. Referring to the apoEtle Paul, she said that he took high rank in the history of the world. At the centres of thought and feelin; be made kis power felt in words and deeds Ont hal deeply tnovtd the mind and heart through all the centuries that had gone since he was here upon earth. Although his name sign'fled little, in reality ho was one of the greatest chara.lers that had ever lived upon the face of the earth. Ha did his work so well that to-day in no less thin on) hundred different languages did his teaching find a place. What was the secret of his power? "Wherefore King Agrippa I was not dis- oheclient unto the Heavenly v sion." In those word we foetid the key which unlocked that great soul In those words wa had the s 3 oret of persist- ent morel earnestness. They were words which uttered great deep and strong convictions, which were always a sourc3 of power. It matte-ed little how, when and where great truthe daweed upon the soul, but it mAtered more than tongue could tell whether those truths were loved and h •noured by their ice. W i all° troth in thought wai much, n' life. was far nobler. Paul's vision bee tme a mighty p moor in the world, beoausa he was 05(3-Bent to it, and because he wrought it into the very tissues of his be,ng. If their faith Jacked power in the world it was due to their lack of loyalty to it, rid not to their glorious faith itself. In everything they siw the power of .the Divine. To them te, Eternal F)wer NVJg manifested not only in the glory of the sunset skies and starry Heavens ; not only in the great and trite life of degas, but in every noble life. They salt min. not fallen from a high estate, but climbing and ever climbing. 11;nn was also saved from sin an I the effects of sin by patient, earnest efforts to build up noble cha.acter. Ins Univeise was luminous with Deity to them Thousends were still in bondage to crual crea,ts, to selfish•icss, to things that dragged down and deleted the siul. Were they eager to share with them the good they teid received, and to help them realise how heauti fel it.w..ts to be alive whea life was tarengthened and up-b0...e be a nobler faith ;n God and hum an destiny? Were they alive to the fact thAt es eternal vigileisce was the plies of liberty, se torstant activity of soul was the price of spirituil growth. The loftiest troths failed to (micron the 5)111 unless they were brought into the soul through' action. Concluling, the speaker slid opinions were nct much without conviction No grc A enterprise was ever cal-a:led forward by mere opitron-. but by stt o ig conviction which armed the soul from on high. Before the soul that had contictiors, obstacles fe" and difficulties vanished away. a</p>
      </div>
