        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>SEEDSIVIEN AND THE SALE OF POISONS.</p>
        <p>There were several long end lively disctrsions at the mon: hly rucAing of the Warrington Town Council which wes held hi the Cot roil nrober at the Tot-1 flail on Tuesday evening. Ihe Mayor presided and there wero also present :—Aldermen A. Bennett, W. Bolter T. Burton, James Ev i end D. Tinnion ; Councillors 'l'. H. Ashcroft, T. P dm J. Brosdlurst, T. Chantler, J. D:ckenson, J. Hllli well, J. Hawthorn, Dr. Jos?ph, F. T. Lashford, J. T. Locker, J. Lord, /' Roberts, V. B. Ibberts, W. Rogers, J. Sankey, II Singleton, J. Skelland. J. Fulton-Smith, 0. Toole, J. Villkinton and W. J. Wilson; Mrrcrs J. I IN hittle (ToIN a Clerk), A. T. Hallaway (Depnty Town Clerk), J. Fairhurst (Borough Treasurer), T. Lonadin (Borough Surveyor),j. De: q (Water Eng'nelr), F. V. L. Matthews (Electrical Engineer), J. M. Murriy (Dirt ;tor of Educition), F. G. Ruddocle (Borough analyst) and Dr. J. C. 'Ebben (Niedicel Officer). 'HIE LATE ADERMAN FILISON.</p>
        <p>A letter -1; -re-171-frOm Mr. D. Eilisor thanking the Council, on behalf (.f his mother. L. rid family, for the kind resolution passed by the Council %%ith reference to his lste fatlu r. They would ever be grateful for the great tribute paid to his mmory. THE LATE COUNCILLOR NTSLD.</p>
        <p>Tne MAYOR said :—I have again the painful duty of formally reporting to the Council the death of another member of the Council. Mr. Nield died on the 27th June last, after a long and, I am sorry to thiuk a plin- ful illness. Mr. Nield joined the Council November, 1906, and was an exceedingly useful member. He served on tie Finene3 end Estate. the 'Museum' the Cemetery, the El( :tricity and Tramways, and the Small Holdings Committees. When be was in good be?lth be attended ' the meetings and discharged his duties most ec-iduously, end he brought to bear upon the work he had in hand keen intelliocace and ability, which the Council could ill spare. I beg to move : "That this Council receive: with deep regret the announcement of the de -.th of Mr. Job, James Nield, a Councillor of the Boroukh, and desires to ;record the great loss which the Borough has sushined by his death, and that the Town Clerk 1:3 requeo2cl to convey to the widow and family of the late Mr. Nield the deep and sincere syrni athv of the Council with them in their great bereavement.' Alderman BENNETT, in s conding, said the late Conncillor Nield was one of his oldest end most valued :rsorsl friends. He had known him intimately for a grafter of a century, and he did not think he hai ever met a man who was more sincerely anxious to use the abilities God had given him is the best in•erests of his fellow-men, and of his townspeople in pa zacular. He should never forget the pathos of his attention to his duties during the last few months of his public life, although he knew that the shadow of a (-sterility Was hanging over him. He stud( to his work to the last and In had no other motive but the good of his fellows. En every walk of municipal and public life he sat. an example which his follow-citizens might do well to follow.</p>
        <p>CJULic;liOr PL.-TON 3MITT, Aldermrl T aid, Aldera—tn EvAss hupported the motion. EDUCATION APPOINTIV•q,NTS. _ _ •</p>
        <p>The Coancil m td.-4 the followink appointment; cotneetion with the Educltion Connuittc 3 : Cot- lei llor Locker and Counillor 11. RobelLs repter3ntative members of the Education C munittee in Pie places of V-e late Alderman Ellison and the late Cot' icillor Nield.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dickenson as a representative manager of fir ttl side Nstionst &amp;boat '1 the pl9,ce of Miss A'Ary Williams, resignel.</p>
        <p>C.ffincillor Pemberton as repres:antative trifinager of S. James' School in the place of the late Alderan.l El!Non.</p>
        <p>TB e: ORDER OF BUSINESS. "SCANDALOUS WASTE OF TIME."</p>
        <p>Alderman TIN-UON moved: " Thst Sr-mdin" g Order N. 3, which reads as follows, be resesded Notices of. motion in the order in which they have been received by the TOA.a. Cleec gill 1 considered and disposed of immediately before the consideration of the pro, ,ed'a:ts of the several committf -1 of the Comcil," and that the following standing order be substituted therefor :—`Notices of motion in the order in which they have b: 2n received by the Touts Clerk *shall be considered end disposed of immediately after the con. sideration of the proceedings of the several committaes of the Council ' " He r lid a Standing Order similar to what he wee proposing was in force for 27 ycars—from 1879 to 1906." The present Standing Order was br:augitt into force in 1906. His principal rea-on for proposing an alteration WeS that he thought the conduet of the business of the Council should be absolutely in the hr ids of the Council., Recent exprience bad Otown that tome or thrc3 menfets emit!, if they wished, take tie a large proportion of the evening with reetion^. red the teealar businces had to be dealt with ins vet), hurried nvntter. The consideration of the proceedings of the various committt is, which to his mind, was the most importret well: of the Council should have fizet claim upon the time abilities and endurance of the members of the Council Many important nutters had been simply rusted through the Council and he thought it was desirable to revert to the old order of things. Want induced him to bring the watt sr to a bead were the unfortunate proceedings which took place a short time ago, vah-ch were in his opinior, the partial cm eof the death of csee of th 4 members of the CJuncil—the late Aldertr in Ellison. On that occfsion Alderman Ellison complained to him (Alderrran Tinnim) of the very great distress of body red mind which was caused lay the unseemly weeeed- logs occasioned by that discussion. He ( In Tirnion said, " Wily don't yo t go Ihome Lut he replied that he wanted to attend to the busin, ss of the committee with which Its Was connecte I. Oa the same night A Ide, man John Evans else said he could n-it stand it, and. Alderman Pierpoint asked him (Aldermin Tinnion) to propose the minutes of the Gas Committee, beetle° he also could not stand the strain. The way in which the business 11.acl been conducted of late bad been uninninesslike and ungentlemanly in,some de ree—in fact he emit(' use stronger language wffich world more fittingly describe it. Alderman BENNETT, in secmiing, raid he w)4 a reluctant convert to Alderman Tinnion's proposition owing to the scandalous way in which the privilege of introducing motions tefore the ordinary procedure had been abused. The proceedings had not reflected credit- ably on the Council or the town. Councillor LORD said he was not convet• yl by. Alderman Tinnion's eloquence. He was certainly or the opinion that the best procedure was to dispase of the resolutions ft -at. Alderman JAMFS EVANS said that recently they had been discussing mations for two hours end a half, which were pre .31;icelly new bus:ness,*bafore the committee procec lid' g,s the primer', object of the meeting had been dispose! oa. Tt would be far better to letroCluce the new Lu.iless at the end. Councillor W. 11. Itousal s said it would ts a roisLake to support the resolutior If a member hal a complaint or question that he wanted to bring before the Cornell and was not allowed to do it until the end of the b isi- ness, the Council would disperss and there woeld he no opportunity to discuss motinr^ If the conduct of' the Council hto not been preperly 'nage 1 and members had beta allowed to speak too long, that %%AS a matter that rconired more str.ngent toles. Councillor Fe uros•Satrea co-tended thst by burrs'. jug the principal business of the Council they bad probably approved actions of committees which in :Imer momeni-,1 they would not have done. They might serionsly commit themselves some time if that state of affairs continued. lie would like a time limit irnpoeed for speeches. (Flesr,heay.) It wou'd result in a saying of time and be tan education, to some of the spetakera in coadensin3 their th nights id better I.angrlgo. Alderman BOLTON slid he was in favour of molons being taken fist if there could be a time limit for them. If the time limit was relate:l the discussion could by adjourned to the end of 'Lae Liiqine:s. l's answer to Councillor Tole, the Tows CLERK sail in almost. every borough the motions were taken at the end of the proceedings. For the motion there veted : Toe %Ever, A idei.nen Bennett. Tim:lion and James Evans, and Councillors Fulton-Smith, Locker, Sankey, Toole EL RoLerts and Hawa.hotat—l I.</p>
        <p>Agaiust : Councillors Bat ;on, Skell—id, %shutoff, Lord, Dickenson, I rshford, Singleton and W. B. Roberts—S.</p>
        <p>The resolution was therefore car. :ea. SEEDSMEN AND THE SALE OF POISONS. The Health C immittee again considetted the ques- tion of the granting of licerses under the Poisons and Pharmacy Act, 1008, nn', after hearing Mr W. B. Forshaw, who atten led before the comm:ttee on behalf of the applicants, Messrs. G. Starkey and T. Pierpoint and Company, it was resolved that licerses ba not granted. - Councillor H. ROBERTS asked the reason why the licenses were not geJltee, reserb:ng his right to move Al amendment. _ _ _</p>
        <p>'After a few moments had elapsed Alder nln BouroN said be would move an amendmept that the minute be teferrrul back. _ .</p>
        <p>_ Councillor ROBERTS : I have reserve the right to do that. and I insist upon that right Alderman Ev.v.cs said the committee hrd ta ice con- sidered the matter. They lid hclrd Mr. Forsbaw, Mr. Starkey and Mr. Piereoint personally, and given every corsideration to wuat they -lid. They were however, aoided by the yr.,..1 aspect of tbo question and they were of the op.nion that the neeis of the public were already met by tbe local chemists. If 'lose requirements were not met by the Ici-il chemists, then the Act empowered the Health althority to grant licenses to such gentlemen as bad applied if they deemed it a ise to do so.</p>
        <p>Councillor H. RouEwrs moved that the minute aith rge ence to the refuEal to criot licenses to Messrs Starkey and Pierpoint be referred back. Alderman TINNION : Has not (-)ancillor Roberts alfeady made a speech? The MAYOR: He reserved his r:gtit to move an amendment.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNION : He cannot ruake two speeches. Councillor ROBERTS: If Alderman 'Bunion had paid atteuticn to what I said—</p>
        <p>Alderman T,.:NION I paid attention to it. You know very well you are out of order. Councillor ROBERTS: I know very well lam not out of order. I asked the Mayor's permission to put a quest:on, and I said it would depend upon the nature of the answer whether I moved an amendment or not. I know something about order and procedure as well as Aldermr-t Tinnion. Proceeding to move the.amen&amp; ruent, Councillor ,Roberts mid the question as to whether the requirements of the district were alreidy supplied by chemists or not was a epiestion of fact in regard to which it was somewhat difficult to form an accurate opinion, but he said as a matter of common- sense that when farAiers and owners of gardens and others who were compelled by the nature of the trade to go to seedsmen and horticultural agents for their general requirements, could not g 4 to the same people to L 3 supplied w;th such things as were necessary for weed-killing, sheep dip, etc., because they happened to be po'sons, it seemed to him to be an unfair attempt on the part of a very powerful shop-keeping mnnopoly to arrogate to itself a business which it never previously possessed, aryl which did not properly belong to it. - Councillor W. B. ROBERTS seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON, in supporting, contended that a ese fir.ns had a vested interest in the business they had built Lip, snd it %%as not right that they should lose it at the whim of any committee. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said there wrs a great danger in selling poisonous articles indiszrimina.ely. Before the first Poisons Act was passed the number of cases of criminal and negligent poisoning isas enormous. After the passing of that Act they dropped something like GO per c(nt. in twelve months, ant in two or three years they practically disappeared. Councillor Roberts spoke rsther offensively at-olt a chemist's shop-keeping monopoly. Chemists %%ere more than shopkeepers.' Councillor H. ROBERTS : I am not referring to analytical chemists. lam referring to chemists who sell over the counter.</p>
        <p>Counciller Dr. Josnrit raid pharmaceutical chemists had to pass very severe examinations, aucl acre to some extent professional men. Ile took it that a man aho knew all about the properees of visons and their disastrous results would feel the responsibility of sell- ing a packet of arsenic tint would poison almost a allele toad. The object of the new Act was to give the public facilities for obtaining what they required in country places %here there were no chemists. In Warrington where they had seven Gr eight chemist.,anel he did not see the necestity for further licenses. He admitted that if any seed people were allowed the privilege of selling these articles, the two gentlemen mentioned would be suitable, for they showed great intelligence. A person who intended to mike bad use of the poTsons would bave greater awe in going into a chemist's shop, because he might be ar-kedaakward questions. Alderman TINNION said Councillor Roberts had statei that this was a care of a shopkeeping monopoly trying to get something it never previously possessed. The fact vas that the seedsmen were trying to get some- thing they never previously possessed. Councillor HENRY RonEirrs : That, is not correc+ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman Ti \ SION : I object to being inter. uptecl. As a matter of facts the gentlemen repretranting these two seedsmen in Varrington dare not admit they previo,tsly sold the poisons containing nicotine and arsenic. If they did they knew they %sere doing an 1110,01 act. _ _</p>
        <p>COuncillor RODE#TS : No they were not, so long as the poisons were nude up in parcels and sold under labels.</p>
        <p>Alderman TINNioN : I appeal to the Town Clerk. ff they wele selling poisons containing n'cotine or ars.nic were they not acting contrary to the law? The TOWN CLERK : Cutainly. Alderman TINNION, continuing, said the - Act was passel for the convenience of country districts in which there 'awe no qualified chemists. The Cammittee were not granting to chemitts any monopoly they did not legally possess before and they were not tAlig from the seedsman any trade they legally hid before. Councillor lifto.tnfluusT, as a member of the Health Committee supported the amendment. He consid.!red a harthhip was beirg inflicted ur on the seedsmen. Councillor SINGLETON also supported and said the action of the Committee would take trade from the secdsmen and place it in one or two people's hands. Councillor Dr. JOSEI'II : Why. Alderman HOLTON ; Pure cussedness. (Laitgl.ter.) Alderman dames EVANS strongly denied that any persotal feelings had entered into the Health Com- mittee's action or that there was a desi-e to boyoot the seedsman.</p>
        <p>C.uncillor WILKINSON supported the amendment and contended that the sale r.f weed 1;11ers would be as safe in the h3nds of Messrs. Pierpoint and Starkey as of 3ny chemist in ‘Varrington.</p>
        <p>oirncillor LORD thought that in view of the fact that the seedstnen were under the same regolat ions as chemists in regard to the supply of poisons, there was no reason why they should not have licenses. fhl TOWN CLERK ald that until the ne v Act was passed, nurserymen and seedsmen had na right to sell poisons. As a matter of fact some of them did so, I:mt the Ph irmwentical Society had never thought it worth while t o take proceedings, except in one ea e. The pr- sent Act was passed to enable-seedsmen ad nurser 3 men to sell the poisons, out it pr rvidad that the local authority befo.e granting liner -e3, shauld take into consideration whether the reaxonal..le re Trirements of the public were already satisfied. • The voting was as follow _ . .</p>
        <p>For the Ainenllnent : Aldermih I3olton, Connell!ors Wilhinson. Henry Roherts, Ashcroft, Wilson, Caantler, Lord, Toole, Dicken on. Halliwell, Hawthorn, Lash- ford, Broadirurst, Singleton, W. B. Roberts-15. Against; Aldermen linnion and James, Evans, CJuncillors D. Joseph, Sp-ikey, Skelland and Birton-3. The amendme_rt was therefore carried. BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee resolved "that the Bowling green sub-committee be dissolved." C nine; I lor BROADHURST as`ced whether the corn mitt ee were dissolved heaths° it hal fln6hed its work. find when some progress was to be made in the provision of ad lit ions! greens? Alderman BOLTON sad the Town Hall, Parks and B ttks Committee %ere now tal,ing the matter in hand. They had h-ti plans before tnem. With regard to the suggested green in Winw;ck-roa I, they did not I now whether the land would be placed at their disposal, as it was bought for school purposes. SLOT METER USERS AND TfIE PRICE OF GAS. MORE COMPL VINE'S ABOUT QUALII f.</p>
        <p>CutIOCMOr BROADHURST asked :-:-:</p>
        <p>1. What nmnber of slot pet.r consumers in the wturle of the Wards in the town have participated in the reduction of the price of gas as missed by resolu- tion of the Council on February 25th? 2. By what date at the present rate c.f progress will the whole of the slot meter consumers have receivt I the benefits,a the reduction?</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON replied (I) None (a). The Gas Committee bave decided to give a rebate to sot con- sumers bat it is not con-idered ndOsable by the secretirial staff and the matter will teceive Itirther consideration by the G s Committee on Friday nexr If the meter Vl heels hive to b 3 changed. it w;ll take three or four months to complete Um whole of the town.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST : I contend thnt 's no onsw or to the questions. At a meeting of the Gas Cunimitt the followiag resolution of the Gene...l Purposes Committee was read : Resolrel. tl-at, the attention of the Gas Cormr'ttee be called to the low illuminating power of the gas and the engineer reported as follows: " The low returns of illrminating pawer at Bpnk House are quite unexplairmble. The tests made by the foreman at Mersey-street and Longford on Febreary 22nd, and March 22nd and 23rd, average about 18 candles. Afor Mr. Raddock reported the test on March 22nd we found the Etandard lamp was not accurately adjusted Isnd gave results too low. If thequality of the gas I 'td been only 14 candics it would have been noticed by the ceniuniell,who did ni.t complain in I'elaiamy or March." Counci.tor lla.witHoTt.N said he considered this a rather lame excuse as to the qvality of thb gas. There had been complaints from time to time, and some of the residents -a the Wait( • cr, ss IVard to!d him they were unable to cook their dinner on Sunday owing to the low pressure. Councillor Loan I thlroc the pub"c ere getting tied of coinplaining. Aldernam Tr,stoN said they never had a 'complaint sent to the Gas ' Committee that was not carefully examined. They could not, however, take notice of general comoiainte. If any memt rr or ratepayer would make a definite con, plaint and tay that in a cert in house at a certain time the gas was not as it should be, a man would be sent to examine the fittings. Generally speaking, it was found that it was not the quality of the gas but the fittings which were at fault. The minutes were npproved. FIRE BRIGADES' DENIONSTR&amp;TION..</p>
        <p>-1.-t-iinAD.lT—UltsT—,-ieferring to the minutes of the Fire Engine Committee, mum ted on the annul° demonstr ttion of the Lancashire Fire Brigades Friendly Society held at Accrington in June. He thought the time hid 'come when it v.as advisable for the Council to coligider whether they should invite the Society to visit Wart ington. Councillor ASIICRCFT : It would take £3OO to Lring them to Warringt,)n. The minutes were passe,d. A DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>The Water Committee having invited to inspect the now vater Ower at Clectborpe4, it was resolve(' tbat the Committee rnslect the same on lnursday, tbe 2lth inst. The Committee visited Cleethorpt-s sad met M-. Hewing, of the Great Grimsby Wat3rworks Co. end made an inspection of the pumping station and new water tower. Councillor CIIANIr Ert askei if it was usual for the whole of a Committee to go on a deputation without the sanction of the Council ? Alderman BOLTON slid the whole of the Committee did not go, but they were all invited because it was inarnt ;ant that they should know the details of the cot, struction of the new tower at Winwick. WARRTNGTON_BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting' OFtTli-e- Ge-nerel Purposes Committee, the proceedings of the Bridges Sub-Committee were submitted rid a rolonged discussion then took place dui ing which the members of the Committee left Vie meeting and r sthere was not aquorum present,the meet ing adjourned. Alderman BOLTON said he thought he moved a resolu- tion that the Bridges Sub-Committee be done cot wi.h</p>
        <p>Tel. No. 123.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>that the whole of the members of theGeneralPurnoses Committee should constitute the Bridges Committee. He understood thit was carried.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : There was not a: quorum. Councillor WILKINSON It is Letter as it is. We can bring it up again. The minutes were passed. _ INCREASES OF SALARIES.</p>
        <p>The Council approved the following increase of salaries :</p>
        <p>Mr. G. A. Dunlop, assistant curator at the Museum, from £llO to RP-0 per annum. Mr. H. P..Lckson, chief essistant engineer in the electricity department, from £l5O to £165.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>PATIENTS FROM OUTSIDE DISTRICTS.</head>
        <p>PATIENTS FROM OUTSIDE DISTRICTS.</p>
        <p>The Omit man of the Health Committee, the Deputy Chairman, Alden' an Tinnior,,, and Councillo.a Joseph and Wi.kinson have been appointed a sub-committee to rliEcuss with the Newton Commissioners the question of the reception of patients from-their area. It has 81s3 been decided that the commits e meet the Corn- missioners of the New ton-in-Mnkerfield Urban District Council, vs;th references to the cbarges for treatment of patients at the hospital. _ _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Alderman JAMES Ev.,ixs said the sub-committee had met the Newton Commissioners, and the matter wig now in a state of trarsition. He would I e able to renoit later t) *he Coricl as to the result of their de.iberati or s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>A keenly contested match was fired on the Club' s range last Saturday between a team of ten members of the Club Ad a team of ten Felected from the War- rington Division of the Lancashire County Branch of the National Service Seague. The conditions were as follows :—Each team to consist of ten merobers, the best eight scores on each side to count; any rifle, any sights, bar telescopic ; seven shots at each of two targets-6in. \ and 3in. bull's eye. Highest possible score at eaohNtarget 35 per man, or an aggregate of 70 per man for the two target,. The match resulted in a victory for the Appleton GO, Nsho made a total of 4P4 against 453 by the National Service League, the former thus winning by a majority of 31. The weather was fine and the interest was well sustained throughout the match, which was fhoroughlyenjoyed both by the competitors and a numerous body of spectatois. among which were many members of the League. Mr. F. C. Bennion, of the Management Com- mittee of the Appleton Rifle Club, kindly offered a prize of a silver sholting spoon for the Club member making the highest score in the match. The honour won by Mr. I!. D.`Gardiner. The following scores were made :</p>
      </div>
