      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>'.'3</p>
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>I was born on the 19th August, 1839, so that I am just on the point of completing my "three score years and ten." Not a great age, you will say, and yet it has been suffi- cient to enable me to see many changes in and around the "good old town" of War- rington, the town in which I have lived all my life, and in which my father and grand- father lived for many years. No doubt the majority of the changes are for the better, but when one remembers the picturesque setting of the town 60 years ago, and com- pares it with its condition to-day, one cannot help but regret that the Beautiful Warring- ton Society and the Garden City Movement were not formulated many years ago to have helped to retain for the town something of its then rural aspect. I was born in St. Austin's-lane, and well remember the gardens which extended, almost without interruption, from there to to Slutcher's-lane (now Arpley-strept.) . A pleasant little babbling brook used to run from Slutcher's-lane along what is now Wilson-Patten-street. Just at the back of the Warrington Slate Company's present offices there was a small foot bridge (two or ,three planks nailed together) crossing the brook, this taking the place of the present bottom end of Bold-street. I remember that a few yards away from this spot there was a garden in which cherry trees grew. The branches of the trees used to'hang well over the fence, and it was an extremely difficult matter for a boy to pass those trees without breaking the commandment. Arpley Station was not in existence at that time, Arpley fields being practically a continuation of ' Spring Gardens, which latter (with fields) occupied the space right. from Bold-street across to L. and N.-W. Railway (where Parker-street now ,is.) There were only a few houses between these two points. One I remember (afterwards Mr.Grimes' house) was ; on the spot now occupied by Mr. Maginn's stables at the corner of Sankey-street and Arpley-street, and was the only house in Arpley-street (or Slutcher's-lane as it was then). My father was a currier, and worked as foreman for Mr. Furnival, who had a large curriers shop in Friars-gate. Mr. Furnival's dwelling house adjoined the works, the house in which he lived being afterwards used as the Warrington Museum before removal to the present Museum at the bottom of Bold-street. The first school I went to (if I may term it a school) was in the kitchen of a house occupied by a Miss or Mrs. Sarah Davis in River-road, behind Wildenspool Causeway. The house is still standing. There were three forms, without backs, , arranged round the walls, with accommoda- tion for 16 to 18 scholars. I cannot remember that anything was attempted to be taught us, not even the alphabet, the idea being, I suppose, to keg') us out of mischief for a short time each day. The mistress used to be continually engaged in knitting or sewing. or carrying on her household duties, during school hours. I believe we paid Id. per week as school fee. I afterw.ards went to St. James' Infants school (the ground floor of the building at present occupied by the Emmanuel Institute,Knutsford -road.) Miss Paul was the head and only teacher in the Infants school. The present educational code, which prescribes a limited number of scholars for each teacher, had not then been dreamed of. The National schools in Church- street was my next move. The boys from the Workhouse used to receive their educa- tion at the National Schools. I remember they used to walk in procession from the Work- house to the schools every morning and after- noon. They were not isolated, but received the sameeducation from the same teachers as the other scholars, and were mixed up in the various classes according to age and ability. I well remember that as the work- house boys grew to be what was then con- sidered old enough for work, they were drafted off mostly to the collieries as pit boys. At least 'I judge this to be the case, ,for I have a distinct recollection of the boys taunting each other with "It'll soon be thy turn fo'th' pits." The old workhouse adjoined the "Bull's Head" public-house in Church- street and extended as far as Mr. Gandy's grocer's shop. The site now occupied by the shop was the gateway to the back of the Workhouse and the Workhouse Infirmary. Mr. Warburton, the farmer who had the farm occupied now by Mr. Sankey, had a right of way through the gates to his hay- lofts, which abutted on to the workhouse yard. Some years after this the new work- house in Guardian-street was built. Mr. or Messrs. Helsby (for there were two brothers) were the builders. Their workshop was Off Bank-street, where the large gasometers belonging to the Corporation now stand. I recollect a very large balloon being sent up from there. There were at that time very few, if any, workmen's houses in Warring- ton supplied with gas. In our house we used nothing but tallow canales. Crosfield's works were formerly a candle works. Young students who now have the advantage of the incandescent and , electric light, and the advantage of a plentiful supply of good cheap literature, will be able to undefstand something of the difficulties of those who endeavoured to study during the long winter evenings, with the fitful light of a candle, and only a sparse supply of literature. I seem to hear my mother say (as she often did, as we all sat round the kitchen trying to get the benefit of the light as much as possible) " Prithee lad snuff th' candle." The old fashioned 'candle snuffers were always handy for the purpose. I remember when the fire had to be kindled with the aid of the flint, steel and tinder box, and when matches were introduced. _The latter were pieces of _ _</p>
        <p>wood abont 5 inches long, tapered- at each end, and with brimstone on each end, so that they could be used on two occasions. While I was at the National Schools I remember wo were marched to au unfinished building</p>
        <p>Of course, I remember when the River Mersey had lots of fish in it. As boys we used to go behind the factory in Knutsford- road with a little basket tied to a stick to catch fish. The old fishermen used to come along in their boats, just at dusk, to fix what they called their " junkets " (long tapered baskets) which they would leave all night, and take up with, the fish in them, the following morning. Many a time have we hidden while the men were fixing the " junkets" (they always put them near the side) and after they had gone away we have pulled them out and put them in another place, causing no end of trouble to the poor fellows. I remember some of the old fisher- men's names, i.e., T. Baxter, Ted Green, Jim Wild, and Laurence Spencer. The favourite pastime of the lads was catching shrimps up by' the weir. I remember when the great slaughter of fish took place; thousands of fish were seen lying dead on the water, poisoned by the dye and chemicals from the works up towards Manchester. Packets used to run to Manchester every day from near , Warrington Bridge. The boats were drawn by horses. There was also a steamer called "Jack Sharp," the paddles of which were at the back. The boats started from Warrington at 2 p.m. and arrived at Manchester at about 7 p.m. There was an " express " service on the canal from London Bridge-to Manchester. The boat was called the " Swift." It was drawn by horses,which were made to trOt the whole of the distance. Of course, there were relays of horses at various points. I went by the " Swift "to Manchester on one occasion, and we did it in (what was looked upon as) the remarkably short time of three hours.</p>
        <p>I remember the Bridge Foundry, the front entrance of which was in Mersey-street, opposite the public-house "Moulders Arms," and another entrance where Bishop's Wharf is now. I saw a small iron boat launched there. I believe it Was a pleasure yacht for the Greenall 'family. It used to .be accom- modated in the Old Quay caual. Cannon balls were cast in hundreds during my boy- hood days at the Bridge Foundry, and also a few cannons.</p>
        <p>The building of the Iron Bridge over the Mersek near Bridge Foot is still fresh in my memory. The temporary railway station (until the bridge was completed) was at the top of Old-road, Wilderspool-road, where the railway crossings are now. There was also a station close to Crosfields works at Bank Quay, called " Whitecross " station. Where the Whitecross works now stands was a brickfield belonging to the late Alderman Holmes. That was before Milner-street wa carried through to Froghall-lane. Bank Quay railway station used to be near the preMises now' used as the Railwaymen's Institute, at the bottom of Bank Quay bridge. This was also the Old Patten Arms Hotel. The station w'as a very primitive affair. I remember a very prominent notice hanging up to the effect that "no trains start from this station within 15 minutes of each other." Whilst on the station on one occasion I heard a woman arguing with the booking clerk. She wanted a ticket to " Yorkshire " and when he asked her what part of Yorkshire, she called him a "crazy beggar" and had to appeal to one of her friends before she knew to what part of Yorkshire she wished to travel. There were no thoughts of week end excursions in those days for the working classes, and a week at the seaside was never thought to be within the bounds of possibility for a working than and his family. I well remember the Wilson Patten family living at Bank Hall. and Mr. Allcard living in Sankey-street, now the Borough Rate Offices. Mr. Allcard was the opponent of the late Sir Gilbert Greenall for Parliamentary honours, but I was not a voter at that olec-</p>
