      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>MR. ALFRED TILLING.</head>
        <p>MR. ALFRED TILLING.</p>
        <p>(which I now learn was the old public baths) in Church-street, almost opposite the end of Howley-lane, and were there supplied with a bowl of soup each for dinner. I have endeavoured to find out what the occasion was, and believe it was at a time of very bad trade. The Corporation or some other body evidently took in hand the feeding of the school children. When I was about 5 years old my father removed to a tobacconist and stationers shop in Bridge-street opposite the Higher Seven Stars, but we did not remain there very long. We afterwards went to live in Latchford. I finished m 37 education at Newton-street schools, after- wards called" Peoples College," which stood on the spot now occupied by Central Station. When I was about 9 years of age I had many little duties to perform. I had to take breakfasts and dinners every day to work- men in districts so far apart as Bank Quay (to the old ship-building yard where the ill-fated" Tayleur " and other vessels were built), Orford, Scotland-road and Latchford. For this I received a few coppers per week. Two days each week I turned one of the old- fashioned mangling machines for an old woman in School-street,Latchford, for which I received 3d. .per week. When taking breakfasts in a morning I used to see the old coach start for Liverpool from the Rose and Crown Inn, Bridge-street. Also at night we used to watch the' stage coach coming into town from Buxton and that district.</p>
        <p>After leaving school I was employed at Artingstalls, in the brush department. The premises then were in adi old cotton faetory in Water-street. Hair weaving and hair spinning (or curling) for sofa and chair seating and covers, was also carried on there. My wages were 2s. per week—hours 6 in the morning to 6or 7 o'clock at night. Shortly afterwards I went as apprentice to the joinery trade, with .Mr. William Gibson, St. James- street, Latchford. Mr. Gibson afterwards removed his works to Church-street. where the old Workhouse stood. I worked for him for about 12 years. I helped in the erection of Mr. George Crosfield's residence, Walton Lea. Some time afterwards the late Mr. John Crosfield sent for pie and offered me a position as joiner at the Soap Works, which I accepted, alid remained with the firm for about 27 years, until my health failed me about 9 years ago. Mr. Joseph Hall was the only joiner, there at the time besides myself. He is still working for the firm. I frequently had to go to Walton Lea to do repairs and alterations. On one occasion it was necessary to remove a' piece of skirting board, and on doing so I found my name written on it. I had evidently scribbled my name on the back of the board when fixing it over 30 years previously. For many years I was a member of 'the St. James' Sunday School. On various occasions, when the scholars were marched to the church, I remember an old man who used to walk along the aisle in the church keeping the boys in order. He had a long pole with a knob at the end, and if he observed any boys talking he bobbed them with the pole. He was called by us boys "Bobber." Later on, in my young manhood days, the Rev. J,ames Wright, then the Incumbent of thechurch, organized a night school. I was appointed a teacher at the school, and although I felt I was far from being qualified for the post (having only a bare knowledge of the rudiments of elemen- tary education) I willingly did my best, night after night, to teach those who had had no schooling at all. The night school was entirely free, and was firly well patronised. _ _ _</p>
        <p>tion. The famous '6B election was sthe first in which I had the honour of voting. I had voted several times previously in Municipal elections. The present generation can have no idea as to the upset state oc the town during an election 40 to 45 years ago. The excitement and rowdyism commenced on Nomination Day. A platform was erected adjoining Walker's Brewery wall, where the market now is, and there the candidates were nominated. A strong wooden fence was fixed right from the centre of the' plat- form, the supporters of one candidate usually being on one side of the fence, and the adherents of the other candidate on the other side. It was known, of course, how every man voted on the polling day. and the result of the polling,. Vas posted up outside the ' booth every polling The booth was closed at four o'clock in the afternoon. Work was almost suspended for some days previous to the '6B election, or if the men: were at work their thoughts were far from it. In a timber yard and waggon works on Cockhedge there were numbers of men " bottled " and kept prisoners (most.of them of their own free will, for they were liberally supplied with beer) to prevent them going to vote. Others were taker. down the river in boats as far as Widnes, and kept out, of the way there. I was captain . over 12 men who volunteered to patrol the streets (the town • being divided up into districts, different gangs being appointed for each district) during the whole night previous to the voting day, to endeavour to prevent our men being taken. Of course, the treating and "bottling "was carried on by both sides. The workingman of to-day has a great deal to -be thankful for in the ballot act. I remember at a municipal election, when I was working as foreman joiner at Roberts, Dale and Co.'s chemical works in Knutsford- road, one of the directors came into the workshop and asked us to put our coats on and go along with him to the polling booth to record our votes. I objected and said I would go in my dinner hour, but was informed " Don't., forget where you?-bread and butter comes from." However, I had my own Way in the matter. I can remember Warrington Fair being held in the Market Place, opposite the big lamp by the Barley Mow. The Council Chamber (with the Fire Engine Station underneath) was in a line- with the Barley Mow, in what ie now Market-street, and the Post Office was also adjoining these premises.</p>
        <p>Many of the old inhabitants will remember the floods at Latchford caused by the river overflowing its banks. On the door post of a house in River-road there is a brass plate giving the dates of the different memorable floods, and the depth of water. In some of the houses the water was as deep as 20in., and in our house I remember the water reaching as high as the bottom of the kitchen oven. -On one occasion a man named Dick Nunnerley sailed in his boat right up to the door of thelouse in which I lived in Sparling- street, off Knutsford-road. I well remember the Russian War and the rejoicings all through the country at its termination. Sports and games of all sorts were held in a field down Arpley. There was also also a tournament on the river. There used to be some excellent boat races, four, six, and eight oared boats. The course was usually from Mondock Bay and Round Walton Arches and back. I was one of the crew of a 47ft. long by 2ft. beam boat out- rigger (8 oared) called the "Alarm." The grand stand to witness the finish of the races was usually erected where the Warrington Cricket Field now is.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>In my boyhood days there used to be four night watchmen for the four main streets of the town. James Davies was the watchman for Bridge-street and I have seen him patrolling the street and heard him (as his duty was) calling out the time every hour, and also the state of • the weather—rough, fine, or whatever the case may have been. A man named Clough was the watchman for Buttermarket-street. There were also I think two constable t for daytime. We called one 1` Bogie Heyes " and the other "Dan Dagnall." How these two managed the crowd during, say an election, I cannot understand. I suppose the fact of the matter was they never attempted, and I think they were wise.</p>
        <p>Whilst employed at Gibsons I assisted in pulling down the Trinity Church old tower (which was of wood) and also helped at putting in the new clock which was presented by the late Mr. John Crosfield. I remember the formation and making of the Cemetery in Manchester-road. There was a farm house standing at the spot where the Cemetery gates are now. Over 50 years ago (when I was 19 years of age) I joined'the Ancient Order of Foresters, in Warrington, and have been actively associated with it ever since. Every office in the district (excepting Secretary and Treasurer) I have filled, and at hresent, am a trustee for several branches in connection with the Order. I look upon the work of friendly societies as being of the utmost importance, and although I have devoted a great deal of my time ana labours to that work throughout my life, I don't regret it, but ' rather rejoice in the thought that I haves een able to do some- thing, to help forward the movement for the encouragement of thrift and mutual helpfulness. A sketch of my life, however brief, would be far from complete if I omitted a reference to my dear wife (previously Miss Elizabeth Porter, also of a Warrington family of long standing) ,whom I married in the year 1863, and who has thus been the sharer in my joys and sorrows for over 46 years. Had it not been for her loving and self-sacrificing nursing during my very severe illness (some of which extended for over a period of 12 months) my "Life's Story" would long since have been "written." Never man had a better wife.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE SMALL HOLDINGS. _ A Question bs Mr. King, M.P. Mr. Alfred King, Liberal member for Knutsford, in the House of Ccrumons on Tuesday, asked the hon. member for South Somerset, as representing the Board of Agriculture, how many applications for small hold- ings had been made to the Cheshire County Council; how many of the applicants have been approved as suitable tennants ; what was the total acreage applied for and the acreage required to supply tile requirements of the suitable applicants ; how many applicants had been supplied and with what acreage; how many applications bad been refused on the grounds that the landowners had refused to enter into a voluntary agree- ment ; and what steps were being taken to meet such cases by theapplication of the compulsory clauses of the Act ?</p>
        <p>. Sir E. Strachey, in a written reply, said : 269 applica- tions for 5,765 acres have been received, and 177 of them for 4,159 acres have been approved. Forty-six applicants had been supplied with 933 acres. The County Council has not found it necessary as yet to put into force their compulsory powers owing to any refusal by landowners to supply the land required. Mr. King also nsked concerning the application of John Oldfield, of Lane Side, Rainow, near Macclafield and was told that the Board were making inquiries.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES:—According to "Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, July 9th, was 137, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 66; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 71. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year: Bankruptcies, 91; Deeds of Arrangement, 92—Total 183; being an decrease of 46. The total number of Commercial Failures recorded during the 28 weeks of the present year is 4,337; the total number recorded in the corresponding 28 weeks of last year wall 4,582, showifig a decrease of 215.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PETER WALKER &amp; SONS, LTD. Trip to Blackpool.</head>
        <p>PETER WALKER &amp; SONS, LTD. Trip to Blackpool.</p>
        <p>It hardly seems over twelve months since several friends of Messrs. Peter Walker &amp; Sons, Ltd., "were nearly drowned" by the weather at Morecambe,but such is the case. It was exactly twelve months last Satur- day since the brewers visited Morecambe on their annual excursion, and those who went were quite ready for another trip, so after elaborate preparations a party of about 1,200 journeyed on Saturday last by special train to Blackpool. The first train left at 5.55 a.m. and the other two followed a few minutes later. Blackpool was reached in good time, the journey only occupying an hour and a half. Arrived there the visitors were free to wander about at will, and many gladly availed them- selves of the opportunities afforded by the firm, the Company having made arrangements for free admission to the North Pier and Pavilion, Tower and entertain- ments. The day was beautifully fine and admirably suited to visits to the South Shore where the visitors enjoyed themselves to their heart's content. That the water chute, switchback and the scenic railway were favourites was shown by the way in which they were patronised. In the evening the staff and a number of invited guests were entertained to dinner at the Hotel Metropole Councillor J. Fulton Smith presidel and was supported by the Mayor (Alderman W. J. loorshaw), Councillor Birtles, W. H. J. Jenkins, Edward Draper,J. J. Dunn, C. J. Smith, Captain Potter, G. Johnson and others.</p>
        <p>After an excellent dinner the Chairman in proposing the toast of the King, said: "We as Warringtonians have been rewarded for our loyalty by a personal visit from his Majesty and I think we cin be congratulated on having such gracious condescension shown to us as a town—to have his Majesty to come and honour us with his presence. We feel that Warrington has received what had really been her due. (hear, hear.) In regard to its loyalty to the Sovereign aad also with ri-grsrd to its development in municipal and industrial affairs, War- rington is a town that is known throughout the whole world and we think we cannot bs blamed for expressing our honest opinions. We think that the time has indeed come for Warrington to be called a royal borough, and I hope that that will soon be culminated and if possible during the reign of our present Mayor." The toast was drunk with musical honours.</p>
        <p>In proposing the health of the "Queen .and Royal Family," .the Chairman atid that most of the child' en bad not seen the Queen, and he hoped that soon we would have another royal visit if only for the sake of the children, because they were those who were to follow us, and it, might be something for them to tell their children that they had. seen the greatest King the world ever saw—(hear, hear) —and his most gracious Consort.. (Hear, hear and applause). 'he toast was heartily drunk. The Chairman said that the annual excursion had been graced by Walker's good weather. He very much regretted the absence of Mr. Hutton, their head brewer. Continuing Mr. Smith observed that Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons Ltd., had always been good friends to their employees, and naturally it was reflected in the employees by them giving good service to their masters. There was always that friendly feeling which should exist between employer and employee. The employer and the employee should work with etch other. The one with the incentive of increasing hie business and the other-with doing his duty to his fellow men and to his God. Ile hoped that that spirit would be carried on by all the men of the nation. (Applause). , Mr. W. H. Jenkins then proposed the toast of "The Mayor,"and said that the better part of his (the speaker's) business life had been spent in Warrington. He bad seen greatly increased activity and development,at which they all rejoiced. There were ever temporary aberrations and depressions in all business mitters. We were pass- ing through, and to some extent were still suffering from that, but with the natural courage which animated our country we still looked forward with hope. We did not intend our working powers to become rusty, rather did he see signs on every side that the Englishman was what a very great statesman said—" waking up." Ihe country was becoming alive to a sense that there must be of necessity a certain amount of selfishness in regard to our home affairs and our success as a nation. Our duty was first, to ourselves. (Hear, hear.) In regard to business, he sincerely hoped that the spirit which we saw awakening—the business capacity—would be for the 'Welfare of the nation. The Mayor like most of them had grown with the growth of his native town, and they rejoiced at his success in life. (Applause.) The toast was drunk to the accompaniment of " For he's a jolly good fellow." A cheer was also given for the Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>The Mayor in responding said he thanked them for the kindly manner in which they had received the toast. He congratulated them on having such a fine day for their excursion. He was ex.-eedingly proud of the people of Warrington and was delighted at the handsome manner in which they responded to receive the Royal visitors. But at the same time be was dis- appointed because they were disappointed in not having the view that they ought to have had. It was not the King's fanit and it was not the Royal family's fault, because they all knew that his Majesty was ever ready to show himself to his people. When he (the speaker) went to see Lord Knollys the promise was that his Majesty should enter the town in an open air. The King while he was in the car, told hint that he was proud of Warrington and of the loyal way in which the people received him. His Majesty was very much disappointed that he was not able to get out or the car. He (the speaker) might sty that his Majesty would consent to come to any little function that we might have—(a Voice : " Warrington Bridge ") —and he would certainly show himself thoroughly to the people of Warrington. Continuing the speaker referred to beer as the national drink, and he thought there was none brewed better than by Sir Peter Walker and Sons Limited. They would see all over the world " Walker's Warrington Ales." (Laughter and applause). Ho was sure most of them had tasted it—(laughter)-- and he was sure they found it very good. Concluding he said he could honestly say that there were no houses better conducted than Walker's houses. (Applause Alderman Tinnion proposed that they thank the firm for the kind invitation and the very handsome manner in which they had catered for them. . He had attended ,t hose functions for a considerable length of time and he was prepared to go to the ends of the earth with them. (Laughter). He could readily endorse what the Mayor had said about the houses. What had pleased him was to notice that not only amongst the officials of the firm but amongst the workmen was a feeling of comradeship, which was always a healthy sign for an important firm. As to the quality of the ale they brewed unfortunately—or rather. fortunately—(hear, hear)—being a teetotaler he could not say. (Laughter). The quality of the ale was according to all those who had tasted it, excellent. He would couple with the toast the names of Captain Potter, Mr. Dunn anl Minoru." The toast was drunk n ith warmth of feeling, to the singing of "For they are jolly good fellows." Mr. Dunn, replying to the toast, mentioned that the firm were going to brew lager beer at Burton-on-Trent, and the cost of doing so was £20,000. They had got the celebrated Dr. Von Leer to show them how to brew it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman next proposed the toast of " The Railway Officials." He observed that a big manu- facturing buEiness could rot get on without having intimate relations with the Railway Companies. The railway officials had certainly done their best for Walker's and had helped it to be&amp;me one of the premier firms of Lancashire. He coupled the names of Messrs. Toanley and Wooding with the toast. Aleesrs. Townley and Wooding suitably responded. Councillor Dirties in proposing the health of the Chairman said that he was about the busiest man be knew. (Applause). He added the name of Mrs. Smith to the toast.</p>
        <p>Mr. F'ulton Smith in replying to the toast which was very heartily drunk, tail his health hal been drunk pretty often and well drunk.—(Laughter)--but he might say that that had been the best drunk. He had always tried to gain the conVence of Messrs. Peter Walker and Sons and if be had encouraged others to d the same for the firm he was rewarded.</p>
        <p>Songs were pleasingly rendered by Mr. J. W.Westcott and Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, Mr. Ford accompanied on the piano. 'I he dinner concluded by the singing of" Auld Lang Syne.". The journey home would have satisfied the trippers had it been two hours less, for it took no less than three hours and twenty minutes:-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>NO. RELAPSE IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>NO. RELAPSE IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>It is often said to a sick person," You're all right now, but we must guard against a relapse." Doan's Pills put you all right and also safegoard you against a relapse. Here is Warrington evidence straight to the point. "I have never been laid up like I used to be since I used Doan's pills; they have made a wonderful differ- ence in my health, and I have told numbers at)out them? The above was told to one of our representatives by Mrs. L. Bolton, 7,oldham-street, Latchford, Warring- ton, over eighteen months after WO first heard of the benefit shebad derived from Doan's pills'</p>
        <p>In describing her case, just after slie wss cured, Mrs. Bolton said : 'When I began with Doan's pills I was almost doubled up with the pains in my back. If I stooped at all, my back was Sc weak that I could not get up for some minutes, an.l then I hid to stand still for a while. At other times the pains caught me like stabs from a knife, and I felt like falling. "But since taking Doan's pills I have lost all the pains, and my back is much stronger. lam better In every way. and Doan's pills are the only medicine, have to thank. as before 1 usen them no medicine did me an good. (Signed) L. Bolton." Backache, shooting or continuous pains in the head. limbs, or joints, urinary disorders, broken sleep, con- tinual tiredness, or nervousness, are some of the symptoms of Kidney disease. Rheumatism, Dropsy, Gravel, Stone, Neuralgia, Lumbago are some of the ills caused by Kidney disease. Doan's pills act directly and effectively upon weak and diseased kidneys. Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post fro°, direct from Foster-McClollon Co., 8, Wolfs-street Oxford-stroet, London W. You are sure to get the right medicine if you ask distinctly for DOAN'S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>THREE MONTHS IN INDIA, SYRIA, AND PALESTINE.</head>
        <p>THREE MONTHS IN INDIA, SYRIA, AND PALESTINE.</p>
        <p>By Truda H Crosfield. (ARTICLE II.)</p>
        <p>ARTICLE 11. RAILWAY TRAVELLING IN INDIA. Travelling in India is on the whole extremely easy. The trains do not make much over thirty miles an hour and the lines seemed to me remarkably well laid. The carriages have long leather couches, either side, with two slung above for night use. One can lie back on these couches by day and watch the scenery glide past; and the only drawback is the dust. There are gillmills, or wooden shutters, to the windows (my spelling of Hindu words is only imitative, and not guaranteed) to try and keep out dust and let in a cool wind. But they do not do so. The dust pervades everything, and in the morning you awake to find throat and nostrils choked with it. Long journeys are made by night when possible, and one travels with pillow and blankets wherever one goes. The scenery of the Indian plains in the cold weather reminded me remarkably of England. The first thing that struck me was the quantity of trees. I in vain tried to remember their names, but in shape and</p>
        <p>trust the English rule-; and however much the Bengali babus.(educated men) may speak or write sedition, they are as the foam froth on top of the deep and silent waves of the real people.</p>
        <p>VILLAGE BANKS. Amongst other schemes, my cousin was deeply interested in the "spreading ofismall savings banks to be run by Government amongst the villages. The poor farmers have hitherto had to borrow money at exorbitant rates, whereas by this new scheme, if the village will unite together and pay a small interest, they can borrow sufficient to obtain cattle, seed, apkliance.o, which they would have been unable to do, acting alone, and having to borrow at such high usury. It is ideal ch-operation at work, and can only be done where goodwill and mutual trust reign in the community. So well has this scheme already succeedetl, thanks to Mr. Freemantle (C.S.) who has the working out of it, that even the tiniest villages were beseeching to have a branch bank established. .THE -BRITISH RAJ.</p>
        <p>size they were not unlike chestnuts and Indeed, the more one travelled, the more elms. Everywhere ondisaw green cornfields one was literally astounded at the superb threaded by yellow sunburnt paths : the rule which a few thousand British hold over rice (or paddy) fields are more common to the three hundred millions of India,and at the Central and Southern India. There were-no unbounded good which our Government has</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>A.N INDIAN POTTER AT WORK.</head>
        <p>A.N INDIAN POTTER AT WORK.</p>
        <p>walls or hedges, but crops, crops every- where, and the one storied dwellings of the cultivators were grouped under groves of trees in their midst, much like ati,English homestead. A feature of the flat landscape is the dried-up water courses, or" nullahs," which intersect it, varied by irrigation channels where there are no nullahs.</p>
        <p>done them. The British raj (dominion) must surely be one of the happiest and at the-same time bravest supremacies the world's history has ever seen. When one sees it at work in India, and hears of its splendid achievements in raising the status of Egypt; when you meet, as. I did, more than one educated Syrian who heartily expressed the wish that Britain would take over and administer his own country, one cannot but feel proud of the nation which has such capacity for good work. For it means that our Englishmen stand in the eyes of the natives for fair- minded dealings, for honourable high endeavour, and a just use of the power which they sway. A bright page in England's history is that which records the great names which have shaped and ruled India for her; out in that vast land, where the English are few, and self-reliance, and endurance and his own strength of purpose along can prosper a man's work, some of our cleverest and bravest have grown and shone forth. Such names as Lords Wellesley, Canning, Lawrence, who made irrigation, Mayo, who made railways, Dalhousie, who enlarged, consolidated and improved, Hardinge, Sir Henry Lawrence who made the Punjab the model province of India, and ir Herbert Edwards, whose counsel saved India for the Empire, should rouse our pi esent generation to fresh courage and endeavour. And if we search for the revers de la medaille, for</p>
        <p>. - : . - . -2•":-. ~••• ' ' • - :', . , ~ ';','',. : ..t.,,. ' ' „lA, -' •., , ,L,4,,,5,- - -!• % ~,,,,.. :‘,-..„ 4•4,„-;,.,..),0,-- . ;. •_, .. :.,;•.... --: ,: . ~.,,,.:' ~,..::,,-,,,,„: , • ,‘„,,,,,,,,,,,,,1k,,,,,,-,,,,,,..x;•,::, , ~,-,,,,,-,,,,....i., . f„.. J.:,-,„ii:.....••.:• -,-,2,;,...4,,, ~,, ..,, ' - 7 : -.; ". '' • , --,--','- '‘-::„. „f•,_ --:,-;,,,.; - .`,:7l';'''''''-' - ,1-•::''.,t4 ''‘,.."Clif.';','',-- ",..,.-\?•t'..'-'-'..*•›.-..'--.:., .44z-: -. , - s----- , ~. ~ ~, •\,. -,..,:,•:,,,-...-,... -- , .-, ...i,---.1- •.4 •-•,.., , , -0. -.. •&lt;4,. ~..4,,,,„. ... ..,. , ~. . , ' ‘, ''s ' - ' • -', . -, \ ' Is'''','s ,i/Ot ~ , - . -. q. &gt;,, ~-.•,,vii1,,:;.:,,,,, .• ~ -,,, ~,,, 'd t 11;1,, ti-- , ecv -" . • I'. ; ...,,,I:41•:•1 . •:,,i: 7'4 ' --411 .' - t' ',., . ~ _ i':.,, -.-:..., •.. ,-*.i • - '''','•.F,,,,, ,- ,- '••••••,«,-.4,'' -• A` -,f -- ~ ':,,,' 1- - , kt,' - --`-'lO-41 4 ...,..;,„,,, ~,itt.. 4, . -.,..... -•,, _ , -1.,... i.,... . .... -,.....*- •' • ,-. 't•.7..., •- • :-.....- -.•,_ • .4 jiii, ~:,„ iy.e.t. ~. - , :‘f",aii , ' ' .. } ' ...• •''' ' ' ' '''''''' . • , i'l , '''' • ttY• 4. i t ' ‘..........• 42,,,„;,.„-• „...p.,..' ~....--..- ' .."21.5il A-41%;;;4,' ''' ; .....,,, • ' ;:gite,..'.....M. .111/Z.5,11.k 31,164.,..'' -'' • , „,.. •.' ;$ t 7441, ,',',ll‘A, ' sL., t,,,--„gr:,',.:',,,,,, '-,..•:••-,''';',- '4llll4l'e. ' 44.1 ''*•-;;t:.&gt;"0:•'r.'"•.t:,••'• • '', -, • '' '• , , . '..-'.• , " '-"qn '',,t,..-,%,..• •::,01 i'-'''', . •-, -, z„.;., )i, \ % , .. , ' ~,,•'-';3•':, '- '' - •,, ',i,,,,"-•;Ctt:;:.'-',,'-‘",: s . ~„ ' „:,,ty. ..0 , , .7., , • .i. , ...e,.....„., • ~ , ~,,5,••,`,.:,-,'• • ..„...tr.,-.„,-1,5,-IIIVI*; • ... i.,_. fi. I ..,.,„ ...... . -, • - ~ • ••••,‘''..-.4: `,,,,,,,,'.;-",',„-',-....?r"."---• ', • i, ,• ~- ,--,,,,,,.-..,,1....,,, .. .:‘,5- . :......., •,,,,, ..,. .... 4:,.. ........,•„ ,••?,.,.,4.1-•,- , ~., ~ :: --; ,• ,4.....="2. . ' 'MP '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>IRRIGATION CIIANNELS AND WELLS.</head>
        <p>IRRIGATION CIIANNELS AND WELLS.</p>
        <p>The amazing labyrinth of these channels is one of the triumphs of our engineering work in India, and the water is either diverted into them from the gredt rivers or pimped up from wells in the midst of the fields. These wells are the most familiar sight of all in India. Usually they are high at the rim and have an inclined plane sloped away from them, down which two bullocks slowly descend, whilst their rope pulls up the great leather pannikin of water, to be tilted into the mouth of the channels. Then the ryot (peasant) drives the bullocks up the plane to relower the pannikin ; and all day the groaning and creaking of the wells go on. In the Punjab, specially, I sa* the ancient Persian wheel at work. This wonder- ful old method is hardly to be beaten. A long last, covered with perhaps a hundred or more earthen jars, goes endlessly round, the bottom jars scooping up water in turn, to spill it into the conduit leading to the channels as they reach the top of the well and turn over. I was strongly reminded of the</p>
        <p>, - . . . . -:. .. ,;., ,„1,..-'4:•,4.., ~ - . .., ...IN ~,,4„0.., .A-.5.••:,:0).';.*:,....4.4'",;..,,,k,:"4-.. - ..... •-''''''' i .. ,e A • ;„..0..;•7•;.,0.4.?• ,t;,, ...,,,lq;,0 '•• .:•:.4."... ,I, a; • ,s • ?::.tfr,,4,-it • 51..,,,..1,40,* •,• ', .5,',- ..2 (.. .• - ...4.•..„.„.„4-.,e4. , . 1,.. 0.,":5„;;,,,, •'• ;;',,,,), - ~,,1,.; ~. - .'•:.r. g'. ' . • s.--. :;;.,.' -: • •., ,',..1' ,•^ - . ' •••' ' ' il-',,i.• • - 0, '..• '!*- &lt; ',s ; ' ...- . ,;131 . ~;.$ 7. c!... :...,:,...- .-..- ~......,.. ....-.-,-;. ~,,, ,_&lt; - ' .. ~ - --,-i., I ''''' ''''': : ''''' 4 'A..:./....../t ' ~" . 'lfi..%' -IT. '''..t: • ', g:'.'l.i,CP—f' 1 T,..C.5,' i,-I:t..*. ,' ~...f sw•••••••••. • • 4, k- ~-. , i; 1, , -,A :•,!--" ~.., • - _.,,,,--,,,,A7.4,--,ki„...4.:„0.__, -.65.,-.4,t-OT,--iiii.,-,:r-:,::“.,, li ~- ' -, - --, -4„',„" ..,;:tzek6,,''. 1.-- .„ , ,:-.,..,- 0,,i,,.' -,--...,-...,,,,A,_ ..,;-tifit,,,:,--..:4-•;:4,-...,,4::,--, „. _. )':-..:;',.*:ii.i7.774..F,:::.7.zik4.,7:1, !.me: i.,':-,,,p-,-;-...-t,*:-....,:e ~,•.,..I.IQ-44,-. ',..,,cti.,:, E.,,,,, .';fie.-.;,., #1.i....;,..Y ..;,:,.." 't". 'l4l'. : ..* *i •lb ,;:,:.%: .'" . "',.' ..*•.::41::.1'..'.*:;,..•.;:, ,- 4-'0%,ni,V4,...1°='-41, - ::': .'. '.- . ,$(..:.:„'-,0e,t,t,5%,,6jr.-.44 - ' ::',:',o**'4l '• :4$ ''-'t.' 'a: ... '',...,'f'.:..i5,-..,-. - "*t.1.7/.::',.''::;':.;'. ' - ~.. . ''' -.4s ; ~,.:;',:1„ ~;:::- 7:.:!'"...,'-: 1',,-;'1...5.r;,,;+,.... . 4;,,,tw:?.. - „..,..., ,•-.. , ..,..-,,,' , •••--- , ~.*".:1,,,, : .:..,,,,,, .....,, , -.. -,...;.'' ....;,,,..... •' .. e_ ..,,.,,,r4.0,,,,,, :.: ~,,,,..„:,,,,...„-....:. ..:,._......... .„,..,..,,.. ..,. , _ 4 !, li</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RESIDENCY, LUCKNOW.</head>
        <p>THE RESIDENCY, LUCKNOW.</p>
        <p>American dredgers at work in the Ship Canal; the principle is the same,but one bullock works the wheel at the well instead of a steamship. Above all, one is struck by the birds. They ,are innumerable and of the brightest plumage. Blue jays flash across the landscape like liv- ing sapphires, and prettygreen parekeets are only too common for the welfare of the crorfs. Black birds with orange wings, doves, magpies, and redbilled birds are everywhere, but the beauty of their colouring is discounted by the hideous shrillness of their cries. Even the bulbul, surnamed,the Eastern nightingale, is only passably pleasant to hear. CIVIL SERVICE WORK.</p>
        <p>_ , I was going to Cawnpore to join a cousin, Miss Moreland, who was on her way home, after five years spent in New Zealand helping her brother, who., headmaster at Christchurch. Another brother is Director of Agriculture in the United. Provinces; and it gives one some idea of the jurisdiction of Englishmen in the Indian Civil Service, to hear that in the U.P. alone there are 50 million inhabitants, 80 per cent. of whom, I suppose, are ryots or peasantry, as Lord Curzon was so constantly quoting when Viceroy. Perhaps I may be allowed to offer my small tribute here en passant to that wonderful administration the Indian Civil Service. The work is tremendously hard, and one wonders how Europeans can stand it through the hot weathers, for it seldom seems to slacken. My cousin was away in camp during the greater part of the year, moving amongst the people, hearing grievances, inspecting conditions of land, and putting the staff of the Agricultural College, which is just beside his bungalow, to work on the different cases. When drought comes, as so frequently happens, and famine,with its attendant relief works, is announced in the different districts, the strain of work becomes terrific. But the ryot is grateful; those great clumb millions</p>
        <p>instances of ex tortion ,oppression and trickery, how few such can be quoted, though rumour is myriad tongued, and malcontents are but too ready to shout aloud a "case." -Surely it must be the innate fairness-of the English race which above all makes them a fit instrument for Government, and the improv- ing of conditions amongst other races.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CAWgPOR E.</head>
        <p>CAWgPOR E.</p>
        <p>Cawnpore is mainly a manufacturing city. Mr. Macßobert's famous Woollen Mills are , established here, and spread out their influ- ence even to Thibet and Turkestan, whence the yaks—and mules bring down their raw material. The only "sight—if one except the bazaar, which is vividly picturesque and swarms with an unsually turbulent popula- tion, as we found when we drove down it—is the beautiful Memorial Garden, in the centre of which stands the Well. I defy anyone possessing the least imagination to read the acconnt of that desperate and valor- ous struggle at Cawnpore with its heart breaking end, without tears. It is given. in Fitchett's admirable "Tale of the great Mutiny," a book which I casually picked up for 6d. on the bookstall at Calcutta. This true tale of what our own people dared and endured, read on the very sites .of -their sufferings and heroic examples, made an impression I shall never lose. One wonders how we of the present genera- tion would have faced those trials. I strongly advise' anyone who cares for a true tale of courage and faithfulness till death to read "The tale of the Great Mutiny ;" it can be had as I said, for 6d. Over the Well, where once dead and dying Englishwomen were thrown down together, and little children ran screaming with fear from the bloodstained mutineers, and there was no man left to pro- tect them—over this Well now stands a white marble angel, its crossed arms bear- ing the martyrs', palm, its face very beautiful. No Hindus or Mohammedans are</p>
        <p>allowed to enter that truly sacred ground. Near by is the little cemetery, where amongst a wealth, of trees and shrubs glimmsr the white graves of the few men whose bodies were recovered after the tragedy. Most fell in the heroic defence of Wheeler's frail entrenchments—surely a deed Without parallel in history for heroic endurance in the face of impossible odds, as Fitchett says. The remainder were shot, as all the world knows, at the Murder Ghat (or bathing steps) where the infamous Nana Sahib and his treacherous general Tantia Topi had lured the surrendered garrison to embark in boats on the Ganges. Only one boat out of fifteen (I think) got away: and of its occupants only four men escaped. Cawnpore memories are heartrendingly tragic, but they are quite as nobly heroic as thdse of Lucknow and Delhi.</p>
        <p>MUTINY GIIOSTS</p>
        <p>The present city stands two or three miles further down the-Ganges bank than did the Cawnpore of the mutiny. My cousin's bungalow is three miles away along a dusty road, mostly shaded however by trees. In his garden, and further down the grounds, in a grove of mango trees, ace the graves of two Englishmen. The natives declare that the ghosts of murdered or murderers walk at night. Be this as it may, I certainly had a terrifying experience my first- night in the bungalow. Indian rooms open out of each other; and though there are doors, these are generally folded back to the wall for the sake of coolness, whilst h curtain replaces them.- All round the house, or back and front, run wide verandahs; and most bungalows are built on the ground floor only. At dead of night I wakened to hear unmis- takable footsteps traversing the rooms. So light were they that one might have put them down to imagination, had they not died away, to recur again, and presently to be heard slowly moving in the verandah. I regretted having extinguished my lamp, and left the small pistol locked in a bag! The thought of the graves recurred unpleasantly for these footsteps were extremely eerie; and it was only in the morning that my cousins explained that every Indian bungalow engages the services of a chaukidar, or night watchman, who prowls round the house and sorrletimes through it, to see that all is well. These chaukidars are drawn from the thieving class, and their presence represents a mild species of blackmail; so long as they are in one's service no other man of the thieving tribe will rob, as it would mean getting a fellow tribesman into trouble 1</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 17, 1909.</p>
        <p>whose husbands and fathers were fighting above,had to pass those dreadful hot months. In the cemetery close, by there are heart- rending inscriptions on the tombs. "Of such is the kingdom of Heaven" occurs over and over again on the grave of some tiny child. There were piteous and pathetic inscriptions of wives for husbands, and husbands for wives. But the gentle green'sward. seems to lie so peacefully on those past woes. We saw the Alumbagh, a country resi- dence of the former Kings of Oudh, which Havelock defended gloriously and where he died. The ruined Dilkoosha palace also con- nected with him and the Sikandarabagh, where our soldiers showed such superb bravery in breaking the high walls which guarded thousancs of rebels within their enclosure. 'Of public buildings, the .only one of note is the Imambara palace and mosque, built of brown grey stone on an enormous scale ; and the Hussainabagh, effective at a distance, but like a Christmas cracker inside, where olid silver is mingled with cracked glass coloured- balls ! Bagh by the way, means garden. Hindustanee is not a difficult language, but it certainly is not prkty ! We acquired a few dozen words, including the indispensable " geldi " (quickly) which is of universal qse, for the Hindu is as slow, and nonchalent as fatalists all the world over. _</p>
        <p>From Lucknow we travelled to Benares, the most sacred city in India. Here every Maharajah of note has a palace lining the bank of the Ganges. Rows and rows of steps, or ghats, lead up to these palaces, whose frowning dark-red walls and handsome architecture reminded me strongly of Florentine palazzos. But we are tired with our journey and will leave sight seeing till the morrow.</p>
        <p>(To be Continued)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>COUNTRY RIDES.</head>
        <p>COUNTRY RIDES.</p>
        <p>_ Miss Moreland and I here started morning rides, and as no side saddle was available', I had my first essay in riding across the horse. Providence alone kept me in the saddle when rounding corners the first two times, but one soon acquires balance, and I at once appreci- ated having two stirrups under one's feet instead of one when trotting. We rode out over the bed of the Ganges, here five miles broad, but shrunk to a sluggish stream some three hundred yards in width, which gave one no idea of what mother Gunga must be in flood time. One recalled Kipling's admirable tales of the bridgebuilders, and the scavengers (this is not its name, butrit relates the views of a crocodile, a wolf, and an adjutant bird.) We looked in vain, however for crocodiles, and I never saw a snake during my whole stay, and but very few insects. The bed of the Ganges is sown with crops and covered in other parts with a low scrub resembling sage, and the only drawback is the innumerable ratholes, which undermine the sandy soil, and give one fits when riding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>VILLAGE LIFE.</head>
        <p>VILLAGE LIFE.</p>
        <p>Other times we rode some miles into the country and generally succeeded in losing our way.. It was truly interesting, howeven-to ride along the dust laden tracks and see the bright birds and handsome trees, the mud- wall villages filled with goats and buffaloes, and thd peasantry baling water to the crops, or slapping clothes on slabs of stone by the water tanks. These tanks were usually covered with a crimson water weed, till they looked like firm red soil. I am told the people drink from the same tank in which they wash, but no harm seems to came from it. In the villages one sees the potter at work turning out we clay vases by the score on his rapidly revolving wheel. Monkeys with blue-grey coats and alert black eyes dancing along the housetops and swinging off into trees whilst they watch the humans* below, and sacred cattle stalking along with a slow and lordly insolence. The cheerful ring of brasswork ,resounds from the metal worker's stalls, and overhead is the serene pale-blue Indian sky. Kipling's lines, though written of the Mediterrean shores, recur to IT one's mind.</p>
        <p>" Day long the diamond weather, • The high unaltered blue., The smell of goats and incense And the cowbells tinkling through." In the evenings we frequently had lurid afterglows before the rapid twilight closed down upon us. The whole sky behind the treaokueldd turn glowing orange h red and purple colour, rkof-tetrneess w clouds which gave an ominous and unhappy look to the sunset, but then the • magnificent stars would come out, and the serene bright moon sail overhead. The land cried out for water, however: how the Hindus must have prayed to Indra, their sky-god ;'and how eagerly reports of rain travelling eVer the Punjab on its way from the .Himalayas were sought in the newspapers. One morn- ing a good shower fell, end instantly the smell of wet dust brought back the taste of England; the clouds softened the metallic brightness of the atmosphere to a:northern gentleness, and every dusty tree got its washing. _</p>
        <p>We Saw the Agricultural College boys at hockey, playing with as much keenness as any English youths; tho influence of this and other colleges upon the sons of Talukdars, or small landowners, and the educated youth of India, must be admirable. I must hasten on, however, for an immensity remains to be told. LUCKNOW AND ITS MEMORIES.</p>
        <p>From Cawnpore we went to Lucknow, and those who have read of the mutiny will imagine what it was to go round those twenty-nine shot-shattered gates and out- posts of the Residency, and to see how to-day, aS sixty years ago in the mild even- ing air _ _</p>
        <p>"Ever upon the toptuost roof our banner of England flew."</p>
        <p>The daring deeds that were done on this ground should, make it dear to us for ever. Not Leonidas at Marathon showed greater heroism than Captain Fulton, the garrison engineer, who would sit patiently- waiting in the mines, pistol in hand, for the counter- mining sepoys to break through; or the civilian .Kavenagh, that" man of quenchless courage and daring," who adventured dis- guised as a native, and accompanied by one, froth the Residency across the Goomtee and the enemy's lines, to carry information to Sir Colin Campbell. And this is only to mention two amongst scores of brave names.</p>
        <p>A glorious purple bougainvilliea was flowering at the foot of the Residency's shattered but invincible walls and beautiful trees mantle the scene of that great and long- drawn-out suffering. We saw the large cellars below ground, in which the women,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>IWARRINGTON CHAPTERS.</head>
        <p>IWARRINGTON CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>WARRiNGION'S FIRST VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>MORE ABOUT THE BLUEBACKS.</p>
        <p>It was a proud day for the Bluebacks when they assembled to receive their colours. Harts Head Meadow teemed with life, and the 'Volunteers were there to a Man, pipe clayed, and with every button shining. Marching to the Parish Church, the streets lined with admiring crowds, they listened to a sermon preached by their Chaplain, Rev. Daniel Mathias, from the text "Then sang Deborah and Barak, the son of Abinoam, on that day, saying `/Praise ye the Lord for the avenging of Israel, when the people willingly offered themselves." The text was felt to be very appropriate to Warrington's willing volunteers, and a printed account which has survived assures us that there was no less judgment shown in applying the text than in selecting it. The banners were dedicated at the altar, and from the Church the corps marched to a large field adjoining the parade -ground, and being drawn up to form the three sides of a square received the banners at the hand of Mrs. Parr.</p>
        <p>Her address has come down to us in both verse and prose. In prose it contains a genuine bit of eloquence. "In presenting you and the gallant men whom you command with a standard, I am sensible that it could not be placed in better hands. Wherever it is waved it will be found the guidance to victory, not the signal for flight Should the event of battle ever prove. dubious, they have only to look to their colours to remember that they are combating in defence of their wives, their children, their property—of everything that is dear to them, and the event of the battle will no longer be dubious."</p>
        <p>She took the silken banner and with a smile (The loyal troop attentive all the while) Thus spoke Accept this gift ye social band, Nor less esteem it from a. woman's hand ; Beneath its blaze our sacred right maintain ; Nor let dishonour tinge it with a stain ; Remember still—they fight in virtue's cause Who guard their being, their liberty their laws.' This said, a plaudit maned the air at large, For there was inspiration with the charge.</p>
        <p>A royal salute and a display of drill closed the outward ceremony, and the Volunteers adjourned to the Assembly Room, to do justice to an excellent dinner. The music and toasts inspired every heart and thunders of applause greeted every loyal sentiment. The heavens seemed to smile upon the happy town and all was sunshine without and within. "If the country as a whole were but like Warrington it might court rather than dread invasion." Such was the closing burst of eloquefte, and doubtless they all believed it. , HOW THE COLOURS CAME HOME. _</p>
        <p>But alas for eloquence. The following November the corps marched to Garswood to do honour at another present ation. The Volunteer Cavalry had come to Warrington and the compliment was being returned. But the sun did not shine and the fields were a quagmire. To resist the damp without the resourceful soldiers tried copious draughts of liquid within, and 'the story (alas, too true) tells that the return was lamentably in con- trast with the outgoing. Three or four, chosen because they could walk straightest, were deputed to march to Captain Dakinis "to see if the colours had come home." As if, forsooth, the colours could walk home of themselves. The next day, or day after,the colours did come home, but the flag followed the regiment and not the regiment the flag. Whether the flag ,came home in the secrecy of a carrier's cart, or whether a contingent fetched it under the cover of darkness, has never been discovered, but it is said that there were anxious looks when at future dinners an orator ventured on the usual phrases about "never deserting the flag." The silken banners with their bold motto "Pro Rege et Pat ria " were carried proudly for the next three years. Then after the delusive peace of Amiens military ardour waned and the flags were hung over the altar of the Parish Church. A quarter of a century brought great changes in the public mind. Waterloo had removed all fears of Napoleon, and the Volunteers no longer appealed to the popular imagination. The flags were taken down as inappropriate to such a place, and only a torn fragment has survived.</p>
        <p>A REVIEW DAY AND AN AWKWARD INCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Early in 1799 they were reviewed by General Nicholls. and another bright day cheered the loyal corps. The days which followed, however, were marred by rumours which they would gladly have disbelieved had disbelief been possible. By a bad piece of management the sentries at the entrance to the ground had been chosen from the " Maltouts." These had been instructed, to welcome the General by presenting arms, and did so the moment he appeared by presenting them at his head. The gallant General's cheek turned pale and the incident made a shockingly bad beginning to the day's review. It says much for generosity of the eminent soldier that at the 'close of the day he complimented them upon their "soldierly bearing and steadiness under arms."</p>
        <p>HOW THE " BLUES " SAW ACTIVE SERVICE. The most important service rendered during those troublous times was the sup.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>pression of a riot in 1799. A party of Irish rebels had saved themselves from being shot as traitors by enlisting in the army. At that period the consequences of almost any crime—even murder itself—could be escaped by enlisting. This party of rebels was being led through Warrington and when near Warrington Bridge overpowered their escort and made a savage attack upon the officer and guard.</p>
        <p>It was an opportunity for which many a loyal Warringtonian had been waiting, and though there was hardly time to put on all the regalia and bring out the colours, they made a fine show against the refractory Irishmen. As might have been expected there was considerable anxiety in the breasts of the relatives when the alarm sounded and arms were buckled on. Among the anxious ones was the affectionate wife• of Joshua Fletcher; who as she handed out his cumbrous hat and gave him a parting kiss (with the awful thought that it might be the last) called after him "Now, Joshua, as soon as ever they begin to be rough, do thee run home again as fast as thee can."</p>
        <p>The f3luebacks were very proud to have been calledpurto active service and promptly called the skirmish "The Battle of the Bridge." And they talked about the " battle " in season and out of season until the town was thoroughly convinced that there had been one. JOSHUA FLETCHER'S " LOYALTY " SIGN.</p>
        <p>The Joshua Fletcher mentioned above was a hatter who resided in Buttermarket-street and who displayed his loyalty, aid at the same time incidentally advertised his wares, by fixing over his shop a colossal wooden bust of the Duke of Wellington, capped by a huge cocked hat. The bust was considered very good but the hat was hideous, and in process of time after Waterloo the bust was purchased by an admiring shipowner and placed as a figure-head to a sailing vessel. The cocked hat remained as a legacy to Warrington, and in order to give it a note- worthy pedestal somebody undertook to carve another. figure. The result was the grinning face which from its point of vantage in Lyme-street has grinned over the heads of two 'or three generations. What- ever contempt we feel for the maker of the bust we may think well of the hat as a relic of one of the original "Blues." Joshua finished his days in London, where he had built up a lucrative business as quack- doctor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>RATTLE OF "ROAST GOOSE."</head>
        <p>THE RATTLE OF "ROAST GOOSE."</p>
        <p>Whilst we are on the subjeCt-o-fWiunteers and local battles it would be,a pity not to tell the story of the battle of" Roas,t Goose." Dr. Kendrick once told it with good effect at Liverpool. Lecturing to the Historical Society which had met at Liverpool he told the story of our famous Joshua Fletcher and his wife. It evoked roars of laughter from his Liverpool hearers, but when the laugh had subsided he proceeded to sober them by telling of the prowess of the Liverpool " Blues."</p>
        <p>It was in the days when Prince Charlie was on his way from Scotland and after passing through Preston had arranged to cross the Mersey at Warrington. To arrest his progress the Earl of Cholmondeley ordered the demolition of Warrington bridge and, probably because he could not trust the people of the town, ordered a party of the Liverpool Bluebacks to come over and take possession. Early on a November morning the party set out, but the shades of night found them weary and straggling on Penketh Common two miles short of War- rington. Suddenly the ears of the soldiers were pierced by horrid shrieks and the most dismal screams. The darkness lent horror to the cries, and as the voice of the Captain rang out ordering them to form close column and prepare for combat, it was a scene to strike fear to all save the boldest hearts.</p>
        <p>Firmly grasping their muskets they waited through moments that seemed like hours. Scouts were sent out and in their absence the suspense increased to agony. Amid the noise the cries of human voices could be distinguished and suggested to the perspiring soldiers that some village was being pillaged by the dreaded Scots. ..Each peered into the .darkness expecting every moment to see the flash of the enemy's steel. When human forms did appear through the gloom they were the welcome forms of the scouts. And what relief! A huge flock of geese had been asleep on Penketh Common and being awakened suddenly had taken alarm and rushed in all directions screeching at their hardest. In a moment with soldierly promptness the Commander grasped the situation and gave the order to charge. With fixed bayonets they rushed after the geese each man bringing a victim back on.the point of his steel. With the enemy borne aloft in triumph they came to Warrington and began to pluck and roast the enemy in a house quite close to the Bridge they had come to destroy. Flushed with triumph they arrested Mr. Wilson-Patten of Bank Hall on a charge of disloyalty, and it was only after two hours of argument that he convinced them of his loyalty. In the end he was released, and amid the fumes of the roasted emmy and of foaming Warrington ale there closed the famous battle of Roasted Goose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>OUR MISCELLANY.</head>
        <p>OUR MISCELLANY.</p>
        <p>In reference to our article of a fortnight ago on the Warrington Bluebacks, Mr. William Rowe, now residing in Stockton Heath, furnishes an interesting reminiscence. When the Volunteer Company was formed fifty years ago, a dinner was given to mark the event, held we believe, in the large room above Arpley railway station. On this occasion Captain John Clare gave distinction to the proceedings by appearing in his old " Blueback " uniform. This was probably the last occasion on which the blue coat of the first volunteers was publicly worn in Warrington.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Possibly only one or • two of the original Volunteers survived to see the company of 1859. James Ashton had just died at the patriarchial age of 86, but Captain Leigh survived, and was able to reflect the almost vanished glories of 1798.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>• The few relics of the first Volunteers which have survived are preserved in a case in the Museum and the visitor may see dis- played side by side the coat of the " Red- breast" and "Blueback," the latter a neatiand worthy uniform. The tattered remains 'of the flag referred to in our article to-day are also displayed and the brass heads of the poles by which the flags were borne aloft. Two flint-lock muskets of 1798 are also shown and are worthy of careful inspection, not only as antiquities but as showing the improvements which a century of experi- ment and invention has Made in the mechanism of firearms. On the opposite side of the room hangs the effective picture of James Ashton, of" Death or glory" fame, dressed in full regimentals, and conveying, we think, a very true and vivid impression of WarringtOn's first Volunteers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WOMEN'S GHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S GHAT.</p>
        <p>The Bath Pageant.—The Duke and Duchess Connaught., with Pi incess Patrice, are to inaugurate the Pageant which is to beheld in the city of Path fr( m July 19th ti 24th. Nearly two thousand years Roman civilisation made Bath a place of ease, wealt."; and luxury, and a few of our citii s a e richer lit material for a grand historical display. The Pegeaur, grounds are in the Victoria Park which our late opened before she catne to the throne, and amongst tre many famous residents and visitors to Bath nitlY „ ' mentioned Henry VIII, Queen Elizabeth, Que4t Charlotte, the Earl of Chatham, Pitt, Ne'son tali a h„ -"- of literary celebrites. Everyone who hr's read Fielding; Smollett, Jane Austen, or Dickens, knows Bath, °ft{ though they have never seen the leautifully aituitet city, and for tbnt reasen it is cf.; tam n that. the Pagea„ud will attract a very urge neml'er of American sfit, Colonial visitors. As a medicinal watering piece, is es popular and luxurious an ever. It is not aCI _ the past, but there is nowhere else in this country sihe,;," Ron:an remains are so dimly defined, where one la 73 stand on the stone slabs on which the Romen selus; e. stood. When the hot springs were first disco .rerei 4.1511 unknown, but it is ceititin that they were in favour with the Romans, and they are to-day jtl't same as they have bubbled torth through all tim centuries. The Empre-s EusaSuie and her Memoirs. —The Enin,r,l,a: EugiSnie has caused her sscretury to announce that has not written, and will not wi De any n e oolr!;_ Several authars have eh alt with the copious inemo' of the &amp;can I I•.;nipire, w,th the Empress as the cent.ral r figure, but much that, has been aid snout L'lmi cretrise Eug4nie is unauthentic. All the stories coucerning "ci first meeting with Nesoleen 111. ale ',piers OA °ssu. the oft-repeated statement th t shespoke of the Fran—, German aar as "my war "may be placed in the eat% category. The aged Fmpress has survived neellY,abil„. tho brave men and fair %%omen of her Court at 'Ss Tuileries, among whom General de, (3'allifTet, fought in the Crimea aid died last week, was a tninent fisure, and altogether it seems rather a P' that we are not to have the Empress's own v the Second Empire, its gaieties, alai its tragedies. Widow of the Balaclava Hero.—Tbe Earl of Cad •g who led the Light Bligade at B larlava, died in 1860 but his widow is still lii hag. Now a nonsgenasil; she is publishing her m. moire, which go back to !so days of Early Victorian Society. She was the bone!' Miss de Horsey, mit amona her suitors was tin ralebse Benjamin Di. raeli. Afteraards she was engaged ° t.„ Count de Montemotin, uncle of Don Carlos of Sielo' csountry, but the engageraets was broken off, and Miss de Hoisey married the then an exile living in this soot' hail of Cardigan. Lady Cardigan's second husband ce!ri the Coma de Laccustre, who clsitned descent from of Gaunt. the The New Marchioness of Ripon.—Lady do Groh ,Le new Marchioness of Ripon, has long Loin one el t'bet most promins nt fgures in Seciety, and eujoYs personal friendship of the King and Queen. Lady Gladys Het bert., one of the beauties of a cemoli; and when quite a girl mastic:l the late Lord Lonsda,..r-, who died in 1882. In 1885 the married Lord de are' only son of the late Lord Ripon,who has been Tresser's in the Household of the Queen since 1901. 110 Marquis is more of a sportsman than asspol:ticiasj whilst his wife follows many intellectual tastes e.an interests, and has done much for the success of ltali.at opera in Louder. Her only child is now Lady intiess Duff, and the Marchioness is still one of the youthful looking looking grandmothers in London Society. a • C: morning Luggage.—Opinions seem t he dislaet, on the suhject of the p ,rtmanteau or box, fitted AS chest of dr 'viers. No douist the listings do take nPett" geed deal of room, but unquestionably they keen °a„s luggage uncruseied, and in delightful order. Much PlZer be done, however, with an ordinary portmanteiti!,:s, means of elastic bands sewn to the bottom and through which boots and shoes, and other heavy ltel",,s can be thrust, thus preventing them from slidiug and crushing the lighter portion of the conies Another excellent plan ii to provide slips of linentbue calico (with tenes at the ends) which eaactly fit 4 poi tmanteau, and between each different layer -of tbillti; arrange it slip. By lifting up these slips, it is easl„,-D come at once to any particular item required, ssit"o-if unpacking, a desirable state of affairs in tbese (We flitting rapidly from one place to another. . More Sensible Modes.—Phis summer—lf we dO etne the season through which we are passing by such —modes generally may be said to have moved indo right direction. For instance, the elbow sleeve for red wear, has disappeared, and we are therefore 5r.„,.,• many appalling exhisitions in the matter of arms. Ina the transparent yoke, I y day, has sanished toe, we therefore no longer look as if we had popped evening blouse in mistake for a shirt, or an aftere°.'" bodice. Further, extremely narrow and sheath-t. skirts, such as have called forth Papal condelnuatt7er . are less and less in evidence, the "no hat " craze wa,Yral said to have passed, and altogether the holiday gin ;sit woman ere much nicer to look at th^n they were " year or the year benne. an Lace and Linen Coats.—Lace coats are marked Ow extraordinary low at the Sales, and tbis is prohltiftit owing not so much to the lack of fine weather ekl„s which to wear them as to our having discovered trio almost any kind of coatis more becoming and desirkllg than the one built of lace. This somewhat swee,Psleal statement does not of coursenpply to the beautiful 'its lace specimens worn over equally beautiful gownsill everything else in keeping, but to the ready-tO-7j,, imitation lace coats that crumple up and look tle,7 from first to tart. The same may be said of the Ito coat, which by the way is in much less favour than been the case for several seasons past. orli An Old-World Fabric.—Many very lovely old-'f,-„d, mualins may be " discovered " just now in shople-a the backgrounds being ivory white, with clustela 0, roe', or other flowers printed lightly upon tbetae Worn over various coloured slips, gowns built of „ sea, printed muslin present a totally different appear so much so as to be almost unrecognisable, thereforelig would seem as though a dress length of this charraltse fabric could scarcely prove a" white elephant bai gain hunter. Every girl or woman who Las to sl,t; e ;„., economy in dress should be able to build her ow 511r:(1 and at the moment many bargains in soft satins i!fig silks suitable for the purpose may he found "fi cheap." 11Y Ear-rings.—Ear-rings are being worn more genelea, than ever, both by day and by night, and theie ar° ways in which sparkling diamonds and jewels more effectively displayed. A very light design 1,,0 fine fringe or tassel, threaded with very scintillating diamonds. Anotht r takes the form eo, cage with diamonds and pearls set about all the bathe but for evening wear, many women are disporting, to very long and large eaarings which have descenden.op them from their grandmothers, the only alteratlip, being the substitution of the pin of gold or nititiusiti • fastening with a clasp, in place of the old and sn-se what unsightly" hook of bygone times, is to Womens Labour Day.—Women's Labour -Day he celebrated by a national assembly of women worker. and a fta eat Earl's Court in London on Saturdusi; July 17th. All occupations °psis to women, from Onto of doctors and teachers to pit brow toilers, and and Section h making factory hands will be represented, nen —re- section will carry its emblem of the bonnet in the pt ,4 cession which is to march t a Earl's Court, There. -a also to be a swerve t in lustries exhibition renvtillyli, open all next week. This will include women ei's maker* from Cniley Heath, girls making cardboltora boxes, clay p'pes, shirts, boots, artificial flowers, stbe other sweated g )(ads, all working wider exactlY he same conditions as when following their trades in '- ordinary way. - MADGE'</p>
