        <head>CURE FOR ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>A CURE FOR ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>Of all the diseases of the skin and scalp ir..l e torture and disfigure mankind, three -fourths ,arie ecxematous. Millions are born with eczema and t_tio. the only thing other millions have left when they " .e Neglect in infancy and childhood, irritating condorne, affecting the skin, .ignorance of its real nat,u,ibt improper remedies dnd many other causes that 1111:61,, be mentioned have created a chronic eczema whts'crs with varying severity, has afflicted countless during their entire lives. Eczema is purely a _ll5 i 6 disease. It is not hereditary, nor contagious mu' ,be impartially distributed among the rich and pttor, 'of high and low. The agonizing itching and burning 's the skin is the most distrest-ing symptom and 'it caused by the bursting of little vesicles filled with 3.0; acrid fluid Which burns as with fire the denuded skli, New vesicles form, fill and burst, scales form 11.Pqe scales, and crusts upon crusts until disfizurement 1 added to torture. t her The most successful cure for eczema, applied to the youngest infant or the oldest person, t'? Cuticura, the great skin cure. For more than dill. t, years it has retained its reputation as the Pitre;e,' sweetest and most efficient of curatives. That tho who have suffered long and hopelessly and who 113ce, lost faith in everything may make trial of CutictV: without charge, Messrs. Francis Newhery Charterhouse Square, London, E.C., will sell".th generous trial package to any address, together a 32-page pamphlet, giving accurate description treatment of every form of eczema, as well as otnt. affections of .the skin, scalp, hair and hands. Sons</p>
        <p>AFTER THIRTY-FIVE YEARS. The state of one of the principal thorouAhfaren Widnes—that leading in the direction of Liverpoolo has been a standing cause of complaint in the town 11 district for the past thirty-five years. By a resolutgo adopted at Tuesday's meeting of the Town Council Al necessary steps will be taken to effeat its thorolll3o repair. The negotiations between the Council and ti fiG frontagers have bet n of a very protracted nature,ar Tuesday's meeting the chairman of the IfighwaY mitten (Alderman Quinn) remarked that it was nat,',9hout tory that that the work was to be carried out w"" mourn to litiption.</p>
