        <p>e CHILD Pus OVER SANDY-LANE.—On Tuesday s.v:4_,ing a little girl named ‘Vils,n was run over in Oughtrington, by a motor car coming from le direction of Burford-lane There happened to Le 4 doctor in the car who saw to the child's injuries at ce• She was badly hurt about the face, but i 3 pro- c'..essing very favourably. NTP(M.NI CONGREGATIONXL CRUM:FL—In connection th the church 'the Senday school Anniversary c7irviees were held cn Sunday last. The pastor of the 14.1reh (Rev. A. Barrett) preached in the morning. in the ll'ernoon the Echo'ars gave a musical service and the • ev• T. Webster of Sale gave an address. In the even- the Rev. R. Sutton, of Manchester cccupied the The spry ices were very well attended and the °.lections for the day proved very satisfactory. Lv 11M WALKING DAY.—In connection with the tir,l'e Churches of Lymm, the annual walking day will of',"3, Place on Wednesday, the 2ist inst. Tne scholars ‘ue various schools will assemble at th 3 Cross and tr2ceed by way of Church Green, Booth; Hill, Eagle thi4.w and Rush Green, which the scholars u ill go ta el' Own schocils for tea and later assemble in Mr. ii;ellll.(lY's field (Booth's Hill) who Las very kindly lent smile for sports, games, etc. Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>- - eßiltrtosm LEAGUE FErE.-1 he members of the %re.PPenhall Habitation of the Primrose League held their annual fete at Grappenhall Hayes on Saturday, at Coe invitation of Mr. J. Charlton Parr, the Ruling ancillor, and Mrs. Parr, the Dame President. There titis an attendance of upwards of V), and a *pleasant e-e:e was spent in inspecting the beautiful grounds and kir ging in• competitions and snorts. The Pioneer under the conduct)rship or Mr. Smith, played 11!"tiots. Tel was servr 1 in a large rnsrquee, Mrs- pr?odcock catering.—After tea Mrs. Parr presented the Vsize3 to the successful competitors at the entrance to .03 hall. The following were the prize-winners:— 4 'Ming Conipetitio3.—',P Clarke; 2, Onions; 3, J. cughton 19a • Yards Handicap.-1, Boden ;2, Mitchell ; 31Hogg. 75 p lards (backwards).-1, Johnson; 2, Starkie ;3, lea Rae.-1, Miss Hankey ;2, Miss K. Edwards; If '53 lq• Wood and Miss L. Hough (dead Lest). Rice (120 yards).-1, J. Thomas; 2, J. Starkie; tjl3. Watson. eg-nf-War.—Mr. Morre3's team beit Mr. Onions's kt-In• The Pioneer Band and Mr. Hanke)-team were ti ad out first round.—Mr. Allan Sykes, prospec- spot, Conservative candidate for the division, lied" at some length in criticism of the 1,1, get which he said was avowedly aimed at one rlrss. Conscrvative party proposed a change in their na7"l.lsislom. They did not want to take all the taxes to soak-the,pockets or their own people. They. wan!el change their fiscal system—so change the•r tariffs the foreigner who came into their markets have to pay for that privilege. Padgate. Padgate Wesleyan Church. VISIT OF DR. BEET. te annual chapel arniversary services were preached b 4nis place on bunday last by the nev. J. Ag r Beet, `-'• In the morning Dr. Beet tot,k his text from it, xx. 28. He delivered a very powerful discourse tnea`„"? inipoitancl of m;nistry, sbevvirg that the true anti sor„Z'oeng of ministry is to render help of any kind to • e else. Christ came into the world not to be nistereJ unto,but to minister. The definite thought tery Purpose of Jesus when he was in the world was to %ouie others. If we A:sh to serve others it will cost us to Ae„til!ng• It cost Christ His life, and it is important coii,erYone who wishes to be a servant of Lutist to tlr• " the cost. He further went on to shew how 4,,ea'at laid down the principle that the only uay to illitness Was to help others, and in conclusion th,orted all to do their utmost to render to others all liiielAbeji) they could. The anthem "Our help and was very ably rendered by the choir, Mr. %V. te, of Culchetb, being the soloist. e evening the text was taken I. Cur. 3c. ix v. te tis'lng of the temple of God he likened the people tr_sn living stones of the temple and Christ as the to,r architect.. C. hrist does not intend His followers uuct aai E,/,a_tonesilying wasted, but wishes them to be built • -enletited together so as to form an immovable tire. here is a great deal in every church which Vfttlivorthy of the great architect and we ought to be • careftil in cur, building not to pull down the work During the service the Magnificat (Maunder) *t• itan anthem" Sweet is the sunlight" (Eggleston). Sang very feelingiy by the choir, the soloist being %It L. Mason, of Fearnhead. The collectirns which e in aid of the trust funds amounted to £l2 ss.</p>
