      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>4anitled 011 L DR. BEET'S LECTURE. b ttk • 'ue Monday evening Dr. Beet delive-el a lecture Christienity, Christ, and Civilization." Mr. by ih" Dock's of Penketh presided and was supported Th--ip Rey. Thomas Wadding and Mr. William Bennett. itra..e chairman said he was sure they would realise the e 073: honour it was to have Dr. Beet amongst them 14°1.e. There was no man anywhere whom they 11,111tht more of than Dr. Beet. Like Gladstone, Dr. ad always sought to find the truth and having sio„"`1 it had gone wherever it led him. Carlyle said lo:nTity WAS the ona thing which g ,verned all great broir• The simple,elmoat savage intense sincerity had (Arag,ht Dr. Beat to the position which ha held to-day. 1) --etaus (3). 1k0,,r; Beet, in a very lengthy addrevs, said that some elohi'tla asked why it %%Eta -that the-Churaes were so MA I A wise man once said that the wonder was not Tho,ao few came, the won ier was that so many came. eanle to the Church because they believed that the visible was a greater invisible, and that 7a4-11t, the present life was a life unseen. Religion 43t tho largest factor of human life, and was a great grature, of man as compared with other animals. At Illght there was a wonderful variety of religions. roli°l.3g all the religions of the world the Christian ten,Von occupied a unique superiority. All the fore- and most progressive -nations of the world Lod 11. Red to wie religion. All the Christian Churches All 'ar more in common than they had in difference. ettt Christiars believed that the Creator of the world Yia only begotten Bon into the wcrld to become a(.O proclaim salvation for man, to die for mar, to dirf,nom the dead and ascend into heaven. The Foo;;eace between Christian religions was trifling tel ecl with those great broad principles which all 114ti0:1! COMMOU. Comparing China (a non-Christian Reim") with Russia (a Christian nation) the rev. aavone,illan said that China possessed every natural tiver--agf`• 'I here was a magnificent cofistline, great The °, Test mineral resouroee and a vast population. thitkainaman was also very industrious. 1 hey would 446 at race by that, that it ought to be above all the 11, ot ;Lila of the world, but of course they knew it was esd 'no cise. It was a nation without machinery. It „rr surgery, and had no modern literature. In the pow etaveen England and China that great nation was befiLriftas- No European would go to live in China and it '-binese rule, but Europeans would go to Russia , 'l'a under Russian rule. We had to live under a</p>
