      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 17, 1909</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 17, 1909</p>
        <p>rule of our ow a in China. If any foreigner were charged with a crime, be was charged Lefore a European Court, because before a Chinese Court justice was so uncertain. Alluding to Russia the lecturer said they bad heard things about Russia vhich were not altogether complimentary to the Tzar. He was sorry for Russia. Nr.tere had be n very Lea% y against Russia. All the advantages of China were turned to dis- advantage in Russia. In Russia we had a country with scarcely any coastline or mountains. NU ,t a difference between Russia and Chins! Dr. Wenham ' had said that to pass from Caine with ell its advantages into Siberia with all its disadvantages was to pass from barbarism into civilizatior The en!que superiority of Christian nations became more remarkable when v e remembered that there was civiliza- tion crlltu-e and statesmanship long before Christ was born. The story of the ancient world was the succession of Empire after Empire. There wos no permanence. For the last 1100 years the story of Europe had been one of progress. Some people had wondered whether England would hecome worn out and die like nations of antiquity. Christian nations did not do that. When he read the history of Europe he found that it was progres- sive, but when he read the history of China he found they are beating time. If anything they were a little backward than they were 2,000 years ago. Someone may want to ',now about Japan? He would ask the question: " INnere would Japan have been but for the Weste-a. powers ?" If it had not been for the European powers she would have been exactly where she was 50 or even 5f 0 years ago. They had simply fo:lowed the eyaraples of the Western natives. They sent their boys to England, G st.nany and France to learn the lessors of the West. 1 hey bad learnt the art of war—perhaps that was not the nicest lesson—from the West, and they gave the Russians a pond lessont but it was with Cnr:stian weapons aril ristian strategy. They imitated us in everything, and their dress even showed what abject apes the Jirs were. Continuing the lecturer said that the great difference between the Christian and the non-Christian nsticns were the : anses of right and v rong, and hunarn kindness. In non-Christian antic-as their morality was inferior to Christian eations. Concluding the rev. gentleman said that civiraation was a bye- product of Christianity. (Applause). Mr. William Bennett in proposing a vote of thanks to Dr. Beetand the Chairman said that it was 30 3 ears since they bad Mr. Dodds at Padgate. Ha had done a splendid work in the town of Warrington and had dne one of the hest things for the to Am. Through his strenuous work he had brought about a state of things which was most desirab!e. He bad made few enemies and gained many friends. Dr. Bennett seconded Bud the vote was heartily carrier'</p>
        <p>He was informed that at the same collif•ries the day wage men were working from 6.30 until 2.30 on Saturday, when they were paid for three-quarters of a day. He hoped they would not go to work on Saturday unless they got a full day's pay. At Ashton's Green they wrre also vorking the extra hour on Friday, end he had written to the mantrg,rs telling them there would he trouble if they continued to do sos MUSIC AND FORTUNE TELLING. CURIOUS CASE AT ST. HELENS. #</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, on Monday, the Mayor (Mr. Foote) and other magistrates were occupied for some time considering Abe aliens' problem. A boy giving the name of Angelo Stroglio was charged with begging. A.constable said that on Saturday rth,ht the lad and ' others were going about the town pretending to play melodeons arid collecting coppers. He ordt:red him off several times, and then locked him up. A stalwart Itabn came forward to net as interpreter 'for- prisoner, He :aid that he bad five boys, but they were playing melodeons. He got a living ty selling fortunes.—The Clerk said that the police ought to route the man and the boys, and t information from eifferent parts of the country which could be forwarded to the Home Secretary. and the whole kt could be deported. The officer said that the man acted as malinger for the toys. • The Mayor: He is making a living out of the boys. It was only foot- months since the party visited the town before.</p>
        <p>'lhe Italie'n denied this, but in answer to the Clerk he said Le had to fortunes with him," but if they would put the case back he would go and fetch them one.</p>
        <p>The Beach fi7.ed the youth 20s. and cost,or fouiteen days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Penketh. Dr. Beet on "The Letter of Paul."</head>
        <p>Penketh. Dr. Beet on "The Letter of Paul."</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. A. Beet, D.D., paid a visit to Penlceth on Tuesd gy evening, when be lectured t 3 a good audience in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on "The Letters of Paul." Mr. John Taylor preside] and was supported by Dr. Beet and Mr. James Dodds.</p>
        <p>_ The Chairman congratulated Nlr. at d !qrs. James Dodds in securing the services of Dr. Beet, who was a very able expositor of Biblical truth. Dr. Beet in a very able and prolonged lecture said that the letters of Paul were very weighty and powerful, Even Paul's enemies adn.itted that they were Eh. Evetyboda about the Mediterranean admitted in the middle of the 2nd century that they were written by Paul. Some people said that it ought. to be St. Paul. Paid was [rho% e any title; his name carried everything before it. Ihe letters of Pail were the most valuable of the thought of the past. The first question was" Are they genuine, and did they actually come from the great Apostle V' The question of the genuineness of those letters touched the interests of everyone of us. the earliest-w itnesscs of the author- ship of those letters were the letters themselvea. He would talk atout " Romars " first. As he read that letter he was dr rply in-preszed with the keen intelli- gence, the width of view, the well talenced judgment, and the high moral character of the man who wrote it. It was indisputable that the man who wrote that letter must have been what he had described hinr to be. It was a letter we could not imitate. The writer stated in systematic order the main points of the Gospel of Christ ; and he trought his letter to bear on the things of daily life. He did that in such a fashion that we could apply it to the direct life of our own day. That letter claimed to have been written ling before Paul went to Rome; but st'll ha told us that he NSEts intent to go to Rome in a little time. Da. 'het proceeded to [rove the genuiness of the letter. He said that a document written 160 years after the birth of Christ gave a list of the books of the New Testament and among them it gave the 13 litters of Paul,end it gave a few points about the cell' enta of each. What it contained exactly agreed with those 13 letters that we had in the New Tes'ament. If it was not written by Paul, who did write it? No. fool wrote it. If any other man lad written it e lay should he palm it off as being written by Paul. It eas written by a man of gigaatic intellect, and the man who wrote would not put anybody else's name to it. Ber;des it would he a fraud. As we real the lettcr we felt it was not a fraud. The details were eo'manifold that it must have been written by Paul or else it was a* deliberate deception It could not have been written after his death, because we have very good evidence that Peal lived for many years after his apostolic activity came to an end. It was written before he sent to Rome. Supposing that ten years alter the death of Paul somebody had brought that letter to the Church of Rome—a letter which prophesied his going to Rome—it would be seen at once that it was a fraud. So clear was that, that every scholarly infidel admitted to-day that that letter was written by Paul. All that the lecturer had said applied just the game to the Ist Corinthians. It was evident that they were from the nine pan. There was further proof at out the Ist Corinthians, which was a severe crndemnatien of the Church of Corinth. Paul charged the Crurch at Corinth with all sorts of things. He charged them with tolerating immorality in the Church. Would any forger writing a letter which he hoped to palm on as Paul's have written a letter like that ? That letter sass an a' iding monument of the world. Another proof of that letter was that a letter written at the close of the first century was sent to the Church of Corinth which referred them to the letter of the blessed Apostle Paul which he wrote to their fathers. It was evident that the people were quarrelling as their fathers had done 30 pars previous. If Corinthians had not heen written by Paul it would have aroused indignation in the Cnurch of Corinth. When we looked into his letters we looked into the heart of Paul. The lette s were too earnest to be insincere. As we real those letters we stood by the side of Perri and looked at Christ as Christ was seen by Paul. We saw bow Paul bowed with loyal homage in the presence of one infinitely greater than himself. We could not find another case where one bovvad to another nal in the fashion theft. Paul bowed to Christ. Con.;lu ling, the rev. gentleman said that the letters of Paul were es.ential and ii peculiar element in their value was that we were quite certain of their authorship. (Applause.) Mr. Withinshaw, in proposing a vote of thanks to Dr. Beet, the Chairman and Mr. and Mrs. Dodds' said that Dr. Beet had certainly let a god deal of light upon the New Testamer,t. Mr. Watson seconded and said that when be first sew Dr. Beet the style and shnplicity of his character affected him most favourably. Mr. J. W. Hayward supported and the resolution was heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LORD NEWTON AND COMPULSORY SERVICE. During the House of Lords debate on Lord Roberts's Bill to introduce compulsory military service, Lord Newton said he was surprised at the attitude of the two front benches, who, he said, had abandoned the customary stage fight. Lord Lansdowne had been so warm in his commendation of iMr. Haldane's policy that it would be particularly difficult for the noble marquis to criticise the right hon. gentleman's scheme in an unfriendly manner in the future. He generally found that when even the two front benches were in agree- ment they were invariably in the wrong, and that it was the duty of every richt-minded man to vote against them. (Laughter). He could not help thinking that any important person who had listened to the debate could only come to the conclusion thatthe voluntary syst3m w,•s almost on its last legs. 'I he collapse of the Territorial Army scheme was only averted by a certain seneational play which was written for the purpose of advocating compulsory service, and was seized hold of by Lord Esher and the Secretary for Warand converted' into an advertisement for the Territorial Army. (Laughter). It seened to him that they would have to have recourse to similar methods to those which had been employed for recruiting the Territorial Army in four years' time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES DROINicING CASE.</p>
        <p>The body of an unknown man was recovered from the Sankey and St. Helens Canal, near Hutchinson's Works. -The description is as follows :—Aged about forty, height sft. 51n., full face, light browd hair, heavy light moustache, three eks' growth of heard. deformed left leg. dressed in navy blue serge suit, old lac A-up boots. A letter was found in one of the pockets bearing on address in the Manchester locality, ar.d the names of John Hayes and William Hayes. ELECTRICITY V. THE CHEAPEST GAS. - The world's cheapest gas, which is that manufactured at the Widnes Corporation Wt rks, is likely in the near future to be Lrought into competition with electricity. At Tuesday's meeting of the Town Corncil it was reported that a private firm had notified their intention of applying for an ordet to supply electricity within the borough. The C i ouncil obtained an Electric Light- ing Older n.1901,40-las a result of the threatened move from outside a suli-conuritt- e has been appointed to tike tl‘e matter in hand for the installation of a Corporation scheme. It was ititin.t.ted at yesterday's meeting that a definite scheme bad been formulated, and would be presented in a few weeks.</p>
        <p>RESCUE BY RUNCORN DOCK POLICE. Late on Monday Inspector 11loores and Police- constable Sutton, of the Dock Company's police heard miles for help, and proceeding to the Alfred Dock they saw, a man sink. They" fished" him out on to a small lighter and applied artificial respiration for half an boar before the man showed signs of life. He wrs then taken to one of the d( ck huts and stripped and placed in blankets until about ftur o'clock in the morning, when he appeared little the worse for his immersion. It transpired that he was Joseph Rose, aged thirty-six years, a native of Littlehampton, Sussex, and a seaman on board the schooner Amanda, of Padstow. Be spent the evening in Runcorn, and when returning to his ship be slipped and fell into the dock. The nrompt action and skill of Inspector Moores and his colleague saved his life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN LABOURER'S UNHAPPY HOME:</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LABOURER'S UNHAPPY HOME:</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, Thomas Antrobus, chemicel labourer, The Holloway, was summoned for an aggravated assault upon his wife Frances. Mrs. Antrobus said on Saturday evening the defendant went home drunk, and without any reason he got hold of her hair and threw her to the floor and kicked her until she was black and blue. He had frequently ill-used her, and had twice thrown her downstairs.—A number of witnesses spoke of seeing defendant get hold of his wire's La'r, but they said Mrs. Antrobus was drunk. Defendant admitted striking his wife once. The reason was that she had not provided him with dinner, and when he went home to tea she was drunk on the sofa.— Inspector Ludlow and Detective Davies sAid Ihe wife was a very drunken woman, and the house was filthy.— Norman described the case as terrible, and bound defendant over to be of gocd behaviour for six mcnthQ. If his wife wds drunk he had no right to strike her. EASY TO SAY, BU r HARD TO PROVE. MR. MAX MUSPRATT AND TORY UTTERANCES. UndeL the auspices of the Widnes Division Liteial Association, Mr. Max Mesprstt held an open-air meet- ing at Cronton on Tircrsday, evening, befo:e a good crowd of villagers. Mr. W. Glover pr, s eed. Mr. Muspratt in the course of his address, enid that he had followed closely the progress of the recent Conservative campaign, and remarked that there was nothing easier to say, as seine of tbe Tory speakers had said, that the present Government was the worst the country had seen. It was extremely easy to say that, but be defied them to prove it. For the last, twenty years, with the. exception of a small l-reak, the Con- servativo party, had the power, and be asked them to comp re what they did with the progress made by the Liberal Government during the last three years. Review ing the measures passed into law, he paid a special compliment to the Children Act and its rro- moter, Mr. Herbert Samuel. 'Touching upon old age pension.. Mr. Mit.cpratt said that Mr. Lloyd George's Act would go down to posterity as a great Radical measure, and one which the Conservatives were arraid to tackle. The enormously-improved Employers' Liability Act of the Liberal Government paid twice as much to injured workmen as was the case before. and he asked for .the hearty support of the peopie as recomperso for the great work done for the crosses. RUNCORN COUNTY SLCONDARY SCHOOL SPORTS.</p>
        <p>These animal sports took place on the school cround od Saturday afternoon. Results : Throwing Cricket Ball.—Junior3 : Duckett, 1, Aspen 2, 53yds. 2ft. 4in. Seniors: Youd, 1, 73yds. Gin. Long Jump.—Juniors: Clarke, 1; Aspey, 2. Seniors: Youd, 1 ; Vernon. 2. High Jump.— Girls: C. Hulse, 1; D. Harm:Tar, 2. Junior Boys: Wright, minor, 1. Senior Boys: Bunce,l. 100 Yards.—Junior Girls: D. Temperley, 1; A. Cul- bane, 2. Senior Girls: 0. Hough, 1 ; M. Gleave, 2; E. Duncalf, 3. Junior Boys: Wright, minor, 1; Jones, minor, 2; Clarke, 3. Boys (open): Youd, 1; Yernon,2; Bunco, 3. Skipping Race.—Junior Girls: D. Temrorley, 1; L. Wood, 2. Senior Girls: F. Roberts, 1; D. Harman 0n,2. Quarter Mile (Boy s).—Youd: scr., 1; Bunce,l2yds., 2 ; Oakes, 26yds. 3. School Handicap.—Vernon (7), 1; Youd (scr.), 2; Wrigbt, minor (30), 3. • Sacondary School Invitation 100 Yards.—Ern Swinton (Widnes), 1; I'. Pennington (Widnes), 2. 220 Yards. Boys (Open).—Oakes (16), 1; Thomrs (20), 2; Bunco (5), 3. Elemen'ary School Invitation 100 Yards.—Boys Leonard Pilgrim Balfour-read. Girls: Ruth Pendle- burr Balfour-road. (60), 1 ; Buxton (55), 2: Clarke (50), 3, The first pre in this was a silver cup, pre- sented by Mr. Horace W. Handley. Hurdle Races.—Jur. iors : Wright, lyd.. 1 ; Ind, 2yds.' 2. Seniors: Bunce (2), 1; Vernon (I); 2; Orkes (4), 3. Obstacle Races.—Girls D. Moody, 1; D. Woodier, 2. Junior Boys: Moore.; 1; Ind, 2. Senior Boys: Bunce 1; Collins, 2. . Dribbling Hockey Ball.—Junior Girls: E. Davenport, 1; A. Broad, 2. Senior Girls: D. lElanamm 1• I. Johnson, 2. Old Runcornians' 100 Yards.—Brown, Winsford. Winsford district won the school championship for wirning the most events, and were awarded the head master's (Mr. Gledhill) silver cup. Mrs. Boston, of the Grange, distributed the prizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS PROVIDENCE FREE HOSPITAL Mr. J. B. Leach, the chairman of this institution, ha received an -intimation from Mr. Samuel Robinson, managing director of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Company, Limited, St. Helens, that his company will be pleased to give .050 towards the extension scheme at present in progress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>"BLACK MARIA'S FASCINATION.</head>
        <p>"BLACK MARIA'S FASCINATION.</p>
        <p>James Paget, Lyon-street, when charged at St. Helens on Tuesriay with assaulting Police-constable Adams, excused hin self by stating that he was attracted to the railway station by the "Black Maria," and wanted to see the prisoners zoing away to Walton.—Tbe constable said that a gang o'f young roughs usually went to the station, and he was on duty in consequence of com- plaints from the railway officials. The group of roughs dispeised when he ordered them away, hut Paget stood his ground and put himself in a fighting attitude, after- wards striking, kicking, and Lutting him. A young man named "Patlock assisted the constable, and was kicked by Paget.—Pr'soner said We officer brought it all on himself. He only wanted to see the prisoners.— Paget was sent to prison for 25 days.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND THE MINERS' EIGHT HOURS BILL MEN ADVISED TO RESIST THE MASTERS.</p>
        <p>11Ir. T. Glover, M.P., the miners' repre,entative, on Monday night addressed a couple of opea-air meetings at NewtoNN.l aid 1 hatto Heath.</p>
        <p>Spraking at Newtown, Mr. Glover dealt chiefly with the Budget and with the Miners' Eight Hours Bill. He welcomed the passing of the bill after th;rty years of fighting for it, and said it was not until the miners had their own represent%tives in the House of Commons that the bill was raesed. It was not what they wanted. They wanted an hours eay from bank to bank, whereas what they had got was an eight hours day underground, time being allowed for the men to go down the pit and come up. I n the new Act there was also provision that on sixty eays of the year an extra hour could be worked. There was an understanding between the employers Pnd the feeetation that that should not be done at</p>
        <p>nreseut. He was informed, however, that at the St</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>11-lens collieries the men were forced to work the extra kour on Friday last. He hoped that if the men were required to do this nest Friday they would cease work and do nothing after the usual hour had elapsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S NEW AUSTRALIAN</p>
        <p>PLAYERS.</p>
        <p>As announced in our last issue the Warrington Club has gone far afield in its search for " new blood,'' and great satisfaction is felt locally in the success which has attended the negotiations between the Club and tw oof last season's Australian team, O'Malby (fin ward), and Frawley (ihreequarter) whose photographs we reproduce below. Both men are expected to have left New South *Wales by the time these lines appear, and be on their way to Wat rington.</p>
        <p>L. O'MALLEY was certainly the star forward of the Kangaroos. Though oa the light side, he is Very fast ' and clever in the open and scored many tries dm ing the tour. He is 25 years old, sft. 10;in. tall, and 11 stones 5 lbs. in weight.</p>
