      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Northern ,Rigby Union Meeting.</head>
        <p>Northern ,Rigby Union Meeting.</p>
        <p>. _ _ At a meeting of the general committee of the Northern Rugby Union, held at Huddersfield on Tueaday night, the follovting officials were oppointed for the next season :—Coairrnan, Mr. J. H. Houghton, St Helens; vice-chairman, Mr. J. W. Wood, Lc 3ds ; treasurer, Mr. J. Lewthwaite, Hunslet ; sczretary, J. Platt, Oldharr The draw for the qullif3 ins round resulted as follows :</p>
        <p>District I.—Millom v. Blitow St Gegfge's; Bro)k- -lands Rovers, bye. _ _ .</p>
        <p>District 2.L-York Groves United v. Hull SA nite Rose York v. Beverley. District 3.—Wot Lley Sh.cninkley. . K:rkstall Forge v. Bramley.</p>
        <p>District 4.—R;strick v. Streethouse Red Rose Furst on White Horse v. Featherstone Rovers, Normanton V. Ccariston ROWEL', Outwo2d Caurch v. Normanton St. John's.</p>
        <p>District s.—Heyside v. Salford St. Bartholomew, Radcliffe Rangers v. Pemt nton, Wigan Highfield V. Egerton. Byes—Leigh Shanarock,, Ince, Cadishe,ad and Irian; Swinton Hotaets, and Wardle Lane. District 6.—Aberdare, a bye. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The venues of the international matches with Wales were fixed as follows :—Englaid v. Wales, at Iludder6- field ; Wales v. England, at Merthyr or Mid-lthondds The date of the semi-Paal of the cup competition as altered to April .2nd next. The sub-committee appointed to interview the mem- bers of the Lancashire Football Association at Man- chester, submitted a report, wl“ch stated that, Mr. Lewis, on behalf of the Lancashire Football Association stated they had had complaints that rq antagonistic) Spirit uas being khos‘n towards clubs playing their code as applications for the use of Northern Union grounds were being refused, it being stated by the Union clubs that the Union did not, allow Association matches to be playt I on Northern Union grounds. The Union representatives replied that the clubs referred to were labotiting t'nder a mistake, as power to let or refuse the loan of their grounds was a question for the di cision of the clubs concerned aid they alone, and in this they did not differ from the position taken up by the Associatlon clubs.</p>
        <p>A letter %yes received from the English Football Arsoc:at ion in respect of the proposer] taxation of bind values and the position of football clubs relitive thereto. It was decided to co-ol )rate with the Foot- ball Association in any action that may be taken Nsi.h regard to the matte: if the new tax affects Vorthern Union clubs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>LANCASHIRE CHIEF CONSTABLE RESIGNS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CHIEF CONSTABLE RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant .Coionel Henry Martin Moorkom, M.V.0., Chief Constable of the Lancashire County Police, has resigned that appointment, which he has held for the prat twenty-r'ne years. No step will be taken to fill the rarancV until the September meeting of the Stand- ing Joint (;ommittee for the county. At a meeting of the Standing Joint Committ?e, held at tie Couniy Offices, Preston, on Thursday morning, the resignation was received and accepted with regret. For thirty-thre3 years Lieut.-Colonel Moorsom served in the Lancashire Constabulaty, txenty-nine of which</p>
        <p>During tl'e evening an admirable musical programme was submitted. Dm Aprllo Quartette (Messrs. Cook, Critchley, Woodcock and Gittens) gave Several choice selections and songs were interpreted in pleasing style by Bros.A.Goulden J. Jackson, jun., au(' Mr.W.H.lreland. Mr. W. Whittle created much n:erriment with his humorous sketches. Mr. G. H. Anderton played the pianoforte accompaniments very tastefully.</p>
      </div>
