      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Police Court and Prison Gate Mission.</head>
        <p>Police Court and Prison Gate Mission.</p>
        <p>The good work which is being done by the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission of the Church of England Temperance Society was ably outlined at a drawing-room meeting held at the Winmarleigh Cafj, Sankey-street, on Tuesday afternoon, at the invitation. of the Mayor and Mayoress (P iderman and Mrs. Forshaw). There was an encouraging attendance,. the clergy present being the I'm. Archdeacon Madden, the Revs. P. W. Pheysey (diocesan secretary), L. J. T. Darwall, B. Percival, G. C. Briggs, E. E. Peters, and T. A. J. Ridpath.—The Mayor, who presided, said they had met to encourage and support the objects of the mission, which were very noble and good—the uplift7n.7 of the downcast and the raising of the fallen. Their local Police Court Missionary, Mr. J. J. Firth, was doing a good work, and the mission deserved their heartiest support and encouragement. (Applause).—Archdeacon Madden in the course of a powerful address, said that they were strengthening their army and adding to the nltmber of their Dreadnoughts, it must be remembered that no great people, no great Empire had continued to exist except in character. The foundation of a- country was sobriety, thrift and godliness. (Applause). As Mr. Gladstone once said, it was. the businegs of the legislature to make it hard for the people to do wrong. (Applause).—The local side of the work as interestingly dealt with by Mr. W. Furness, one of the borough magis- trates, and Mr. Firth, the missioner,—The Rev. P. W. Pheysey also addressed the meeting, and a hearty vote- of thanks was accorded to the Mayor for presiding, on the motion of the Rev. B. Percival, seconded by the Rev. E. E. Peters.</p>
      </div>
