      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>NEW COUNTY MAGISTRATES WHO SHALL BE ALDERMAN? LOCAL APPOINTMENTS. LIVELY DISCUSSION ON SENIORITY. It was announced on Thursday that the names of Town Council Makes Another Change thirty-three gentlemen had been placed on the Corn- At the meeting of the Town Council on Tueefly mission of the Peace for the County of Chester. The Councillor WILKINSON moved that the following Stand- local appointments are : log Order be rescinded—" 52 (1) For the purnose of Thomas Baxter, Hill Cliffe, Warrington, retired determining the senioety of members of the Council, farmer. with a view to the aPotment of their sea's In the William Fletcher, Spring View, Higher Whitley, Council Chamber and for other pu.Nses, a list shall be Warrington, iron manufacturer. - • prepared.by the Town Clerk in which shall be inserted in the firgt place the names of the several Aldermen in the or&amp; rof their respective periods of tenure of that office,. and in the next place the names of the several Councillors in the order of their respective periods of tenure of the office of Councillors, provided that the Deputy-Mayor shall in all cases take priority nest after the Mayor," and thret the following one be substituted therefor :—" 52. (1) For the purpose of determining the seniority of members of the Council with a view te the allotment of their seats in the Council Chamber, and for other purposes, a list shall he prepared by the Town Clerk in which shall be inserted in the first place the names of the several Aldermen in ordcr of their respective periods of continuous and unint rrupted tenure of that office • and in the next plaee the names of the several Councillors in the order of their respec- tive periods of continuous and uninter.rupted terure of the office of Councillor, but any interval not exceetding fifteen months in dureticn shall not he cons'dcred to be an interruption, provided that the Deputy-Mayor shall in all pates take priority next after the Mayor." - COUNCILLOR WILKINSON'S MOTION. Councillor WILKINSON said he thought the resolution would commend itself to the mrjor:ty of the Council if not to all. Several members would remember tho circumstances which led to the pasiug of the present Standing Order. He appealed to the Council to reAore a Standing Order which for many years acted so admir- ably and was only alte.ed to meet a case which in all human probability might never cccur nib]. lie wished to call attention to the fact that histcry aeain repeated itself, for it was on such a Standing Order that he had now the pleasnre of bringing before the Council, that his old friend Councillor Henry Roberts was in the year 1898 elected to the Aldermanic Bench. He well remem- bered the well-chosen words that were then u-el by Mr. Roberts, who said:—"l stand to-night in this Ceuncil as a result of the acceptance of that prineiple. I am proud of the fact and I feel in honour bound to respect it on every occasion it is brought before the Council." That aldermanic seat was occupied by Alderman Roberts until the year 1902, when he so honourably vacated the exalted position. It Was then thought by mem- bers of the Council that the time had arrived when some consideration ought to be given to the case of then Councillor Pierroint. He was first elected to the Council in the year 1882, and sat continuously entil 1891—a per:od of nine years—when hesuffered a defeat in Town Hall Ward. He again returned to the Council in 1891, which positicn he occupied until 1903, he then having a second term of nine years' continuous and uninterrupted service to his credit, when it was felt that be ought to be made analderman, but-he was defeated. In the June following several members thought that the rule that debarred him should be set eside, and in November the same year be was elected as an alderman, having been a member with only one break for, a period of 21 ,s ears. At the time it was thought right to waive the st.nding order. Alderman Pierpoint bad again to his credit nine years continuous service, and was second in the order of seniority, being only two months beh'nd the senior memter. .\-Vas it fair that members who left the Council for a period of years and returned should immediately claim priority over those who had years of unbroken service to their credit ? The motion safeguarded the interest of the man who might be unfortunately defeated at a November election, but who secured election in the succeeding year. The break in the continuity of his service was not serious' and he did net get out of touch with the work of the Council. Under the existing Standing Order it would be possible for a man who bad been out of the Council eight or ten years to come bairk and almost immediately secure an alder- manic seat, and probably thereby the chairman,hip of a committee, the work of which he knew absolutely , nothing about, end the best interests of the town would seriously suffer. ' Councillor FuriroaeSMlTH seconded. Councillor ROGERS sdd be was more than surprised at Councillor Wilkinson's action. Some years ago the Standing Order was altered to elevate the then Councilor Pierpoint and rightly so after giving the town nine years' service and, after a short break, a further nine years' service. If the Standing Order bad been altered sooner he would have been an alderman sooner. Ile (Councillor Rogers) after nine years' service was turned out of the Council. He endeavoured to serve the rate- payers honourably. He d;d not Fay he was one of the most enlightened members of the Council, but he was as honest yrobably as any councillor sitting round the table. After three years' retirement, he was returned. Councillor Wilkinson was the first, to get up in the Council and oppose him being put in his proper position according to his previous service. When the matter was explained the Council thought he (Councillor -Rogers) was entitled to have nine years' service credited to him and that placed him senior to Councillor Wilkinson by one. Had Councillor Wilkinson's name been in front of his (Councillor Rogers') they would have heard nothing of this. The old rule had been introduced to try to prevent rolitical bickerings in the Council. It bad worked well and he hoped it would be continued. There.was no man in the Council who had had to fight for his position like he (Councillor Rogers) had, and he thought it was unjust of Councillor Wilkin- son to try to throw him out of his position. He was not wanting dead men's shoes; he wasperfectly willing to wait for his turn. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS lIAS A TILT AT THE "EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>Birtles.</p>
        <p>Mouil d: Morrison (Liverpool.)</p>
        <p>Councillor HENRY ROBERTS said he thought it was due to himself that he should pet his position fully and frankly before the Council. He was not sorry to have the opportunity of putting a curious inaccuracy right. iv It as ststed n the "Warrington Examiner" of Saturday last, ord he rather fended from the remarks of Courcillor Wilkinson that he had allowed himself to some extent to be guided in his action by the same inaccnracy—he (Councillor Roberts) would not use any stronger langi;age, because he wanted, as far as he could, to conduct the debate, so far as his int-rvention in it was concerned, calmly and quietly and with due regard to facts and fair play. The writer of the little Ileaderette in the f• Examiner" had discovered, on an absolutely illusory condition of fact, what he called a " Gilbertian situation" of humour. If the editor had bad the same desire to put before his readers a true statement of fact in regard to this matter as be had to claim some reputition for a spirit of humour, he would never have written that article. The article made it out—and he knew it had been the imprtssion which several people bad held, because he had had to contra- dict it before—that the old Standing Order enabled him (Councillor Roberts) to profit by being elected an alderman over the head of his respected friend, Alder- man Pierpoint. When he was elected an alderman be was twelve months senior to Councillor Pierpoint in regard to service. Councillor Pierpoint was first elected to the Council in November, 1882, and was defeated by Alderman Smethurst in November, 1891. He had up to that date nine years' service. lie was elected a second time in November, 1894. He (Coun- cillor Roberts) was elected to the Council in November. 1884, and was elected an alderman on May 10th, 1898. At that time he had thirteen years and six months' uninterrupted service, while Councillor Pierpoint's two periods of service amounted to twelve years and six months. So that, so far as the old Standing Order was concerned, it did not benefit him (Councillor Roberts) in the slightest degree. Either the old Standing Order or the present Standing Order would have elected him alder- man as the senior member of the Council. He would like to say at this moment that he claimed it had been possible for him, although he had been out of the Council for six years, to keep himself sufficiently in touch with municipal work and affairs as to make it not quite accurate to suggest that he Ras a stranger to either one or the other on re-enteling the Council on that occasion, tie objected to Councillor Wilkinson's motion. "(1) Because the Council has much more important work to do than to waste time in discussing a proposition which is opposed to the spirit of fair play, which is supposed to be the special characteristic of every Englishman. (2) Because if carried it is bound to intensify THAT BITTER PERSONAL FEELING which has been the bane of this Council far too long, and which every true lover of the town's good name will do his utmost to eliminate from the future deliberations of this Corporation. (3) In view of the fact that while there is no vacancy on the aldermanic bench at present, the common knowledge that at this moment two of our most respected and ablest alder- men have been stricken down with serious illness makes a discussion of this nature, in my opinion, positively indecent." The motion was aimed at two members of the Council—Councillor Rogers and himself, and it was to say t'e lea t of it a very curious coincidence that Councillor Wilkinson should hive selected the fli.st moment of his re-anpear- ance in the Council fur what, on the face of it, !voiced very much like a personal attack. Whatever lack of regard was shown to him in this matter, at least Cowl: cillor Wilkinson might have had sufficient regard, for the feelings of the families of the gentlemen he bad already alluded to to prevent him at this stige of events from putting such a proposition on the agenda. ' SHAKESPEARE QUOTED AT COUNCILLOR WILKINSON. He would remind Councillor Wilkinson of the rebuke administered by Henry IV. to his impatient son who, when his father lay a-dying and supposed to be asleep, and the son was left in charge of bim, stole the crown and put it on his he :d in the hope that he might some day wear it. On discovering the fact the King addressed him thus : " Dost thou so hunger for this empty place That thou, wilt needs invest thee with another's honours Before thy hour be ripe ?" He (Councillor Roberts) left his friend Councillor Wilkinson to reflect upon that. Councillor WILKINSON: He bath done. (Laughter.) Councillor ROBERTS: Well, I hope he has. Con- tinuing he said the resolution had been pretty freely discusad by the public of Warrington and there was</p>
        <p>MR. WILLIAM FLETCHER, J.P,</p>
        <p>Mr. William Fletcher is the only son of the late Mr. Richard Fletcher, of the firm of William and Richard Fletcher, builders, stonemasons and quarry masters, of Warrington, who, it is interesting to note, built Walton Hall and worked the stone quarry at Appleton. He was born at Latchford and educated at 'People's College, then known as Newton-street School, situated off Winwick-street, Mr. Brewtnall being the headmaster. Mr. Fletcher was connected with the firm of Messrs. F. Monks and Co., iron manufacturers, from the commencement, and when it was converted into a limited company under the name of Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd., in 1878, he became a director and has held that office ever since. He is the chair- man of Messrs. J. M. Greenhalgh and Co., cabinet makers, Warrington, a director of Messrs. Taylor's, Ltd., disinfectant manufacturers, Liverpool, chairman of Messrs. John Buckley and Co., Ltd., contractors, Warrington, and director of the Land Company, Ltd., Warrington. Mr. Fletcher was very successful in business as a draper in Bridge-streb, Warrington, for eighteen years. He has lived in his present picturesquely situated residence at Whitley for the past thirty-five years and takes a great pride in his beautiful garden. Mr. Fletcher has been a. devoted public servant in many capacities. He was a member of the united district of the Whitley School Board for eighteen years and he represented the parishes of Seven Oaks and Higher Walton on the -Runcorn Rural District Council and Board of Guardians for several years, during which he acted as Chairinan of the House Committee. Mr. Fletcher is a member of the Education Administrative Sub- committee for the Frodsham district and has occupied most positions open to a public man in a rural dis- riot, including those of overseer and way warden. He takes considerable interest in agricultural matters and was for many years a member of the Royal Agricul- tural Society. He is at present connected with the 'Warrington and District and. Wirral Agricultural Societies. Mr. Fletcher is a Conservative and a Church- man. His wife is a daughter of the late Alderman John Burgess, a former Mayor of Warrington.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad M 133 never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WritTa &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw st., opposite top of Bold et., Liverpool.</p>
