      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>11IR. THOMAS BAXTER, J.P.</head>
        <p>11IR. THOMAS BAXTER, J.P.</p>
        <p>Photo.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Baxter, is the son of Mr. William Baxter, farmer, of Walton, and was born in that village in 1838. He -first attended Daresbury school and received further instruction at a private school in Cairo-street, Warrington, kept by Mr. Savage. All his life Mr. Baxter has been closely-identified with farming and has done a great deal to encourage the agricultural industry in his native county. For twenty- three years he kept a farm at Ashton Hayes and for seventeen years he lived at Dutton Hall and farmed with great success. Five years ago he retired and came to reside at The Rock, Hill Cliffe, Warrington. 21r. Baxter commenced public life early as a collector of rates and taxes. In several important capacities he has rendered valuable services, and he enjoys the confidence and regard of all whom he has represented. For seventeen years he has been a County Councillor for the Frodsham district and he is also a member of the Cheshire County Education Committee. Mr. Baxter has long represented Dutton on the Runcorn Rural District Council and Runcorn Board of Guardians, and The present is his third year as chairman of the Guardians. He also holds the office of chairman of the Old Age Pensions Committee for the Budworth district. Mr. Baxter is chair- man and director of the firm of Gartons Ltd., seedsmen, Warrington, and also of the Runcorn Bone Works Company. ..He is a member of the Cheshire Chamber of Agriculture, the Milk Producers' Associa- lion, the Cheshire Agricultural Society, and the Warrington and District Agricultural Soc iety.Mr.Baxter has had great experience as a valuer and his services are also frequently in request at agricultural shows as a judge ,of produce. He is a Liberal Unionist and a Churchman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TRAMWAYS.</head>
        <p>THE TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>A Record Weeks Traffic.</p>
        <p>The following are the returns of traffic on the War- fington tramways for the week ending July Bth : No. of Receipt". Rec'ts per Route. Pass'grs s. d. Mirge Car 'mile. Sankey 25,936 97 5 9 1876 12.45 pence. Latchford 38,516 143 16 2 2306 11.96 „ Stockton 36,870 161 3 8' 2072 18'67 „ Cemetery 39,715 151 3 6 1660 2185 „ Longford</p>
        <p>- -- -- 150,014 587 11 8 8866 159</p>
        <p>The receipts show an increase over the previons bighest week since the opening of the tramways of £133.</p>
        <p>Off-licencebolders' Picnic.</p>
        <p>The annual trip of the Warrington and District Off Licence holders Association took place on Thursday, to Chester, Famdon, Itossett and ' Gresford. About 41 members and friends went by train to Chester,and then boarded motors for a drive through charming country to the places named, the far-famed strawberry gardens at Farndon being visited. The beautiful weather that prevailed greatly added to the pleasure of the trippers. Dinner was partaken of at Parndon and tea was enjoyed at Chester. Warrington was reached about 9.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 17, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>only one opinion about it—that it was both extremely unfair in its proposal and extremely inopportune in the time in which it was brought forward. The present Standing Order was passed in 1903 to remedy an admitted injustice to Alderman Pierpoint, and C oun- cillor Wilkinson voted for it because he believed it to be fair, honest and honourable. C ,uncillor Wilkinson now asked them to recreate a similar injustice to two members of the Council. The Council, for the sake of its %111 honour and credit, could not afford to blow hot and cold upon a que3tion of principle such as was involved in the resolution. He (Councillor Roberts)</p>
        <p>DID NOT c9ME INV) VIZ COUNCIL TO BE MADE AN</p>
        <p>ALDERII</p>
        <p>but to serve his town according to the best of his ability. He valued the intimate touch which an elected member of the Council had or ought to have ' with his constituents, and he vionld only accept an honour at the halals of the Council if it were the pre- ponderating feeling of the large majority of its mem- bers that for services rendered he should receive that recognition. They were supposed to elect Aldermen for services rendered. He ventured to suggest that the • services of Councillor Rogers would bear favourable comparison with the services of Councillor Wilkioscn —at eny rate he had rendered services over a much, longer period—and it would have been far better if Councillor Wilkinson had let some other memter :of the Corporation bring forward a proposition of that character rather than put himself. in the, invidious position of practically claiming for himself at the expense of other men a reward 'for services which, at any rate for length of time, did not compare with either one or the other. With regard to his (Councillor RoberLs') own services, they were not limited to that Corporation and they had extended, in regard to the Corperati n, over twice the period of Ceuncillor Wilieinson'e. He did not venture to suggest that his (Conn 41:or Roberts') services were fit to rank against Councillor Wilkinson's in point of ability and time, and everything that went te make a valuable Councillor. He w‘es quite prepared to aimit that what Conneillor Wilkinson hid done for Warrington might outweigh anything that be (emincillor Roberts) ever bad done ot could possibly di. At the same time the Council had not discriminated too carefully on points of difference of that kind, but-had gone on the principle of services over a period of years. If they said that services should be recognized on their quality, be ventured t submit that his services would be entitled to some consideration compared with Cmincillor Wilkinson's. If the Council chose to go back again to a state of things which made the Council an extremely unpleasant place whenever the election of an alderman occurred, by all means let it do so, but let it do so with the full knowledge that the responsibility for any personal feeling which it might introduce would rest on its own shoulders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SIX TEARS LAPSE TOO LONG.</head>
        <p>SIX TEARS LAPSE TOO LONG.</p>
        <p>Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said that all along he had been against seniority. It vk as a pity seniority was the only qualification they could recognize for promotion. A lapse of six years was certainly too long to count previous service. If c )uncillors could return to the Council after an indefinite absence and claim preferment he (Dr. Joseph) would remain a councillor until he was as old as Methusaleh. He would support the resolution with more readiness if it were even stronger. Councillor BROADH UR ST said that as a strong admirer of Councillor Henry Roberts, after listening to his appeal for fair play, he felt somewhat surprised that Councillor Roberts did not appeal to the electorate of Whitecross Ward six years ago. Unfortunately Mr. Roberts did not think the time was opportune for him to do tbat.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: You are wandering from the special motion.</p>
        <p>Councillor BROADHURST : lam sticking exactly to the point. Councillcir Roberts has been appealing for justica and fair play. He spoke of the opinions of %%Torrington and if at that time he thought he was justifieJ, ahy did he not appeal to the electorate to return him to the Council six years ago and not come now.- _</p>
        <p>The 31Avon. :—You are wandering from, the subjent- of the motion.</p>
        <p>--60-t-MCiiiO-r-bROADHITRST : I am sorry you allow Councillor Roberts to travel over all this space and then stop m. Continuing, he said that if they allowed the whole of the gentlemen who retired six years ago to return to the Cooncil, and the whole period of past ser- vice t.) be counted for seniority in the future, in his opinkn they would be creating an injustice to every member wit:, sat round that table. He should support the resolution and he admired the pluck and courne Councillor Wilkinson had di played in bringing it for- ward.</p>
        <p>"THE SEE-SAW."</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT congratulated Councillor Roberts on coming back to the Council under very favnurable auspices. They welcomed him back to the fighting line which he adorned torso many years and in which he (Alderman Bennett) was sure he would render equal service in the future. He, however, thought they had made a great mistake in discussing " Roberts " or " Rogers, ' or any other gentleman. No names should be introduced. It was not cignifled that the personal equation should enter into matters of such importance. He thought the Standing Order as it stood at present was the fairest which had yet been found. It was a distinct hardship that a man who had served the best part of his life should lose his position, and all his years' work be absolutely forgotten and w iced cut through having been out of the Council for a short time. Something might be said for a time limit but it, ought to be much larger than Councillor Wilkinson proposed. He deprecated the continual tendency of the Council to wobble and change its mind. He some- times wonckred whether they were a Corporation or a "merry-go-ronnd " (Laughter). Alderman TINNION : A see-saw. (Renewed laughter). Alderman BENNETT : It certainly is a see-saw and many other things I scarcely dare put into English. (Laughter). Continuing, he said they ought to have a definite policy and not keep changing their Standing Orders. They were making themselves a laughing stock to the town.</p>
        <p>. _ Alderman BOLTON said Alderman Bennett wobbled so °fun that he (Alderman Bolton) as not surprised that he should think everybody else did so. (Laughter.) With regard to the motion, he considered that when a man had served his Council for a sufficient number of ,vears,he had no right to expect a chance of promotion. It was, therefore, too bad to have a man coming into the Council and stepping over them and taking away the plums which they expected to get. Councillor ROBERTS: I rise to a point of order. I never such suggestion grestion in any speech of mine. ImAladkee m rmnflodaen claimany BOLTON:whatever. Alderman Where is the point of order? Councillor ROBERTS: There. You are making mis- statements of facts. Alderman BOLTON (to the Mayor) : I think he might wait till I have finistind. He has had his speech and 1 i hope you will keep him n order. This sort of thing used to occur frequently a dozen years ago and I hope it will not occur again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SENIORITY NEVER RECOGNISED.</head>
        <p>SENIORITY NEVER RECOGNISED.</p>
        <p>Alderman TM.iros' said be regrettei the spirit and the tone which the dirusbion had taken and he could not congratulate Councillor Roberts on the line he had adopted. Councillor Roberts had had considerable experience in and out of the Council and he (the speaker) suggested that he should not have spoken in such a personal manner. Lie (Alderman Tinnion) was the proposer-of the Standing Orzler as it btood arm. He would, therefore, be compelled to vote against the motion, but if a motion were brought forward to wipe out the whole business, be would vote for it. The question of seniority, be contended, hal never been recognised except in the case of Alderman James Evans. lie (Alderman Tinnion) had had to fight f.,r alderman. ship. The wh,le thing had been continuilly violated. Men had given their pledges and delib3rately violated them and he hopel that if they had any men of that kind left, they would be protected from themselves and that the question of seniority would b 3 swept away altogether. Councillor FULTON-SMITH said that when he seconded the resolution he had hoped tLat the discussion would have been of an academical character. instead of that it had drifted into personllities—a thing he dreaded most in those meetings.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD id he supported the Standing Order as it stood and he could not blow hot and cold. Unless Le had more information supplied than be had at present he should support the existing Standing Order</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EDITORS NOT INFALLIBLE.</head>
        <p>EDITORS NOT INFALLIBLE.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON, in replying on the discussion, said that with reference to Councillor Roberts's remarks as ta the inaccuracy in the " Examiner " article, he (Councillor Wilkinson) had nothing to do with thtt and he was sorry the time of the Council was wasted in that way. Editors were no more infallible than other gentlemen, and they were liable to make mistakes. He agreed there was an error as pointed out by Councillor Roberts and that Councillor Roberts was Alderman Pierpoint's senior by twelve months. As to Henry IV's rebuke to his son, they must remember that the son was after the crown which his fattier possessed. John Wilkinson to-day was try ng to stop two men from taking the crown he was entitled to. (Laughter). Nothing would give him greater pleasure than to see Conncillors Roberts and Rogers aldermen of the borough, if they would take their proper order. Councillor Roberts contendEd ti at the new Standing Order would intensify bitter feelino. What were his remarks on his election in 1898 :—"lf anything could add to the pleasure which I feel in connection with it, it is that we have vindicated the principle of seniority which I firmly believe will do more than any- thing else to prevent a revival of party strife and feel- ing in the Council." How was he going to reconcile his statement that the proposed new Standing Order would create strife when in days gone by he said a Standing Order which specified continuous and uninterrupted service would stop bitter feelings? Councillor ROBERTS: I said the principle of seniority, I did not say anything about uninterrupted service. Councillor WILKINSON, continuing, said the danger they had to face was that if six or seven Councillors who had raTred were to return and count all their back periods of service, the other members would all be pushed back. The voting was as follows : Fon : AGAINST:— Alderman Bolton. . Alderman Bennett _</p>
        <p>Coun. Fulton-Smith „ Barton „ Wilkinson</p>
        <p>„ Tinnion Coun. ff. Roberts „ Singleton</p>
      </div>
