      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The ever popular and romantic equestrian spectacle " Turpin's Ride to York" is very ably pourtrayed by Mr. Fred Ginnett's Company at the above place of amusement this week. Dick Turpin, the once notorious highwayman, who died by the hangman's rope in 1737, has an excellent double in Mr. George Jennings, who brings moisture to the eyes of the majority of the audience in the last act—that of parting with his favourite, Black Bess, a mile from his destination—York. The sketch also shows the shoot- ing of his friend and. colleague, Tom King, when he had fallen in the hands of the" beaks,' rather than allow him to die on the scaffold ; the memorable jump over the six-barred toll-house gate at Stamford Bridge. Black Bess, a fine black mare, is wonderfully trained, ilnd earns rounds of applause for its almost human performance. The staging and the accurate and elaborate costumes of the period are much admired. Carl Lynn well earns his title of the king of animal mimics. The familiar cries of farmyard dwellers are imitated to perfection, and that of the, lion, done by means of the mouth add lamp glass, make one fancy the jungle is at hand. The repre- sentation of the gramophone is also perfect. A laughable twenty minutes sketch, given by Ginnett's Rejected Remounts shows a retired farmer attempting to take riding exercise on spirited horses and finally wrecking his house and home. Leo Fields proves a favourite with his amusing parodies and fastidious ways. Lona presents a charming and refined musical act, and is exceedingly clever on the violin and one- stringed fiddle. He introduces a new feature that of playing a popular air on his violin and thncing a difficult step at the same time. Queenie West takes well as a comedienne and dancer and Rose Cass presents a pathetic two verses song. The Palace Perfection Pictures are splendid, presenting a highly dramatic picture, entitled " Sacrifice'of a young girl'' and a side-splitting one entitled" Hiccoughs. The orchestra under the baton of Mr. E. Widdop leaves nothing to be desired. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week's bill is topped by Harry Levarne, the famous comedian, and the other attractions will be : —James and Jenny Jee, champion tight wire act of the world ; Cliffon and Gibson, comedians in song, patter and dance, Marion Scott, character vocalist ; the Wedbums, comedians, vocalists and instrumen- talists ; Albert Tdwnsend, society entertainer and mimic ; Rose and Challoner, " the porter and th e singer."</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Way of tile World." This melodrama of sporting and circus life has proved—a popular attraction at the Rylands-street house, its many excising incidents being followed with tense interest, while the humorous touches are cordially welcomed. Messrs. Herbert Skardon and H. Sherwood Martin's Company gives an admirable performance and the play is splendidly mounted. Chief honours go to Miss Marie. Robson who, in the unpopular part of Lady Adeli Hardy, displays talent of a high order and gives a most realistic presentment. She i has a faithful accomplice n Captain Fred Slade, ably impersonated by Mr. Victor Gordon. Mr. Stenson Liddard wins great favour as Cecil Hardy and the humorous side of the piece is well undertaken by Miss ViOlette Vivienne as Polly Bell, "the strong woman," Mr. F. Etheridge as Horatio Bluff, and Mr. D'Arcy Kilway as ~,Joe Sparrow, the clown. The orchestra, under the cc nductorship of Mr. Norman Latham, plays some e#ellent selections, one of the most acceptable being , the "Poet and Peasant" overture. NEXT WEEK'S__PROGRA.MME.</p>
        <p>_ A complete change will be provided next week, when the programme will consist chiefly of a splendid selection of animated pictures. The management have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>MARRIED LADY TEACHERS.</head>
        <p>MARRIED LADY TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>NOTICES WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>A. sudden and surprising change in the situation arising out of the Warrington Education Committee's recently expressed determination to dismiss the married lady teachers in their employ became known on Thursday.. It will be . remembered that the com- mittee gave three months' notice to all the married lady teachers in their employ, but subsequently they made exceptions in the cases of several teachers who were able to adduce special reasons for the con- tinuance of their services. The National Union of Teachers took up the matter on behalf of two of their members—Mrs. Stansfield and Mrs. Grounds—who had rece:ved notice. and an application for an interlocutory injunction to restrain the committee from giving effect to the notices pending the trial of an action for a permanent injunction, was down for hearing in the Chancery _Division yesterday. The members of the Education Comuittee were summoned to a speCial and.private meeting which was held on Wednesday afternoon. Councillor J. Fulton- Smith presided over a small attendance. The Law Clerk (Mr.J.Lyon Whittle), who had taken counsel's opinion, submitted a report and in consequence the committee decided to withdraw the notices to the two ladies con- cerned. Letters intimating that this course had been taken were sent out the same evening. What Will be done with regard to the other married lady teachers who received notice has not yet been decided. The situation is rendered additionally interesting by the fact that several of the teachers, on hearing of the committee's intention to dispense with their services, voluntarily sent in their resignations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>LEGAL PROCEEDINGS</head>
        <p>THE LEGAL PROCEEDINGS</p>
        <p>In the Chancery Division, yesterday, before Mr. .Thstice Eve, the actietns of Mrs. Stansfleld and Mrs. Grounds v. the Education Committee of the War- rington Corporation were mention3d by Sir It. Finlay, for the plaintiffs. Sir R. Finlay applied for an interlocu' ory injunction restrsining the Corporation from proceeding upon a notice discharg:ng Mrs. Stansfield from-. her post as teacher in the defenda Its' school at Warrington. Mr. Sargent said he appeared in both actions for the Corporation. His learned friend was no doubt aware that the notices sent to the plaintiffs hid been with- drawn. Under these circumstances, he would suggest that the only order to be made on the motion should be costs in the a3tion. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Sir It. Finlay thought his learned friend ought to give an,undertaking that no other notice of dismissal would he served, pending the hearing of the action. Mr. Sargent FAA he could not do that. The Town Clerk assured him that there was no intention, or threat of giving another notice. There might be a further question which could not be determined that day, as to whether the plaintiffs had any right to sue, but for the moment the notices had been withdrawn, and there was no intention ol renewing them. Si R, Finlay asked for more than that. Mrs. Stans- Beld, be said, was dismisse lon the ground that she was a married woman, in pursuance of a resolution paned by the local education authority. She mint have some security that diarnissal would not be served again when the motion was withdrawn. Mr. Sargent, replying to his lordship, said the only ground of the notice was that the teachers were married women. He could not give an undertaking not to give notice on that ground pending the hearing of the action but, so far as he knew, nothing would be done which would cause any application to be necessary in the long vacation. Sir R. Finlay, having regird to what had lateen said, an 4 to the fact that the Corporation if they did any- thing of the Itharl, would find themselves in an unenvi- able position, said he accepted the view that plaintiffs might feel assured that nothing would be dons pending the hearing of the action. He understood this applied to both actions. He added that the question raised was whether it was "intra vires" of the Corporation to give notice of dismissal solely on the ground that the teacher is a married woman. There was no other sort of question. On the contrary, the absolute efficiency of the pla'ntiffs and their high character in every respect was beyond all question. Mr. Sargent quite assented to that. Mr. Northcote said he appeared for the foundation managers who altogether opposed the dismissal of. these ladles. He asked if his clients could be dis- misFed from the action. Sir It., Finlay could not agree, and his lordship said there would be no order on the moticn except that the costs be costs in the action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT. Their Majesties' Gratification.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT. Their Majesties' Gratification.</p>
        <p>The Mayor of Warrington (Alderman W. el. Forshaw) has read the following letter from Lorc Derby : Derby House, St. James's-square, S.W. 9th July, 1909.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Mayor,—l must write you one private letter to congratulate you most warmly on the way in which Warrington was decorated and the manner in which it received their Majesties the other day. Everything was absolutely perfect and their Majesties were much delighted. I think their only regret was that owing to the very bad neuralgia from which her Majesty was suffering it wis impossible to open the motor. This is a fact which I think cannot be too publicly known, as I believe there was a certain amount of disappointment amongst the Spectators, whereas if they knew the truth they would realise that it was only her Majesty's great pluck which enabled her to undertake the motor journey at all. Princess Victoria was quite delighted with her present, so much so that although the Town Clerk had told me to tell her that she was to send it back so that other necklaces should ,be sent to her in order that she might make a selection, she positively declined to do this as she liked the one selected so much and wore it the same nighl. Again let me congratulate you most heartily on all the arrangements you made. DERBY.</p>
        <p>Yours sinctrely,</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor, Warrington.</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL AND THE visrr.</p>
        <p>At the commencement of the meeting of the Warring- ton Town Council on Tuesday, when the Mayor asked the Council to confirm the minutes of the Bth June, Councillor Dr. JOSEPH said :—May I ask if you (the Mayor) intend to make any statement in regard to the minutes of the sth July? The MA.YoR : We cannot discuss that, You will have an opportunity of doing so to your heart's con- tent another time. Alderman BOLTON rose to speak. The MAYOR: You cannot discuss the matter.</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON : I understand that various sug- gestions were made. Is the record a correct one? If so, I think the minutes are in order.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK: The minutes have been carrcotly recorded.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: When shall we have an opportun- ity of discussing the matter? The MAYOR: At the next meeting. Alderman TINNION : As chair.nan of the Council meeting held on the sth July, so far as I know and understand, the resolutions are correctly recorded. Councillor Dr. JOSEPH : How about the adrainistra- tion of them ?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : I have nothing to do with that. In answer to Councillor Singleton, the MAYOR said the minutes of the sth July had been forwarded to the membez S. The minutes were then agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-07-17_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Diamond Necklace. [TO TILE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER:]</head>
        <p>The Diamond Necklace. [TO TILE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER:]</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am a loyal and obedient subject of the King and along. with many others rejoiced in anticipation of his Majesty's visit to the good old town. I hope I won't be wantonly misjudged if I utter a protest against the action of the authorties in presenting Princess Victoria with a £3OO necklace. I consider it money absolutely misplaced. If her Royal Highness' birthday had to be comthemorated what better way could be found than appropriating the money to meet the dire distress existing in our very 'midst. I feel sure such a course would have afforded Princess Victoria more satisfaction than the handing her of a mere necklace of which she may possess many-such. I am not a political Socialist, but I do think that those victims of " dire misfortune " and" vicious circum- stance " of whom, sad to relate, we have in our midst not a few, 'deserved consideration before diamond necklaces, if only in the name of humanity, let alone practical Christianity.—Yours,</p>
        <p>THE ODD MAN.</p>
